/* ============================================================
   BIO 004 Human Anatomy, Fall 2026
   practice-exam-bank.js

   Every question Dr. Rennie has written for a BIO 004 lecture
   exam, pooled so practice-lecture-exam.html can deal a fresh
   paper in her format for any of the five exams.

   SOURCES
     src:'module'  the five Fall module exams
                   (bio004-module-1..5-exam.html), one full paper
                   per exam: 20 true/false, 40 multiple choice,
                   1 matching set of 10, 4 free response.
     src:'summer'  the three Summer midterm practice papers, kept
                   for the extra depth they add to exams 2 to 4.

   Items are deduplicated on normalised question text across both
   sources, module wording winning any tie.

   TOPICS AND COMPETENCIES
   Every item carries a topic. TOPIC_COMPS maps each topic to the
   competency ids in competenciesfall2026.js. That map does two
   jobs: it spreads a generated paper across competencies instead
   of letting one clump, and it is what lets a finished paper move
   the weak spot board. A topic missing from the map still gets
   asked, it just posts no evidence, so adding a line here is all
   it takes to connect a new topic.
   ============================================================ */

window.BIO004_EXAM_BANK = {"tf":[{"statement":"A **parasagittal** plane runs vertically but off the midline, so it divides the body into unequal left and right parts.","answer":"T","why":"Only the midsagittal plane splits the body into equal halves, so any sagittal cut beside the midline is parasagittal and gives unequal parts.","topic":"Planes and Sections","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The stratum **granulosum** is found only in thick skin, such as the palms and the soles.","answer":"F","correction":"lucidum","accept":["lucidum","stratum lucidum"],"why":"The granulosum is present everywhere, while the lucidum is the extra layer that appears only in thick, hairless skin.","topic":"Epidermal Strata","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A slide showing tall cells whose nuclei sit at several different heights, with every cell still touching the basement membrane, is **stratified** columnar epithelium.","answer":"F","correction":"pseudostratified","accept":["pseudostratified","pseudostratified columnar","pseudostratified ciliated columnar"],"why":"Staggered nuclei with every cell reaching the basement membrane is the definition of pseudostratified, which only looks layered.","topic":"Epithelial Identification","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **visceral** layer of a serous membrane is the layer that clings directly to the surface of the organ.","answer":"T","why":"Visceral always goes with the organ and parietal always lines the wall, in the pleura, the pericardium, and the peritoneum alike.","topic":"Serous Membranes","dok":1,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A patient with an enlarged spleen has tenderness in the **left upper quadrant**, which is the same area the nine-region grid calls the left hypochondriac region.","answer":"T","why":"The spleen sits in the left hypochondriac region, which falls inside the left upper quadrant on the faster four-quadrant grid.","topic":"Abdominopelvic Regions and Quadrants","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"Proximal and distal describe position along a limb, so a cut on the wrist is **proximal** to a cut on the elbow.","answer":"F","correction":"distal","accept":["distal","more distal"],"why":"The wrist is farther from the point of attachment to the trunk than the elbow is, so it is distal to it.","topic":"Directional Terms","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"Skeletal muscle fibers are long, unbranched, and **multinucleated**, with the nuclei pushed out to the edge of the fiber.","answer":"T","why":"Skeletal muscle is the only muscle type with many nuclei in one fiber, and those nuclei sit at the periphery just under the membrane.","topic":"Muscle Tissue Identification","dok":1,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The dorsal body cavity is made up of the cranial cavity, which holds the brain, and the **pleural** cavity, which holds the spinal cord.","answer":"F","correction":"vertebral","accept":["vertebral","spinal","vertebral cavity","spinal cavity","vertebral (spinal)"],"why":"The spinal cord lies in the vertebral cavity, while the pleural cavities are ventral spaces that hold the lungs.","topic":"Body Cavities","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The finger-like projections that build the brush border of an intestinal cell have an actin core and are called **cilia**.","answer":"F","correction":"microvilli","accept":["microvilli","microvillus","micro villi"],"why":"Microvilli have an actin core and do not move, while cilia are built on a nine plus two microtubule pattern and beat.","topic":"Cell Surface Specializations","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A cell that secretes large amounts of digestive enzyme would be expected to carry an unusually large amount of **rough endoplasmic reticulum**.","answer":"T","why":"Protein destined for export is built on ribosomes bound to the rough endoplasmic reticulum, so heavy secretors carry a lot of it.","topic":"Organelle Identification","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"Sebaceous glands release sebum by **merocrine** secretion, in which the entire cell fills with product and then ruptures.","answer":"F","correction":"holocrine","accept":["holocrine","holocrine secretion"],"why":"Holocrine secretion sacrifices the whole cell, and sebaceous glands are the classic example, while merocrine glands use exocytosis and survive.","topic":"Glandular Epithelium","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"Meissner corpuscles, the receptors for light touch, sit in the dermal papillae of the **papillary** layer of the dermis.","answer":"T","why":"The papillary layer is thin, areolar, and close to the surface, which is exactly where a fine touch receptor needs to be.","topic":"Dermis Layers","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"In a blistering condition where the entire epidermis lifts away from the basement membrane in one sheet, the junction that has failed is the **desmosome**.","answer":"F","correction":"hemidesmosome","accept":["hemidesmosome","hemidesmosomes"],"why":"Hemidesmosomes anchor cells to the basement membrane, while desmosomes bind cell to cell and their loss separates keratinocytes from each other instead.","topic":"Cell Junctions","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **organ system** level of structural organization is a group of organs working together toward one broad function, and the integumentary system is a good example.","answer":"T","why":"An organ system sits one step above the organ level, because it is several organs cooperating on a single large job such as covering and protecting the body.","topic":"Levels of Structural Organization","dok":1,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A tumor that arises from tissue built by **mesoderm** could turn out to be made of bone, muscle, or blood, since all three trace back to that same middle germ layer.","answer":"T","why":"Mesoderm is the middle germ layer, and it gives rise to muscle, bone, blood, and most of the other connective tissues.","topic":"Embryonic Germ Layers","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A surgeon reaching the kidneys has to work behind the parietal peritoneum, because the kidneys are **intraperitoneal** organs.","answer":"F","correction":"retroperitoneal","accept":["retroperitoneal","retro peritoneal","retroperitoneal organs"],"why":"The kidneys are the K in SAD PUCKER, so they sit behind the peritoneum and are covered by it only on their anterior surface.","topic":"Peritoneal Relationships","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A **mucous** membrane lines every cavity that opens to the outside of the body, and its surface stays moist with the secretions it produces.","answer":"T","why":"Mucous membranes line the digestive, respiratory, urinary and reproductive tracts, all of which open to the exterior, and they are kept wet by their own secretions.","topic":"Body Membranes","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A burn that destroys the **stratum basale** across an area removes the dividing cells that would resurface it, so healing has to come from the wound edges or from a graft.","answer":"T","why":"All new keratinocytes come from mitosis in the stratum basale, so once the basale is gone the epidermis cannot rebuild itself from the surface.","topic":"Epidermal Strata","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **anterior** boundary of the mediastinum is the vertebral column, which is why a mass that grows backward in that space can press on the esophagus.","answer":"F","correction":"posterior","accept":["posterior","posterior boundary","dorsal"],"why":"The sternum forms the anterior boundary of the mediastinum and the vertebral column forms the posterior one, and the esophagus runs in the back of that compartment.","topic":"Mediastinum","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"Blood is classified as a connective tissue because its cells sit in an extensive extracellular **matrix**, which in this case is the fluid plasma.","answer":"T","why":"Every connective tissue is defined by cells scattered in a matrix, and in blood that matrix simply happens to be liquid.","topic":"Connective Tissue Identification","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The intervertebral discs, the knee menisci, and the pubic symphysis are all built from **elastic cartilage**.","answer":"F","correction":"fibrocartilage","accept":["fibrocartilage","fibro-cartilage","fibrous cartilage"],"why":"Those three structures are built from fibrocartilage, with its thick bundles of collagen, while elastic cartilage is limited to the external ear and the epiglottis.","topic":"Cartilage","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The patella is classified as a **sesamoid** bone because it forms within the quadriceps tendon.","answer":"T","why":"A sesamoid bone is a small bone that develops inside a tendon, and the patella is the standard example.","topic":"Bone Classification","dok":1,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"In an adult long bone the medullary cavity of the shaft holds mainly **red** marrow.","answer":"F","correction":"yellow","accept":["yellow","yellow marrow","yellow bone marrow"],"why":"In the adult shaft the medullary cavity stores fat as yellow marrow, and the red marrow that makes blood cells sits in the spongy bone of the epiphyses.","topic":"Long Bone Anatomy","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"**Canaliculi** are the tiny channels that connect neighbouring lacunae and let osteocytes exchange materials.","answer":"T","why":"Canaliculi are the narrow passages that link one lacuna to the next and back to the central canal, which is how a cell walled into hard matrix stays fed.","topic":"Bone Microanatomy","dok":1,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A slide showing concentric lamellae circling a central canal is a slide of **spongy** bone.","answer":"F","correction":"compact","accept":["compact","compact bone","cortical","cortical bone"],"why":"The osteon with its central canal is the structural unit of compact bone, and spongy bone has no osteons at all, only trabeculae.","topic":"Bone Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **osteoblast** is the large multinucleated cell, arising from a blood-cell line, that breaks down and resorbs bone matrix.","answer":"F","correction":"osteoclast","accept":["osteoclast","osteoclasts"],"why":"The osteoclast is the outsider of the four bone cells, coming from a blood-cell line and taking bone away, while the osteoblast builds new matrix.","topic":"Bone Cells","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The flat bones of the skull form by **intramembranous** ossification, in which bone develops directly within a fibrous connective tissue membrane.","answer":"T","why":"Intramembranous ossification lays bone straight into a membrane and builds the flat skull bones and the clavicle, while endochondral ossification replaces a hyaline cartilage model.","topic":"Ossification","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"Moving from the epiphysis toward the diaphysis, the zone that comes immediately after the zone of proliferation is the zone of **calcification**.","answer":"F","correction":"hypertrophy","accept":["hypertrophy","zone of hypertrophy","hypertrophic","hypertrophic cartilage"],"why":"The order is resting, proliferation, hypertrophy, calcification, then ossification, so the chondrocytes enlarge before the matrix calcifies.","topic":"Growth Plate","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **sphenoid** bone forms the central wedge of the cranial base and articulates with every other cranial bone.","answer":"T","why":"The sphenoid sits in the middle of the cranial floor and touches all the other cranial bones, which is why it is often called the keystone of the skull.","topic":"Cranial Bones","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **vomer** bones are paired, form part of the medial wall of the orbit, and are grooved for the tear duct.","answer":"F","correction":"lacrimal","accept":["lacrimal","lacrimal bone","lacrimal bones"],"why":"The lacrimal bones are the small paired bones grooved for the tear duct in the medial orbital wall, while the vomer is a single bone forming the lower nasal septum.","topic":"Facial Bones","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The optic nerve leaves the orbit for the cranial cavity through the **superior orbital fissure** of the sphenoid bone.","answer":"F","correction":"optic canal","accept":["optic canal","optic foramen","the optic canal"],"why":"The optic canal carries cranial nerve II, while the superior orbital fissure beside it carries the nerves that move the eye.","topic":"Skull Foramina","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A blow to the **pterion**, the thinnest part of the skull, can tear the middle meningeal artery running just beneath it.","answer":"T","why":"The pterion is the H-shaped junction of the frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones, and the artery lies directly under that thin plate of bone.","topic":"Sutures","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **posterior** fontanelle is the largest of the fontanelles and does not close until about 18 to 24 months.","answer":"F","correction":"anterior","accept":["anterior","anterior fontanelle","anterior fontanel"],"why":"The anterior fontanelle, where the frontal and parietal bones meet, is the largest and the last to close, while the posterior fontanelle closes at about two months.","topic":"Fontanelles","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The thoracic and sacral curvatures are the **primary** curvatures, present at birth.","answer":"T","why":"The two curves that keep the fetal C shape are the primary ones, and the cervical and lumbar curves are secondary, earned by holding the head up and by standing.","topic":"Vertebral Curvatures","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A loose vertebra is identified as cervical by the **costal facets** in its transverse processes.","answer":"F","correction":"transverse foramina","accept":["transverse foramina","transverse foramen","foramina transversaria"],"why":"Only cervical vertebrae have transverse foramina, which pass the vertebral arteries, while costal facets are the thoracic giveaway because ribs attach there.","topic":"Regional Vertebrae","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"Rib pairs **8 and 9** are called floating ribs because they have no anterior attachment at all.","answer":"F","correction":"11 and 12","accept":["11 and 12","eleven and twelve","pairs 11 and 12","rib pairs 11 and 12","the eleventh and twelfth"],"why":"The floating ribs are pairs 11 and 12, the only ones with nothing in front, while pairs 8 and 9 are false ribs that join the costal cartilage of the rib above.","topic":"Thoracic Cage","dok":1,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **sternoclavicular** joint is the only joint that links the upper limb to the axial skeleton.","answer":"T","why":"The clavicle meets the manubrium at the sternoclavicular joint, the single bony link to the axial skeleton, while the acromioclavicular joint joins two appendicular bones.","topic":"Pectoral Girdle","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **tibia** carries body weight in the leg, which is why a length of the fibula can be harvested as a bone graft.","answer":"T","why":"The fibula is a muscle attachment and an ankle stabilizer rather than a weight bearer, so a segment can be removed without collapsing the limb.","topic":"Leg Bones","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The epiphyseal plate is a cartilaginous joint of the **symphysis** type, held together by hyaline cartilage.","answer":"F","correction":"synchondrosis","accept":["synchondrosis","synchondroses","a synchondrosis"],"why":"A synchondrosis is joined by hyaline cartilage, as at the growth plate, while a symphysis is joined by a broad disc of fibrocartilage, as at the pubic symphysis.","topic":"Cartilaginous Joints","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **saddle** joint at the base of the thumb, where each surface is both concave and convex, is what allows the thumb to move across the palm in opposition.","answer":"T","why":"The carpometacarpal joint of the thumb is the body's saddle joint, and its biaxial freedom is what lets the thumb swing over to meet the fingertips.","topic":"Synovial Joint Types","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **endomysium** is the delicate connective tissue sheath that wraps each individual muscle fiber.","answer":"T","why":"Endomysium wraps the single fiber, perimysium wraps the fascicle, and epimysium wraps the whole muscle.","topic":"Muscle Structure","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"When a sarcomere shortens, the width of the **A band** decreases along with the H zone.","answer":"F","correction":"I band","accept":["i band","the i band","i-band","i bands"],"why":"The A band is as wide as the thick filament is long, so it never changes; the I band and the H zone are the regions that narrow.","topic":"Sarcomere","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The many nuclei of a skeletal muscle fiber lie flattened against the inner surface of the **sarcolemma**.","answer":"T","why":"A muscle fiber is multinucleated, and those nuclei sit right up against the plasma membrane of the cell, which is called the sarcolemma.","topic":"Muscle Fiber","dok":1,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"Cardiac muscle cells are joined end to end by **intercalated discs** that contain both desmosomes and gap junctions.","answer":"T","why":"The desmosomes hold the cells together mechanically and the gap junctions let the signal pass from cell to cell.","topic":"Cardiac Muscle","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"**Titin** runs the length of the thin filament and sets how long that filament will be.","answer":"F","correction":"nebulin","accept":["nebulin","nebulin protein"],"why":"Nebulin runs along the thin filament and sets its length, while titin spans from the Z disc to the M line and anchors the thick filament.","topic":"Myofilament Proteins","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"Of the four rotator cuff tendons, the one pinched under the acromion by repeated overhead work is the **infraspinatus** tendon.","answer":"F","correction":"supraspinatus","accept":["supraspinatus","supraspinatus tendon","the supraspinatus"],"why":"The supraspinatus tendon passes under the acromion, a bony roof with no give, which is why impingement and cuff tears concentrate in that one tendon.","topic":"Rotator Cuff","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"Nodding the head at the atlanto-occipital joint works as a **first class** lever, with the fulcrum sitting between the effort and the load.","answer":"T","why":"The joint is the fulcrum, the posterior neck muscles pull from behind it, and the weight of the face is the load in front.","topic":"Lever Systems","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The valve guarding the left atrioventricular opening has **three** cusps, which is why it is also called the bicuspid valve.","answer":"F","correction":"two","accept":["two","2","two cusps","2 cusps"],"why":"The mitral valve is the one heart valve with two cusps, and the tricuspid, pulmonary, and aortic valves each have three.","topic":"Heart Valves","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **coronary sinus** collects blood from the cardiac veins and empties it into the right atrium.","answer":"T","why":"It is the large vein on the posterior heart and it returns the heart's own venous blood to the right atrium.","topic":"Coronary Circulation","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **internal elastic lamina** is the fenestrated sheet of elastic fibers that marks the border between the tunica intima and the tunica media.","answer":"T","why":"It sits at the boundary of the two innermost tunics and is easiest to pick out in a muscular artery.","topic":"Vessel Wall","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The signal leaving the atrioventricular node travels next into the **Purkinje fibers**, which pass down through the interventricular septum.","answer":"F","correction":"AV bundle","accept":["av bundle","atrioventricular bundle","bundle of his","his bundle"],"why":"The AV bundle carries the signal into the septum first, and only after the bundle branches do the Purkinje fibers spread it through the ventricle walls.","topic":"Conduction System","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"**Fenestrated** capillaries have large gaps and an incomplete basement membrane, which is why whole cells can cross them in the liver and the spleen.","answer":"F","correction":"sinusoid","accept":["sinusoid","sinusoids","sinusoidal","sinusoidal capillaries"],"why":"Sinusoids are the leakiest type, while fenestrated capillaries have only small pores and let fluid and small solutes through.","topic":"Capillaries","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **ductus arteriosus** shunts blood from the pulmonary trunk into the aortic arch before birth and is left behind as the ligamentum arteriosum.","answer":"T","why":"That shunt keeps blood away from the collapsed fetal lungs, and it closes in the first days of life to become a fibrous cord.","topic":"Fetal Circulation","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The fetal **foramen ovale** leaves behind the ligamentum venosum on the inferior surface of the liver.","answer":"F","correction":"ductus venosus","accept":["ductus venosus","the ductus venosus"],"why":"The ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum, while the foramen ovale becomes the fossa ovalis in the interatrial septum.","topic":"Fetal Circulation","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"**Albumin** is the most abundant plasma protein and the one that holds water in the bloodstream by osmotic pressure.","answer":"T","why":"Albumin makes up roughly sixty percent of plasma protein and is the chief osmotic and carrier protein.","topic":"Plasma","dok":1,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **eosinophil** is the largest leukocyte and is identified on a smear by its kidney-shaped nucleus.","answer":"F","correction":"monocyte","accept":["monocyte","monocytes","the monocyte"],"why":"The monocyte is the largest white cell with a kidney-shaped nucleus, while the eosinophil is smaller with a two-lobed nucleus and coarse red-orange granules.","topic":"Leukocytes","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"Platelets are fragments shed from the cytoplasm of a giant marrow cell called the **megakaryocyte**.","answer":"T","why":"Platelets are not complete cells, they are pieces broken off one very large marrow cell.","topic":"Formed Elements","dok":1,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The artery that a blood pressure cuff squeezes against the humerus is the **axillary** artery.","answer":"F","correction":"brachial","accept":["brachial","brachial artery","the brachial"],"why":"The brachial artery runs down the medial side of the arm, which is exactly where the cuff sits and where the stethoscope listens.","topic":"Upper Limb Arteries","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A knock to the **median** nerve where it passes behind the medial epicondyle sends tingling into the ring and little fingers.","answer":"F","correction":"ulnar","accept":["ulnar","ulnar nerve","the ulnar nerve"],"why":"The ulnar nerve is superficial and unprotected behind the medial epicondyle, which is the funny bone, and it serves the medial side of the hand.","topic":"Upper Limb Nerves","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **median cubital vein** crosses the cubital fossa between the cephalic and basilic veins and is the usual site for drawing blood.","answer":"T","why":"It is superficial, large, and anchored by fascia, which is exactly what makes it easy to enter.","topic":"Upper Limb Veins","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **cricoid** cartilage is the flexible, spoon-shaped flap of elastic cartilage that folds over the laryngeal inlet during swallowing.","answer":"F","correction":"epiglottis","accept":["epiglottis","the epiglottis","epiglottic cartilage"],"why":"The epiglottis is the single elastic cartilage that covers the airway during a swallow, while the cricoid is the complete hyaline ring below the thyroid cartilage.","topic":"Larynx","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The right lung has three lobes separated by a horizontal fissure and an **oblique** fissure, while the left lung has two lobes separated by an oblique fissure alone.","answer":"T","why":"The right lung carries both a horizontal and an oblique fissure to make three lobes, and the left has only the oblique fissure and two lobes.","topic":"Lungs and Pleurae","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **right lymphatic duct** returns lymph from the left leg and the left side of the abdomen to the bloodstream.","answer":"F","correction":"thoracic duct","accept":["thoracic duct","the thoracic duct","left lymphatic duct"],"why":"The right lymphatic duct drains only the upper right quadrant of the body, and everything else, including both legs, drains through the thoracic duct.","topic":"Lymphatic Vessels","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **submucosal** plexus lies between the circular and longitudinal layers of the muscularis and controls the movement of the gut.","answer":"F","correction":"myenteric","accept":["myenteric","myenteric plexus","auerbach","auerbach plexus","plexus of auerbach"],"why":"The myenteric plexus sits between the two muscle layers and controls motility, while the submucosal plexus sits in the submucosa and controls secretion.","topic":"Gut Wall","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **isthmus** is the narrow bridge of thyroid tissue that crosses in front of the trachea and joins the gland's two lateral lobes.","answer":"T","why":"The thyroid is two lobes on either side of the trachea joined across the front by the isthmus, which is the part a surgeon divides first in a thyroidectomy.","topic":"Thyroid Gland","dok":1,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The stomach carries a third layer of muscle, an inner **oblique** layer, that the rest of the alimentary canal does not have.","answer":"T","why":"The stomach muscularis has an inner oblique, a middle circular, and an outer longitudinal layer, one more than the two layers found elsewhere in the tube.","topic":"Stomach","dok":1,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"An inhaled peanut most often ends up in the **left** main bronchus, because that bronchus is wider and more vertical than the one on the other side.","answer":"F","correction":"right","accept":["right","right main bronchus","right primary bronchus"],"why":"The right main bronchus is the wider and more vertical of the two, so gravity and the straighter angle carry aspirated objects into the right lung.","topic":"Bronchial Tree","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"Lymph enters a node through several afferent vessels and leaves through efferent vessels at the **hilum**.","answer":"T","why":"Afferent vessels arrive at the surface of the node and the filtered lymph leaves through efferent vessels at the indented hilum.","topic":"Lymph Nodes","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The duodenum, the pancreas, and the kidneys are all described as **intraperitoneal** organs.","answer":"F","correction":"retroperitoneal","accept":["retroperitoneal","retroperitoneal organs","behind the peritoneum"],"why":"All three of those organs lie behind the peritoneum rather than inside its cavity, which is what retroperitoneal means.","topic":"Peritoneum","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"Cartilage disappears from the airway wall at the level of the **bronchioles**, which is why bronchioles can narrow and bronchi cannot.","answer":"T","why":"Cartilage falls away and smooth muscle takes over as the tree gets smaller, so the bronchioles are the airways that can constrict.","topic":"Bronchial Tree","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The three features that belong to the large intestine and to no other part of the canal are the teniae coli, the haustra, and the **omental appendices**.","answer":"T","why":"Those three features, the muscle bands, the pouches they gather, and the small fat-filled tags of peritoneum, are unique to the large intestine.","topic":"Large Intestine","dok":1,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A stone that blocks the **common hepatic duct** on its own traps bile inside the gallbladder but still lets liver bile reach the duodenum.","answer":"F","correction":"cystic duct","accept":["cystic duct","the cystic duct","cystic"],"why":"Only the cystic duct carries bile both into and out of the gallbladder, so a stone there shuts off the gallbladder while liver bile still runs down the common hepatic and common bile ducts.","topic":"Biliary Tree","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **zona glomerulosa** is the thin outer zone of the adrenal cortex, lying just beneath the connective tissue capsule.","answer":"T","why":"The cortex runs glomerulosa, fasciculata, then reticularis from the capsule inward, with the medulla at the core.","topic":"Adrenal Glands","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"When a puncture lets air into the sealed space between the two pleurae, the seal is lost and the lung on that side collapses, a condition called **emphysema**.","answer":"F","correction":"pneumothorax","accept":["pneumothorax","a pneumothorax","collapsed lung"],"why":"Air in the pleural cavity is a pneumothorax, while emphysema is destruction of the alveolar walls themselves.","topic":"Pleurae","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A premature newborn whose alveoli collapse at the end of every breath is short of the surfactant normally made by the **type I** alveolar cells.","answer":"F","correction":"type II","accept":["type II","type 2","type II alveolar cells","type II pneumocytes"],"why":"Type II alveolar cells are the cuboidal cells that secrete surfactant, while the thin type I cells form the exchange surface.","topic":"Alveoli","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **submandibular** duct opens beside the lingual frenulum, so a stone caught in that duct can often be felt through the floor of the mouth.","answer":"T","why":"The submandibular gland sits along the inner side of the mandible and sends its duct forward under the tongue to open near the lingual frenulum, which puts the duct within reach of a finger.","topic":"Salivary Glands","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The two primary lymphatic organs, the places where lymphocytes are made and matured, are the red bone marrow and the **thymus**.","answer":"T","why":"Lymphocytes are born in the red bone marrow, B cells mature there, and T cells finish maturing in the thymus, so only those two are primary organs.","topic":"Lymphatic Organs","dok":1,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The diaphragm takes its motor supply from the **vagus** nerve, which is why an injury high in the neck can leave a patient unable to breathe without help.","answer":"F","correction":"phrenic","accept":["phrenic","phrenic nerve","the phrenic nerve"],"why":"The phrenic nerve, from spinal segments C3, C4, and C5, is the motor nerve of the diaphragm, so a cervical injury above that level takes the diaphragm out of action.","topic":"Diaphragm","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **falciform ligament** attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and the diaphragm, and it carries the ligamentum teres in its free edge.","answer":"T","why":"The falciform ligament is the peritoneal fold that suspends the liver in front, and the round ligament, a remnant of the fetal umbilical vein, runs in its lower edge.","topic":"Liver","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"Because the pituitary gland sits in the sella turcica of the **ethmoid** bone, a surgeon can reach a pituitary tumor through the nose and the air sinus inside that bone.","answer":"F","correction":"sphenoid","accept":["sphenoid","sphenoid bone","the sphenoid","sphenoidal"],"why":"The sella turcica is the saddle-shaped pocket of the sphenoid bone, and the sphenoid sinus lies right below it, which is what makes the route up through the nose possible.","topic":"Pituitary Gland","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **precentral gyrus** of the frontal lobe holds the primary motor cortex, the area that initiates voluntary movement.","answer":"T","why":"The primary motor cortex sits on the precentral gyrus, just in front of the central sulcus.","topic":"Functional Cortical Areas","dok":1,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A person who loses a great deal of body fat can develop nephroptosis, because the **renal fascia** is the layer that normally cushions the kidney and holds it in position.","answer":"F","correction":"adipose capsule","accept":["adipose capsule","fat capsule","fatty capsule","perirenal fat","perinephric fat"],"why":"The adipose capsule is the fat cushion that holds the kidney in place, so losing it lets the kidney drop and kink its ureter.","topic":"Kidney Coverings","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **posterior** root of a spinal nerve carries motor axons out of the cord to skeletal muscle.","answer":"F","correction":"anterior","accept":["anterior","ventral","anterior root","ventral root"],"why":"The anterior root is motor out and the posterior root is sensory in, and only the posterior root carries a ganglion.","topic":"Spinal Cord Roots","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"Because the urethra passes straight through the **prostate**, an enlargement of that gland squeezes the urethra and makes urination difficult.","answer":"T","why":"The prostatic urethra runs through the middle of the prostate, so an enlarged prostate narrows the urinary route.","topic":"Male Accessory Glands","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A mass growing in the midline groove between the two cerebral hemispheres would press first on the **tentorium cerebelli**.","answer":"F","correction":"falx cerebri","accept":["falx cerebri","cerebral falx","falx"],"why":"The falx cerebri is the dural fold that dips into the longitudinal fissure between the hemispheres.","topic":"Dural Reflections","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The kidneys lie **retroperitoneal**, against the posterior abdominal wall between the levels of T12 and L3.","answer":"T","why":"The kidneys sit behind the peritoneum on the posterior abdominal wall, with the right kidney slightly lower because of the liver.","topic":"Kidney Position","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **hippocampus**, a limbic structure in the temporal lobe, is essential for forming new long-term memories.","answer":"T","why":"The hippocampus is the limbic structure that lays down new long-term memories, while the amygdala at its tip handles fear and aggression.","topic":"Limbic System","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **cremaster** muscle is the smooth muscle of the scrotum that wrinkles the skin to reduce heat loss.","answer":"F","correction":"dartos","accept":["dartos","dartos muscle"],"why":"The dartos is the smooth muscle that wrinkles the scrotal skin, while the cremaster is skeletal muscle that raises and lowers the testes.","topic":"Scrotum and Testis","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A cut axon in a peripheral nerve can regrow because the **oligodendrocyte** leaves behind a neurolemma that guides it.","answer":"F","correction":"Schwann cell","accept":["schwann cell","schwann cells","schwann","neurolemmocyte"],"why":"Schwann cells myelinate PNS axons and leave a neurolemma that guides regrowth, and oligodendrocytes of the CNS leave none.","topic":"Myelination and Regeneration","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A lumbar puncture needle placed between the L4 and L5 spines enters the **lumbar cistern**, a pool of cerebrospinal fluid below the end of the spinal cord.","answer":"T","why":"The cord ends at the conus medullaris near the L1 to L2 disc, so the subarachnoid space below it holds only CSF and the floating cauda equina.","topic":"Lumbar Puncture","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"Blood leaves the glomerulus through the **afferent** arteriole and travels on to the peritubular capillaries.","answer":"F","correction":"efferent","accept":["efferent","efferent arteriole"],"why":"The afferent arteriole delivers blood into the glomerulus and the efferent arteriole carries it out toward the second capillary bed.","topic":"Renal Blood Supply","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A cluster of neuron cell bodies located outside the central nervous system is called a **ganglion**.","answer":"T","why":"Cell bodies cluster as a nucleus inside the CNS and as a ganglion outside it.","topic":"CNS and PNS Tissue Terms","dok":1,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A stroke that destroys **Wernicke's area** leaves comprehension intact but makes speech slow, halting, and effortful.","answer":"F","correction":"Broca's area","accept":["broca's area","brocas area","broca area","broca","broca's"],"why":"Broca's area in the inferior frontal gyrus produces speech, so losing it leaves understanding intact while speech becomes laborious.","topic":"Functional Cortical Areas","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"Fertilization normally happens in the **isthmus**, the widest and longest region of the uterine tube.","answer":"F","correction":"ampulla","accept":["ampulla","ampulla of the uterine tube","the ampulla"],"why":"The ampulla is the wide middle stretch of the uterine tube and the usual site of fertilization, while the isthmus is the narrow part next to the uterus.","topic":"Uterine Tubes","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"In the spinal cord the **gray matter** forms a butterfly at the core and the white matter surrounds it, the reverse of the arrangement in the brain.","answer":"T","why":"Gray matter is inside and white matter outside in the cord, while the brain has gray cortex outside and white matter within.","topic":"Gray and White Matter","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"A patient who can no longer turn reflexively toward a sudden loud noise has damage near the **superior** colliculi of the midbrain.","answer":"F","correction":"inferior","accept":["inferior","inferior colliculi","inferior colliculus"],"why":"The inferior colliculi are the auditory reflex centers, and the superior colliculi above them serve visual reflexes.","topic":"Midbrain","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The **corpus albicans** is the scar that remains after the corpus luteum degenerates.","answer":"T","why":"After ovulation the follicle becomes the corpus luteum, which later breaks down into the whitish scar called the corpus albicans.","topic":"Ovary and Follicles","dok":1,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"The preganglionic cell bodies of the **parasympathetic** division sit in the lateral gray horns of the T1 to L2 spinal segments.","answer":"F","correction":"sympathetic","accept":["sympathetic","sympathetic division","thoracolumbar"],"why":"The sympathetic division is thoracolumbar, leaving from T1 to L2, while the parasympathetic division is craniosacral.","topic":"Autonomic Divisions","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"Damage to the **phrenic nerve** of the cervical plexus weakens the diaphragm on that side and makes breathing harder.","answer":"T","why":"The phrenic nerve leaves the cervical plexus and is the sole motor supply to the diaphragm.","topic":"Nerve Plexuses","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"In an autonomic pathway, the axon that runs from the ganglion out to the effector belongs to the **preganglionic** neuron.","answer":"F","correction":"postganglionic","accept":["postganglionic","postganglionic neuron","post-ganglionic"],"why":"The preganglionic axon runs from the CNS to the ganglion, and the postganglionic axon carries the signal from the ganglion to the effector.","topic":"Autonomic Two-Neuron Pathway","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"statement":"**Albumin** is the most abundant plasma protein and helps maintain the osmotic pressure that holds water in the blood.","answer":"T","topic":"Blood","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **central canal**, also called the Haversian canal, sits at the core of an osteon and carries blood vessels and nerves.","answer":"T","topic":"Bone Microanatomy","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **mitral** valve lies between the right atrium and the right ventricle.","answer":"F","correction":"tricuspid","accept":["tricuspid"],"topic":"Heart","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **tunica media** is the vessel wall layer whose smooth muscle sets the diameter of the vessel.","answer":"T","topic":"Blood Vessels","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **left** main bronchus is wider and more vertical, so an inhaled object most often lodges there.","answer":"F","correction":"right","accept":["right"],"topic":"Respiratory","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"**Neutrophils** are the rarest leukocytes and release histamine during inflammation.","answer":"F","correction":"Basophils","accept":["basophils","basophil"],"topic":"Blood","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The anterior interventricular artery, or **LAD**, is a branch of the left coronary artery.","answer":"T","topic":"Coronary Circulation","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **A band** of a sarcomere contains thin filaments only.","answer":"F","correction":"I band","accept":["i band","i-band","iband"],"topic":"Muscle Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **left ventricle** has the thickest wall of the four heart chambers because it drives the systemic circuit.","answer":"T","topic":"Heart","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"**Merocrine** sweat glands release their product by exocytosis while the cell stays intact.","answer":"T","topic":"Integumentary","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"After birth, the foramen ovale is left behind as the **ligamentum arteriosum**.","answer":"F","correction":"fossa ovalis","accept":["fossa ovalis","fossa ovale"],"topic":"Fetal Circulation","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **triad** is the structural unit of one T tubule flanked by two terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","answer":"T","topic":"Muscle Microanatomy","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **axis** is the ring-shaped C1 vertebra that has no body and holds up the skull.","answer":"F","correction":"atlas","accept":["atlas"],"topic":"Axial Skeleton","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The brief delay of the impulse at the **AV node** lets the atria finish emptying before the ventricles contract.","answer":"T","topic":"Conduction System","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **scaphoid** is the carpal bone most frequently fractured, usually from a fall on an outstretched hand.","answer":"T","topic":"Appendicular Skeleton","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"**Capillaries** have all three tunics in their walls.","answer":"F","correction":"Arteries","accept":["arteries","artery","veins","vein"],"topic":"Blood Vessels","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"**Osteoblasts** are the large multinucleated cells that break down and resorb bone matrix.","answer":"F","correction":"Osteoclasts","accept":["osteoclasts","osteoclast"],"topic":"Bone Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"**Type II** alveolar cells secrete the surfactant that lowers surface tension and keeps alveoli from collapsing.","answer":"T","topic":"Respiratory","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"**B cells** are the lymphocytes that mature in the thymus.","answer":"F","correction":"T cells","accept":["t cells","t-cells","t cell","t lymphocytes","t lymphocyte"],"topic":"Lymphatic","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"A **gomphosis** is the fibrous joint that anchors a tooth in its bony socket.","answer":"T","topic":"Joints","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"During ventricular systole the **atrioventricular** valves are forced open so blood can be ejected into the great arteries.","answer":"F","correction":"semilunar","accept":["semilunar"],"topic":"Heart","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **vertebral** arteries enter the skull through the foramen magnum, while the internal carotid arteries enter through the carotid canals.","answer":"T","topic":"Neck Vessels","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"During contraction the **I band and H zone** narrow while the A band stays the same width, exactly as the sliding-filament model predicts.","answer":"T","topic":"Muscle Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"Blood pressure is most reliably measured over the **radial** artery as it crosses the front of the elbow.","answer":"F","correction":"brachial","accept":["brachial"],"topic":"Upper Extremity Vessels","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The transverse foramina that transmit the vertebral arteries are a distinctive feature of the **thoracic** vertebrae.","answer":"F","correction":"cervical","accept":["cervical"],"topic":"Axial Skeleton","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"T lymphocytes mature in the **spleen**, which is classified as a primary lymphatic organ.","answer":"F","correction":"thymus","accept":["thymus"],"topic":"Lymphatic","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"**Elastic arteries** such as the aorta stretch during systole and recoil during diastole, smoothing pulsatile flow into a steadier stream.","answer":"T","topic":"Vessel Histology","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The myocardium receives most of its blood flow during **diastole**, when the relaxed wall is no longer squeezing the coronary vessels.","answer":"T","topic":"Coronary Circulation","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The calcium ions that trigger contraction are released into the sarcoplasm from the **T tubules**.","answer":"F","correction":"sarcoplasmic reticulum","accept":["sarcoplasmic reticulum","terminal cisternae"],"topic":"Muscle Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The large deep vein that drains most of the blood from the brain is the **external jugular** vein.","answer":"F","correction":"internal jugular","accept":["internal jugular","internal"],"topic":"Neck Vessels","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The blood-brain barrier is formed by **fenestrated** capillaries, whose pores tightly regulate what reaches the brain.","answer":"F","correction":"continuous","accept":["continuous"],"topic":"Vessel Histology","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"Because mature erythrocytes lack **mitochondria**, they make ATP only by anaerobic glycolysis and do not consume the oxygen they transport.","answer":"T","topic":"Blood","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"In anatomical position the **radial** artery lies on the lateral, thumb side of the forearm.","answer":"T","topic":"Upper Extremity Vessels","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"Gas exchange first becomes possible in the **terminal** bronchioles, where alveoli begin budding from the walls.","answer":"F","correction":"respiratory","accept":["respiratory"],"topic":"Respiratory","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"A sharply elevated **eosinophil** count is the expected finding in an acute bacterial infection.","answer":"F","correction":"neutrophil","accept":["neutrophil","neutrophils"],"topic":"Blood","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"A long bone lengthens at the **epiphyseal plate**, a disc of hyaline cartilage between the epiphysis and diaphysis.","answer":"T","topic":"Bone Microanatomy","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **left ventricle** generates far higher pressure than the right, which is why its myocardial wall is much thicker.","answer":"T","topic":"Heart","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The shoulder's great mobility comes at the cost of stability, largely because its **glenoid cavity** is shallow relative to the large humeral head.","answer":"T","topic":"Joints","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"An osteocyte buried deep in compact bone receives nutrients by diffusion through the solid **lamellae** of the matrix.","answer":"F","correction":"canaliculi","accept":["canaliculi"],"topic":"Bone Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"If the SA node fails, the **Purkinje fibers** normally take over as the next pacemaker, though at a slower rate.","answer":"F","correction":"AV node","accept":["av node","atrioventricular node"],"topic":"Conduction System","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **epiglottis** folds down over the laryngeal opening during swallowing to keep food and drink out of the airway.","answer":"T","topic":"Respiratory","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The neck vessel that ascends into the cranium to supply the brain is the **external carotid** artery.","answer":"F","correction":"internal carotid","accept":["internal carotid","internal"],"topic":"Neck Vessels","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"Hematopoiesis, the formation of blood cells, takes place mainly in **yellow** bone marrow.","answer":"F","correction":"red","accept":["red"],"topic":"Bone","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **biconcave** shape of a red blood cell gives it both the flexibility to fold through narrow capillaries and extra surface area for gas exchange.","answer":"T","topic":"Blood","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"At the elbow the brachial artery divides into the radial artery and the **cephalic** artery.","answer":"F","correction":"ulnar","accept":["ulnar"],"topic":"Upper Extremity Vessels","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"On an ECG the **QRS complex** represents repolarization, the electrical recovery of the ventricles.","answer":"F","correction":"T wave","accept":["t wave","the t wave"],"topic":"Conduction System","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"Blood in the **pulmonary veins** is oxygen-rich, an exception to the general rule that veins carry oxygen-poor blood.","answer":"T","topic":"Heart","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"In the sliding-filament model the thin filaments slide past the thick filaments, so it is the **A band** that shortens during contraction.","answer":"F","correction":"I band","accept":["i band","i-band","iband","h zone","h-zone"],"topic":"Muscle Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **thymus**, larger in children than adults, is the primary lymphatic organ in which T lymphocytes mature.","answer":"T","topic":"Lymphatic","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **cephalic** vein is a superficial vein that runs up the lateral side of the arm.","answer":"T","topic":"Upper Extremity Vessels","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **tricuspid** valve is the one that prevents backflow from the left ventricle into the left atrium.","answer":"F","correction":"mitral","accept":["mitral","bicuspid"],"topic":"Heart","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"In a centrifuged tube of blood, the thin buffy coat between the plasma and the packed red cells is made of **erythrocytes**.","answer":"F","correction":"leukocytes and platelets","accept":["leukocytes and platelets","white blood cells and platelets","leukocytes","white blood cells"],"topic":"Blood","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"**Sinusoidal** capillaries in the liver and spleen are leaky enough to let whole blood cells pass across their walls.","answer":"T","topic":"Vessel Histology","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **neuromuscular junction** is the synapse where a motor neuron passes its signal to a skeletal muscle fiber.","answer":"T","topic":"Muscle Microanatomy","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The layer of a vessel whose smooth muscle contracts to narrow the lumen is the **tunica intima**.","answer":"F","correction":"tunica media","accept":["tunica media","media"],"topic":"Vessel Histology","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **tibia** is the longest and strongest bone in the body and forms the thigh.","answer":"F","correction":"femur","accept":["femur"],"topic":"Appendicular Skeleton","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"Each **common carotid** artery divides in the neck into an internal and an external carotid branch.","answer":"T","topic":"Neck Vessels","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"Gas exchange occurs across the respiratory membrane, formed where the alveolar walls meet the walls of the **arterioles**.","answer":"F","correction":"capillaries","accept":["capillaries","capillary"],"topic":"Respiratory","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **circumflex** artery, a branch of the left coronary artery, runs in the groove between the left atrium and left ventricle.","answer":"T","topic":"Coronary Circulation","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"statement":"The **fontanelles** are the fibrous soft spots between the not-yet-fused bones of an infant's skull.","answer":"T","topic":"Axial Skeleton","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"summer"}],"mc":[{"stem":"A radiologist opens a CT study and sees a single slice showing a ring of abdominal wall with the liver on one side, the spleen on the other, and a vertebral body at the back. Which plane produced that image?","options":["Midsagittal plane","Frontal plane","Transverse plane","Parasagittal plane"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"A CT slice is a transverse section, so it cuts across the body and separates superior from inferior. A midsagittal or parasagittal cut would run vertically and show right and left parts, and a frontal cut would separate anterior from posterior rather than giving a ring of body wall.","topic":"Planes and Sections","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which sequence lists the levels of structural organization correctly, from simplest to most complex?","options":["Chemical, cellular, tissue, organ, organ system, organismal","Cellular, chemical, tissue, organ, organ system, organismal","Chemical, tissue, cellular, organ, organ system, organismal","Chemical, cellular, organ, tissue, organ system, organismal"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"Each level is built from the one before it, so atoms and molecules build cells, similar cells build a tissue, and two or more tissues build an organ. The other three sequences all invert a step, and putting tissue before cellular or organ before tissue breaks the rule that a level is assembled from the level below it.","topic":"Levels of Structural Organization","dok":1,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient has a stroke affecting the left side of the brain, and the weakness that follows appears in the right arm and the right leg. Which single directional term describes the relationship between the lesion and the weakness?","options":["Ipsilateral","Superficial","Proximal","Contralateral"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"Contralateral means on the opposite side of the body, which is exactly the relationship between a left brain lesion and right sided weakness. Ipsilateral would mean the same side, and superficial and proximal describe depth and limb position rather than sidedness.","topic":"Directional Terms","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A student charts a structure as lateral while the patient is lying prone on the table with both arms resting at the sides. Why is that description still correct?","options":["Because lateral is measured from the examiner's midline rather than the patient's","Because directional terms are always given as though the body were in anatomical position","Because lateral and medial are the only two terms that do not depend on body position","Because the patient is prone, and prone reverses anterior and posterior"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Anatomical position is the fixed reference, so a description does not change with how the person is standing or lying. Right and left are always the patient's, not the examiner's, and being prone does not swap the meaning of anterior and posterior.","topic":"Anatomical Position","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which grouping contains only appendicular regional terms?","options":["Antecubital, popliteal, carpal, sural","Cervical, thoracic, umbilical, vertebral","Brachial, carpal, occipital, patellar","Femoral, crural, mental, tarsal"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The appendicular region is the limbs, and the front of the elbow, the back of the knee, the wrist, and the calf all sit on a limb. The second group is entirely axial, and the third and fourth groups each smuggle in an axial term, the back of the head and the chin.","topic":"Regional Terms","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"The diaphragm is the most useful boundary in the ventral body cavity. Which two spaces does it separate?","options":["The pleural cavity from the pericardial cavity","The dorsal cavity from the ventral cavity","The thoracic cavity from the abdominopelvic cavity","The abdominal cavity from the pelvic cavity"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The diaphragm forms the floor of the thoracic cavity and the roof of the abdominopelvic cavity, so it tells you which membranes and fluids you are dealing with. The pleural and pericardial cavities are both above it, the dorsal and ventral cavities are separated by the body wall rather than by a muscle, and the abdominal and pelvic cavities are divided by the pelvic brim.","topic":"Body Cavities","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A stab wound opens the chest wall, air rushes into the space around the right lung, and the lung collapses. Which layer was the last one the blade crossed before it entered that space?","options":["Visceral pleura","Parietal pleura","Visceral pericardium","Parietal peritoneum"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The parietal pleura lines the inside of the thoracic wall, so a blade coming from outside reaches it just before entering the pleural cavity. The visceral pleura is deeper still, on the lung surface itself, and the pericardial and peritoneal layers belong to entirely different cavities.","topic":"Serous Membranes","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A CT shows a mediastinal mass lying with the pericardium in front of it and the vertebral bodies behind it. The patient reports increasing trouble swallowing solid food. Which compartment holds the mass, and which structure explains the symptom?","options":["Superior mediastinum, compressing the trachea","Anterior mediastinum, compressing the thymus","Middle mediastinum, compressing the pulmonary trunk","Posterior mediastinum, compressing the esophagus"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The posterior compartment runs from the pericardium back to the vertebral bodies and carries the esophagus, the descending thoracic aorta, and the thoracic duct, so a mass there presses on the swallowing tube. The anterior compartment holds the thymic remnant and fat, and the middle compartment is the pericardial sac and its contents, neither of which would produce dysphagia first.","topic":"Mediastinum","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Using SAD PUCKER, which group of structures is entirely retroperitoneal?","options":["Kidneys, ureters, pancreatic body, ascending colon","Stomach, spleen, jejunum, transverse colon","Liver, gallbladder, ileum, sigmoid colon","Spleen, duodenum, jejunum, rectum"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"SAD PUCKER collects the structures that sit behind the parietal peritoneum, and the kidneys, ureters, pancreatic body, and ascending colon are all on that list. The second and third groups are intraperitoneal organs suspended in the cavity, and the fourth mixes a retroperitoneal duodenum and rectum with an intraperitoneal spleen and jejunum.","topic":"Peritoneal Relationships","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A chart note places a patient's tenderness directly over the cecum and the appendix. Which pairing names that same spot on the nine-region grid and then on the four-quadrant grid?","options":["Right lumbar region, right upper quadrant","Umbilical region, right lower quadrant","Hypogastric region, left lower quadrant","Right iliac region, right lower quadrant"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The cecum and appendix sit in the right iliac region, which falls within the right lower quadrant, so both grids point at the same place using different vocabulary. The right lumbar region holds the ascending colon higher up, the umbilical region covers the small intestine centrally, and the hypogastric region sits over the bladder in the midline.","topic":"Abdominopelvic Regions and Quadrants","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which three major components make up the generalized cell?","options":["Nucleus, nucleolus, and cytosol","Cell wall, cytoplasm, and nucleus","Plasma membrane, cytoplasm, and nucleus","Plasma membrane, cytosol, and chromatin"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The generalized cell is a membrane that wraps it, a nucleus that holds the DNA, and a cytoplasm filled with organelles. Human cells have no cell wall, the nucleolus is a region inside the nucleus rather than a component of the cell, and cytosol alone leaves out the organelles.","topic":"Generalized Cell","dok":1,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes integral membrane proteins from peripheral membrane proteins?","options":["Integral proteins sit on the surface of the membrane, and peripheral proteins span the bilayer","Integral proteins span the bilayer, and peripheral proteins attach loosely to one face of it","Both types span the bilayer, and the peripheral ones carry the attached sugar chains","Integral proteins are built from carbohydrate, and peripheral proteins are built from lipid"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Integral proteins are embedded through the bilayer, which is what lets them work as channels and carriers, while peripheral proteins bind to one surface and are easily removed. The first option simply reverses the two, and both proteins are made of amino acids rather than sugar or lipid.","topic":"Plasma Membrane","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient has had sinus and lung infections since childhood, and a biopsy of the airway lining shows surface projections that do not move. Which structure is defective, and what has the patient lost?","options":["Microvilli, and with them the absorption of nutrients","Flagella, and with them the propulsion of the cell itself","Hemidesmosomes, and with them the anchoring of the epithelium","Cilia, and with them the sweeping of mucus up toward the throat"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"Cilia on the pseudostratified columnar lining of the airway beat in coordinated waves and move mucus and trapped debris toward the throat, so immotile cilia leave secretions sitting in the lower airway and infections recur. Microvilli never move and belong to absorptive surfaces, the only human flagellum is on sperm, and hemidesmosome failure would present as a blistering problem rather than an infection problem.","topic":"Cell Surface Specializations","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which structure inside the nucleus is the site where ribosomal RNA is made and ribosomal subunits are assembled?","options":["Nucleolus","Nuclear envelope","Chromatin","Nuclear pore"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The nucleolus is the dense region where ribosomal RNA is synthesized and the subunits are put together before they leave. The envelope is the double membrane boundary, chromatin is the DNA and histone complex, and the pores are the channels the finished subunits pass through on the way out.","topic":"Nucleus","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"The nuclear envelope is continuous with which organelle, and what does that continuity make possible?","options":["The Golgi apparatus, so that finished protein can ship straight out of the nucleus","The rough endoplasmic reticulum, so a new protein passes straight into the endomembrane system","The lysosome, so that damaged DNA can be digested right where it sits","The mitochondrion, so that ATP can be delivered directly into the nucleus"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The outer membrane of the nuclear envelope runs directly into the rough endoplasmic reticulum, which is why the envelope is counted as the first station of the endomembrane system. The Golgi receives vesicles from the rough endoplasmic reticulum rather than touching the nucleus, and neither lysosomes nor mitochondria are continuous with the envelope.","topic":"Nucleus","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which sequence traces a protein bound for secretion correctly, from where it is built to where it leaves the cell?","options":["Smooth endoplasmic reticulum, transport vesicle, Golgi apparatus, lysosome, plasma membrane","Golgi apparatus, rough endoplasmic reticulum, transport vesicle, secretory vesicle, plasma membrane","Rough endoplasmic reticulum, transport vesicle, Golgi apparatus, secretory vesicle, plasma membrane","Rough endoplasmic reticulum, lysosome, Golgi apparatus, transport vesicle, plasma membrane"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Protein for export is built on ribosomes bound to the rough endoplasmic reticulum, travels by transport vesicle to the Golgi for modification and sorting, and leaves in a secretory vesicle that fuses with the plasma membrane. The smooth endoplasmic reticulum handles lipids rather than exported protein, the Golgi never comes before the rough endoplasmic reticulum, and a lysosome is a destination for digestion rather than a stop on the way out.","topic":"Endomembrane System","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A young child's nerve cells fill with undigested lipid, swell, and eventually die. Which organelle has failed, and what is its normal job?","options":["The lysosome, which digests macromolecules and worn out organelles","The peroxisome, which breaks down fatty acids and hydrogen peroxide","The smooth endoplasmic reticulum, which stores calcium and builds lipid","The mitochondrion, which produces most of the cell's ATP"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"Material building up inside a cell that should have been broken down points to the lysosome, the organelle that carries the hydrolytic enzymes for digestion and autophagy. A peroxisome problem would show up as failure to handle fatty acids and hydrogen peroxide, and a mitochondrial problem strikes the most energy hungry tissues rather than filling cells with stored lipid.","topic":"Organelle Identification","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Cardiac muscle cells are packed with mitochondria far more densely than adipocytes are. Which structural feature of the mitochondrion explains why that packing matters so much?","options":["Nuclear pores, which let gene products leave quickly","A single membrane, which keeps its enzymes close to the cytosol","Ribosomes on the outer surface, which build contractile protein","Cristae, folds of the inner membrane that multiply the surface available for ATP production"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The mitochondrion has a double membrane, and the inner one folds into cristae that greatly increase the working surface, so a cell that never stops contracting stocks up on them. Nuclear pores belong to the nucleus, the single membrane description is wrong for a mitochondrion, and the ribosomes a mitochondrion carries are inside it and make its own proteins rather than contractile protein.","topic":"Organelle Identification","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A liver biopsy from a patient on long term medication shows hepatocytes with a strikingly expanded smooth endoplasmic reticulum. Which job of that organelle best explains the change?","options":["Building protein for export to other tissues","Assembling ribosomal subunits for the cytoplasm","Detoxifying drugs, along with lipid synthesis and calcium storage","Digesting worn out organelles and debris"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The smooth endoplasmic reticulum detoxifies drugs and other chemicals in the liver, so sustained drug exposure expands it. Secretory protein is built on the rough endoplasmic reticulum, ribosomal subunits are assembled in the nucleolus, and digestion of worn out organelles is lysosomal work.","topic":"Organelle Identification","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which primary germ layer gives rise to the epidermis of the skin and to nervous tissue?","options":["Mesoderm","Ectoderm","Endoderm","Mesenchyme"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Ectoderm is the outer sheet of the early embryo, and it produces the epidermis with its appendages along with the brain, spinal cord, and peripheral nerves. Mesoderm builds most connective tissue and all muscle, endoderm builds the linings of the digestive and respiratory tracts, and mesenchyme is an embryonic connective tissue rather than one of the three germ layers.","topic":"Embryonic Germ Layers","dok":1,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Once you have a sheet of epithelium in focus under the microscope, what is the rule for naming it?","options":["Name the shape of the deepest cells first, then say whether the tissue is vascular","Name the tissue underneath it first, then count the layers above it","Name the type of junction first, then name the shape of the basal cells","Name the number of cell layers first, then the shape of the cells at the free surface"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"Epithelium is classified by layering and then by shape, and the shape you report is always the shape at the apical or free surface. Basal cell shape can be misleading in a stratified sheet, all epithelium is avascular so vascularity never distinguishes types, and junctions are not visible on a routine slide.","topic":"Epithelial Identification","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A slide taken from a hollow organ shows several layers of epithelium with rounded, dome shaped cells at the free surface, and the note with the slide says the organ was empty when the sample was taken. Which tissue is this, and which organ is it from?","options":["Stratified squamous epithelium, from the esophagus","Transitional epithelium, from the urinary bladder","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium, from the trachea","Simple cuboidal epithelium, from a kidney tubule"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Surface cells that sit rounded when the organ is empty and flatten as it fills are the identifying feature of transitional epithelium, which lines the bladder, the ureters, and part of the urethra. Stratified squamous keeps flat surface cells whether stretched or not, pseudostratified columnar is a single layer that only looks stacked, and simple cuboidal is one layer of box shaped cells.","topic":"Epithelial Identification","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"What lies immediately deep to the basement membrane of an epithelium, and why does that arrangement matter?","options":["Connective tissue carrying blood vessels, because epithelium has none of its own","A second epithelial sheet, because the load has to be shared between layers","Muscle tissue, because the epithelium is dragged across the surface as it works","A serous cavity, because the free surface has to stay lubricated"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"Epithelium has no vessels of its own, so it sits on a basement membrane with vascular connective tissue underneath and takes its nutrients by diffusion from below. That is also why destroying the vessels in the layer beneath an epithelium, as a deep burn does, makes regeneration impossible.","topic":"Basement Membrane","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which junction seals the gap between neighboring epithelial cells so that material has to pass through the cells rather than between them?","options":["Desmosome","Gap junction","Tight junction","Hemidesmosome"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Tight junctions are built from claudins and occludins that fuse adjacent membranes together near the apical surface, which blocks leakage between cells and keeps the apical and basolateral surfaces distinct. Desmosomes rivet cells together against tension, gap junctions open a channel for small ions and molecules, and hemidesmosomes anchor a cell downward to the basement membrane.","topic":"Cell Junctions","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Cardiac muscle cells have to hold together under repeated tension and also pass ions directly from one cell to the next. Which pair of junctions does that work?","options":["Tight junctions and adherens junctions","Desmosomes and gap junctions","Hemidesmosomes and tight junctions","Adherens junctions and hemidesmosomes"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Desmosomes give the strong cell to cell binding that keeps contracting fibers from pulling apart, and gap junctions give the connexin channels that let the impulse spread from one cell to the next, and both sit in the intercalated disc. Tight junctions seal the spaces between epithelial cells, adherens junctions add mechanical stability but carry no channel, and hemidesmosomes anchor a cell downward to a basement membrane rather than to its neighbor.","topic":"Cell Junctions","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which three ingredients are present in every connective tissue, from areolar tissue to bone to blood?","options":["Cells, fibers, and ground substance","Cells, a basement membrane, and cilia","Collagen, elastin, and keratin","Cells, lacunae, and canaliculi"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"Every connective tissue is cells scattered in an extracellular matrix, and that matrix is fibers plus ground substance, so changing the proportions is what makes blood liquid, cartilage firm, and bone hard. Basement membranes and cilia belong to epithelium, keratin is an epithelial protein, and lacunae and canaliculi are specific to cartilage and bone rather than general to the category.","topic":"Connective Tissue Identification","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A slide shows dense bundles of collagen all running in the same direction, with flattened fibroblast nuclei squeezed between the bundles. Which tissue is it, and what does that fiber direction let the tissue resist?","options":["Dense irregular connective tissue, which resists pull coming from any direction","Areolar connective tissue, which resists compression from above","Elastic cartilage, which resists repeated bending","Dense regular connective tissue, which resists pull along one line"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"Parallel collagen bundles with fibroblast nuclei wedged between them is dense regular connective tissue, the build of tendons and ligaments, which are loaded along a single axis. Dense irregular tissue has collagen running every way and shows up in the reticular dermis and organ capsules, and areolar tissue is loose and open rather than densely packed.","topic":"Connective Tissue Identification","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A slide shows cells that each hold one large clear droplet, with the nucleus pushed flat against the rim of the cell and very little matrix between cells. Which tissue is this, and where would you normally find it?","options":["Reticular tissue, in the wall of the trachea","Hyaline cartilage, at the end of a long bone","Adipose tissue, in the hypodermis and around the kidneys","Areolar tissue, inside a tendon"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"An adipocyte stores a single fat droplet so large that it flattens the nucleus against the cell membrane, and adipose tissue lies in the hypodermis, around the kidneys, and in the orbit. Reticular tissue forms a fine mesh in lymph nodes, spleen, and bone marrow, and hyaline cartilage would show chondrocytes sitting in lacunae in a glassy matrix instead.","topic":"Connective Tissue Identification","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A slide shows cells sitting in lacunae inside a smooth glassy matrix with no fibers visible at ordinary staining. Which tissue is it, and where would you expect to find it?","options":["Hyaline cartilage, at the ends of long bones and in the trachea","Fibrocartilage, in the intervertebral discs and the pubic symphysis","Elastic cartilage, in the external ear and the epiglottis","Compact bone, in the shaft of the femur"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"A glassy matrix with no visible fibers and chondrocytes in lacunae is hyaline cartilage, which gives joints a smooth surface and supports the airway. Fibrocartilage shows thick obvious collagen bundles, elastic cartilage shows a dense web of elastic fibers, and compact bone shows osteons with a central canal ringed by lamellae.","topic":"Connective Tissue Identification","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A slide shows cells that branch, carry a single central nucleus, show clear cross striations, and meet each other at dark transverse lines. Which tissue is it, and which feature is the giveaway?","options":["Skeletal muscle, identified by its nuclei at the edge of the fiber","Smooth muscle, identified by its spindle shape","Dense regular connective tissue, identified by its parallel bundles","Cardiac muscle, identified by intercalated discs together with branching"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"Branching fibers joined by intercalated discs occur nowhere else in the body, so that pair settles the identification as cardiac muscle. Skeletal muscle fibers are long, unbranched, and multinucleate with the nuclei pushed to the periphery, and smooth muscle is spindle shaped with one central nucleus and no striations at all.","topic":"Muscle Tissue Identification","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which sequence follows a signal through a multipolar neuron in the correct direction?","options":["Axon terminals, axon, axon hillock, cell body, dendrites","Dendrites, cell body, axon hillock, axon, axon terminals","Cell body, dendrites, axon hillock, axon, axon terminals","Dendrites, axon hillock, axon, cell body, axon terminals"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Signals arrive on the dendrites, converge on the cell body, leave at the axon hillock, travel down the axon, and reach the terminals. The other options either run the sequence backward or place the hillock and cell body out of order, and the hillock is always the transition between the cell body and the axon.","topic":"Nervous Tissue Identification","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A surgeon opens a knee joint, passes through the fibrous capsule, and reaches the layer that lines the joint cavity and produces its lubricating fluid. Which membrane is that?","options":["Cutaneous membrane","Mucous membrane","Synovial membrane","Serous membrane"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"A synovial membrane lines the cavity of a freely movable joint and secretes the synovial fluid that lubricates the cartilage surfaces. The cutaneous membrane is the skin itself, mucous membranes line passages that open to the outside of the body, and serous membranes line closed cavities such as the pleural and peritoneal spaces.","topic":"Body Membranes","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A goblet cell scattered among the columnar cells of the intestinal lining is best classified as which kind of gland?","options":["A multicellular endocrine gland","A unicellular endocrine gland","A multicellular holocrine gland","A unicellular exocrine gland"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The goblet cell is the classic single celled exocrine gland, releasing mucus onto a free surface rather than into the bloodstream. Endocrine glands are ductless and release into blood, and holocrine secretion destroys the whole cell, which is the sebaceous pattern rather than the goblet cell pattern.","topic":"Glandular Epithelium","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which pairing correctly matches a layer of the skin, or the layer just beneath it, with the tissue it is made of?","options":["Epidermis, keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Dermis, simple columnar epithelium","Hypodermis, dense regular connective tissue","Epidermis, areolar connective tissue"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The epidermis is keratinized stratified squamous epithelium and is avascular, which is why it depends on diffusion from the dermis. The dermis is connective tissue rather than epithelium, and the hypodermis is areolar and adipose tissue rather than the parallel collagen of dense regular tissue.","topic":"Skin Layers and Hypodermis","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which list gives the epidermal strata of thick skin in order from deep to superficial?","options":["Corneum, lucidum, granulosum, spinosum, basale","Basale, granulosum, spinosum, lucidum, corneum","Basale, spinosum, granulosum, lucidum, corneum","Spinosum, basale, granulosum, corneum, lucidum"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Cells are born in the basale and climb through the spinosum, granulosum, lucidum, and corneum as they keratinize and die. The first option runs the list backward, and the other two swap the spinosum and granulosum or misplace the corneum, which is always the outermost layer.","topic":"Epidermal Strata","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"After a summer of sun exposure, a patient's skin is noticeably darker. Which cell is responsible, and what does its product actually do inside the neighboring cells?","options":["The keratinocyte, which stores carotene in the stratum corneum","The melanocyte, which passes melanin granules that shield the keratinocyte nucleus","The intraepidermal macrophage, which absorbs ultraviolet light in the stratum spinosum","The tactile cell, which releases pigment when a sensory neuron fires"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Melanocytes sit in the stratum basale and hand melanin granules to keratinocytes through long slender processes, and inside the keratinocyte the granules gather on the surface side of the nucleus to shield the DNA. Keratinocytes make keratin rather than pigment, intraepidermal macrophages handle immune surveillance and are themselves damaged by ultraviolet light, and tactile cells serve touch.","topic":"Epidermal Cells","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A forensic examiner needs to know which part of the skin actually creates a fingerprint, and a surgeon needs to know which part carries most of the tensile strength. Which answer gives both correctly?","options":["The papillary layer raises the epidermal ridges, and the reticular layer carries the strength","The stratum corneum raises the ridges, and the papillary layer carries the strength","The reticular layer raises the ridges, and the hypodermis carries the strength","The stratum basale raises the ridges, and the papillary layer carries the strength"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"Dermal papillae in the thin superficial papillary layer push up into the epidermis and produce the ridges you see as a fingerprint, and they also carry the capillary loops and Meissner corpuscles. The reticular layer is four fifths of the dermis and is dense irregular connective tissue, which is where the tensile strength and the resistance to stretch come from.","topic":"Dermis Layers","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which structure inside a hair follicle is the germinal layer that actually divides to lengthen the hair?","options":["The arrector pili muscle","The papilla of the hair","The hair matrix","The cuticle of the shaft"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The hair matrix in the bulb is the germinal layer arising from the stratum basale, and its dividing cells push the shaft upward and also form the internal root sheath. The papilla is areolar connective tissue carrying the blood supply to the bulb, the arrector pili is smooth muscle that raises the shaft, and the cuticle is the dead outer layer of the shaft itself.","topic":"Accessory Structures of the Skin","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient repeatedly bites the skin at the base of a fingernail and develops an infection that destroys the tissue just proximal to the nail root. Which structure was lost, and what is the consequence?","options":["The hyponychium, so the free edge is no longer secured to the fingertip","The nail matrix, so the nail can no longer build new cells and stops growing","The lunula, so the pale crescent at the base of the nail body disappears","The eponychium, so the seal between the nail wall and the skin is lost"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The nail matrix lies proximal to the nail root and holds the dividing cells that produce the nail plate, so destroying it stops or deforms nail growth. The hyponychium is the thickened stratum corneum under the free edge, the lunula is only the pale crescent where thickened epithelium hides the vessels below, and the eponychium is the cuticle, so losing any of those three would not stop the nail from growing.","topic":"Accessory Structures of the Skin","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A teenager notices that body odor has appeared in the axillae since puberty, while the sweating on the palms during an exam has been happening since early childhood. Which explanation matches both observations?","options":["Both sets are eccrine glands, and eccrine sweat simply turns odorous once the glands mature at puberty","Both sets are apocrine glands, and the palms carry a far denser population of them than the axillae","The axillary glands are sebaceous and holocrine, while the palmar glands are ceruminous and open into follicles","The axillary glands are apocrine and open into hair follicles at puberty, while the palmar glands are eccrine from infancy"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"Apocrine sweat glands are limited to places like the axillae, groin, and areolae, empty into hair follicles, add lipids and proteins that skin bacteria break down into odor, and switch on at puberty. Eccrine glands are spread over most of the body, are densest on the forehead, palms, and soles, open directly onto the epidermal surface, and have been working since soon after birth.","topic":"Skin Glands","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A surgeon removes a torn wedge of tissue from inside the knee joint. Under the microscope it shows thick parallel bundles of collagen with chondrocytes lined up in rows between them. Which tissue was removed, and where else is the same tissue found?","options":["Hyaline cartilage, also found in the external ear and the epiglottis","Elastic cartilage, also found in the costal cartilages and the trachea","Fibrocartilage, also found in the intervertebral discs and the pubic symphysis","Hyaline cartilage, also found capping the articulating surfaces of long bones"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Thick collagen bundles with chondrocytes in rows is fibrocartilage, the tissue of the menisci, the intervertebral discs, and the pubic symphysis. Hyaline cartilage looks glassy because its collagen is too fine to resolve, and elastic cartilage shows a dark mesh of elastic fibers, so neither matches the description even though both options name real hyaline locations.","topic":"Cartilage","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A cartilage model in a fetal limb is getting both longer and thicker at the same time. Which statement correctly pairs each kind of growth with where it happens?","options":["Appositional growth from within the matrix lengthens it, and interstitial growth at the perichondrium thickens it","Interstitial growth from within the matrix lengthens it, and appositional growth at the perichondrium thickens it","Both lengthening and thickening come from cells of the perichondrium adding new matrix at the surface","Both lengthening and thickening come from chondrocytes dividing deep within the matrix"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Interstitial growth expands cartilage from within because the chondrocytes already sitting in lacunae divide and lay down matrix around themselves. Appositional growth works from the outside in, starting at the perichondrium, which is why it adds width rather than length. The two options that pick a single mechanism for both directions ignore that cartilage grows in two places at once.","topic":"Cartilage","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which list gives a correct example of each bone shape in the order long, short, flat, irregular, sesamoid?","options":["Humerus, sternum, carpal, vertebra, patella","Femur, vertebra, parietal bone, carpal, patella","Femur, carpal, vertebra, sternum, patella","Humerus, carpal, sternum, vertebra, patella"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The humerus is long, a carpal is short and roughly cube shaped, the sternum is flat, a vertebra is irregular, and the patella is sesamoid. The other lists shuffle those middle three, putting a carpal, a vertebra, or the sternum somewhere it does not belong.","topic":"Bone Classification","dok":1,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which membrane lines the medullary cavity and also covers the trabeculae inside a bone?","options":["Periosteum","Endosteum","Articular cartilage","Perichondrium"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The endosteum is the thin membrane on every internal bone surface, including the walls of the marrow cavity and the struts of spongy bone. The periosteum covers the outer surface instead, articular cartilage caps the epiphysis at a joint, and perichondrium wraps cartilage rather than bone.","topic":"Long Bone Anatomy","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A tendon pulls hard on the shaft of a bone during a heavy lift, yet the periosteum does not peel away from the surface. Which structure holds it down?","options":["Perforating fibers, also called Sharpey fibers, embedded in the bone matrix","Canaliculi that link the lacunae of neighbouring osteocytes","Circumferential lamellae that wrap the outer surface of the shaft","The interosseous membrane that binds neighbouring long bones"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"Perforating or Sharpey fibers are bundles of collagen that leave the periosteum and embed in the bone matrix, anchoring the covering against pull. Circumferential lamellae are real rings of bone at that surface but they are matrix, not an anchor, canaliculi connect cells deep inside, and an interosseous membrane joins two bones to each other rather than a covering to a bone.","topic":"Long Bone Anatomy","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"On a cross section of compact bone a student sees several complete osteons and, wedged into the spaces between them, irregular arcs of lamellae with no central canal of their own. What are those arcs?","options":["Circumferential lamellae, the rings that wrap the entire outer and inner surface of the shaft","Trabeculae, the struts that give spongy bone its open lattice","Concentric lamellae belonging to an osteon that happened to be cut on the diagonal","Interstitial lamellae, the leftover fragments of older osteons that remodeling has cut into"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"Interstitial lamellae are the remnants of osteons that were partly resorbed during remodeling, so they are incomplete and lack a canal of their own. Circumferential lamellae do exist but they run around the whole shaft at its surfaces rather than filling gaps between osteons, and an obliquely cut osteon would still show its central canal.","topic":"Bone Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which canal runs crosswise through compact bone, at right angles to the long axis, and links one central canal to another?","options":["The central canal, also called the Haversian canal","The perforating canal, also called the Volkmann canal","A canaliculus running between two lacunae","The nutrient foramen of the shaft"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The perforating or Volkmann canal is the crosswise connection between central canals, which is how vessels get from the periosteal surface into the depths of the bone. The central canal runs lengthwise at the core of each osteon, a canaliculus is a microscopic channel between cells, and the nutrient foramen is a single opening in the shaft rather than an internal network.","topic":"Bone Microanatomy","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A section through a parietal bone of the skull is placed under the microscope. Which arrangement of bone tissue would you expect to see?","options":["Osteons packed through the full thickness, with marrow inside their central canals","A layer of spongy bone on the outside covering a solid core of compact bone","A layer of spongy bone sandwiched between two plates of compact bone","Trabeculae through the full thickness, with red marrow filling the spaces"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"A flat bone is built as a sandwich, two thin plates of compact bone with spongy bone between them, which is strong for its weight and holds marrow. Compact bone lies on the outside of a bone rather than forming an inner core, and the marrow of a flat bone sits in the spongy layer between the plates, not inside the central canals of osteons.","topic":"Bone Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which bone cell sits alone in a lacuna, maintains the matrix around it day to day, and got there by walling itself in with the matrix it once secreted?","options":["Osteogenic cell","Osteoblast","Osteocyte","Osteoclast"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"An osteoblast that walls itself in with the matrix it secretes becomes an osteocyte, the maintenance cell that lives in a lacuna. The osteogenic cell is the dividing stem cell earlier in that same lineage, the osteoblast is still on the surface actively building, and the osteoclast is a large multinucleated cell from a different line that resorbs bone.","topic":"Bone Cells","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient takes a medication that shuts down osteoclast activity almost completely while the other bone cells keep working normally. In a bone that is still growing, which outcome would you predict?","options":["The medullary cavity would fail to widen even as the shaft thickens from the outside","New bone matrix would stop being deposited beneath the periosteum","The osteocytes would leave their lacunae and revert to osteogenic cells","The epiphyseal plate would stop making new cartilage and close early"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"A bone widens because osteoblasts add bone at the periosteal surface while osteoclasts remove it from the endosteal side, hollowing out the cavity, so blocking the osteoclasts leaves a thick shaft with a narrow cavity. Deposition under the periosteum is osteoblast work and continues, osteocytes do not reverse back into stem cells, and lengthening at the plate is a cartilage process that osteoclasts do not drive.","topic":"Bone Cells","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A fetal skeleton is stained so that bones built on a hyaline cartilage model take one color and bones laid down directly inside a fibrous membrane take another. Which group takes the membrane stain?","options":["The femur, the humerus, and the vertebrae","The frontal bone, the parietal bones, and the clavicle","The tibia, the ribs, and the sacrum","The carpal bones, the tarsal bones, and the sternum"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Intramembranous ossification builds the flat bones of the skull and the clavicle straight inside a connective tissue membrane. Everything else in the lists, the long bones of the limbs, the vertebrae, the ribs, the sacrum, and the short bones of the wrist and ankle, forms endochondrally by replacing a hyaline cartilage model.","topic":"Ossification","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"In the epiphyseal plate, which zone lies closest to the diaphysis?","options":["The zone of resting cartilage","The zone of proliferation","The zone of hypertrophy","The zone of ossification"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The five zones run resting, proliferation, hypertrophy, calcification, and ossification, from the epiphysis down toward the shaft. The zone of ossification is where cartilage is finally replaced by bone next to the diaphysis, and the zone of resting cartilage is at the opposite end, anchoring the plate to the epiphysis.","topic":"Growth Plate","dok":1,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A ten year old fractures the distal femur and the fracture line runs straight through the epiphyseal plate. Why does the surgeon warn the family about the future growth of that limb?","options":["The periosteum has been torn, so the bone can no longer grow wider at the shaft","The medullary cavity has been opened, so yellow marrow will replace the red marrow","Damage to the plate can slow or stop lengthening, so that leg may end up shorter than the other","The epiphyseal line has been broken, so the epiphysis will separate from the articular cartilage"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The epiphyseal plate is the only site where a long bone lengthens, so a fracture through it can arrest interstitial growth and leave one limb short. Widening happens by appositional growth at the periosteum and is a separate process, and the epiphyseal line is the closed remnant of the plate in an adult, not a structure a growing child still has.","topic":"Bone Growth","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A student is sorting the bones of a disarticulated skull into a cranial pile and a facial pile. Which bone belongs in the facial pile?","options":["Ethmoid bone","Palatine bone","Sphenoid bone","Temporal bone"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The palatine bones are paired facial bones that form the posterior part of the hard palate and part of the orbit. The ethmoid, sphenoid, and temporal bones are all cranial bones, and the ethmoid is the easiest to misplace because it contributes to the nasal cavity and the orbit even though it is part of the braincase.","topic":"Cranial and Facial Bones","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"After a blow to the front of the head a patient can no longer smell anything, although vision and eye movement are normal. Which structure was most likely damaged, and which bone carries it?","options":["The cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone","The sella turcica of the sphenoid bone","The petrous part of the temporal bone","The crista galli of the frontal bone"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The olfactory nerve filaments pass through the perforations of the cribriform plate, so a fracture there shears them and smell is lost while the optic nerve in its own canal is spared. The sella turcica holds the pituitary, the petrous temporal houses the ear, and the crista galli is a real marking but it belongs to the ethmoid, not the frontal bone.","topic":"Skull Markings","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A pituitary tumor enlarges over several years and begins to erode the bony seat the gland sits in. Which marking, on which bone, is being eroded?","options":["The hypoglossal canal of the occipital bone","The sella turcica on the body of the sphenoid bone","The crista galli of the ethmoid bone","The internal acoustic meatus of the temporal bone"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The sella turcica is the saddle on the body of the sphenoid whose deep seat cradles the pituitary gland, so a growing pituitary mass remodels that bone first. The other three are correctly paired markings and bones, but the hypoglossal canal passes a nerve to the tongue, the crista galli anchors a fold of the brain covering, and the internal acoustic meatus carries the nerves for hearing and facial movement.","topic":"Skull Markings","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A pediatrician examining a four month old finds that the soft spot where the frontal and parietal bones meet is bulging and tense. Which fontanelle is being examined, and what does a bulging one suggest?","options":["The posterior fontanelle, suggesting dehydration","The posterior fontanelle, suggesting raised pressure inside the skull","The anterior fontanelle, suggesting dehydration","The anterior fontanelle, suggesting raised pressure inside the skull"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The anterior fontanelle lies where the frontal and parietal bones meet, it is the largest, and it stays open to about 18 to 24 months, so it is the one still available to check at four months. A bulging fontanelle points to raised pressure inside the skull, while a sunken one points to dehydration, and the posterior fontanelle has usually closed by about two months.","topic":"Sutures and Fontanelles","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient has a deviated nasal septum. Which two bones, along with the septal cartilage, build that septum?","options":["The vomer and the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid","The vomer and the palatine process of the maxilla","The inferior nasal conchae and the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid","The nasal bones and the cribriform plate of the ethmoid"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The septum is the vomer below, the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid above, and the septal cartilage in front. The palatine process of the maxilla builds the hard palate underneath the cavity, the inferior nasal conchae project from the lateral wall rather than the midline, and the cribriform plate is the roof.","topic":"Nasal Cavity and Palate","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"An abscess at the root of an upper molar spreads upward into an air filled space that lies just above the tooth. Which sinus has been entered, and which bone holds it?","options":["The frontal sinus, in the frontal bone","The sphenoidal sinus, in the body of the sphenoid","The maxillary sinus, in the maxilla","The ethmoidal air cells, in the lateral masses of the ethmoid"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The maxillary sinus is the largest paranasal sinus and sits in the body of the maxilla directly above the roots of the upper teeth, which is why a dental infection can reach it. The frontal sinus lies above the orbits, the sphenoidal sinus sits deep in the body of the sphenoid, and the ethmoidal air cells lie between the orbits, all far from a molar root.","topic":"Paranasal Sinuses","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A surgeon planning an approach to the base of the skull must avoid the opening that carries the internal carotid artery into the cranium. Which opening is that, and which bone does it pierce?","options":["The jugular foramen, between the temporal and occipital bones","The carotid canal, in the temporal bone","The foramen ovale, in the sphenoid bone","The stylomastoid foramen, in the temporal bone"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The carotid canal of the temporal bone is the route the internal carotid artery takes into the skull. The jugular foramen nearby carries the internal jugular vein out, the foramen ovale passes a branch of the trigeminal nerve to the lower jaw, and the stylomastoid foramen is the exit of the facial nerve between the styloid and mastoid processes.","topic":"Skull Foramina","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Pressure inside the rigid skull rises after a head injury and brain tissue is forced downward. Through which opening is that tissue pushed, and what normally passes through it?","options":["The optic canal, which normally passes the optic nerve","The internal acoustic meatus, which normally passes the nerves for hearing and facial movement","The hypoglossal canal, which normally passes the nerve to the tongue muscles","The foramen magnum, which normally passes the spinal cord and the vertebral arteries"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The foramen magnum is the one large opening in the floor of the cranium, so rising pressure drives brain tissue down through it and onto the brainstem, which is why it is an emergency. The other three openings are real and correctly matched to their contents, but each is a narrow channel for a nerve or vessel and far too small to pass brain tissue.","topic":"Skull Foramina","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"How many separate vertebrae are in the cervical, thoracic, and lumbar regions, and how many bones does the adult vertebral column contain in total?","options":["7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, and 26 bones in the adult column","12 cervical, 7 thoracic, 5 lumbar, and 26 bones in the adult column","7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, and 33 bones in the adult column","5 cervical, 12 thoracic, 7 lumbar, and 24 bones in the adult column"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The counts are 7, 12, and 5 from the top down, and once the 5 sacral and 4 coccygeal vertebrae fuse the adult column is 26 bones, the 24 separate vertebrae plus the sacrum and the coccyx. The figure 33 is the developmental count before fusion, and the option that reverses cervical and thoracic ignores that only the thoracic vertebrae carry ribs.","topic":"Vertebral Column","dok":1,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A one year old is just beginning to stand and take steps. Which curvature of the vertebral column is developing at this stage, and how is it classified?","options":["The thoracic curvature, a primary curvature","The sacral curvature, a secondary curvature","The lumbar curvature, a secondary curvature","The cervical curvature, a primary curvature"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The lumbar curvature is secondary and appears as a child stands and walks, balancing the trunk over the pelvis. The cervical curvature is also secondary but it develops earlier, when the infant lifts its head, and the thoracic and sacral curvatures are primary and present from birth.","topic":"Vertebral Curvatures","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient lifts a heavy box badly and the gel-like core of a lumbar disc pushes posterolaterally through the torn outer rings. Which structures are involved, and why does the pain shoot down the leg?","options":["The anulus fibrosus pushes through the nucleus pulposus and presses on the spinal cord inside the vertebral canal","The nucleus pulposus pushes through the anulus fibrosus and presses on a spinal nerve root at the intervertebral foramen","The nucleus pulposus pushes forward through the anterior longitudinal ligament and presses on the sympathetic trunk","The anulus fibrosus tears away from the vertebral body and presses on the vertebral artery"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The nucleus pulposus is the soft center and the anulus fibrosus is the tough outer ring, so a herniation is the nucleus escaping through the anulus. A posterolateral escape lands right where a spinal nerve leaves through the intervertebral foramen, which is why the pain travels down the limb the nerve serves rather than staying in the back.","topic":"Intervertebral Disc","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A loose vertebra on the lab bench has a heart-shaped body, a long spinous process angled sharply downward, and small smooth facets on the body and on the transverse processes. Where did it come from, and what articulated with those facets?","options":["The thoracic region, and a pair of ribs articulated with those facets","The cervical region, and the vertebral arteries passed through those facets","The lumbar region, and the sacrum articulated with those facets","The cervical region, and the occipital condyles articulated with those facets"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"Costal facets are the thoracic giveaway, and the heart-shaped body with a long downward spinous process confirms it. Cervical vertebrae are identified by transverse foramina, which are openings rather than facets, and lumbar vertebrae have thick kidney-shaped bodies with short blunt spines and no costal facets at all.","topic":"Regional Vertebrae","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A fall drives the head backward and fractures the bony peg that projects upward from the second cervical vertebra. Which structure is broken, and which movement is lost at the joint it serves?","options":["The vertebra prominens, and the nodding movement that says yes is lost","The transverse process of the atlas, and lateral flexion of the neck is lost","The dens of the axis, and the rotation that shakes the head no is lost","The anterior arch of the atlas, and flexion of the neck is lost"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The dens, or odontoid process, is the peg of the axis that the atlas pivots around, so the atlanto-axial joint provides the head shake that says no. The nodding yes movement happens at the atlanto-occipital joint between the occipital condyles and the atlas, and the vertebra prominens is simply the long spinous process of C7.","topic":"Atlas and Axis","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A clinician needs to count a patient's ribs and starts by feeling for a bony ridge on the front of the chest. Which landmark is being used, and what rib lies at that level?","options":["The jugular notch, level with the first rib","The xiphoid process, level with the seventh rib","The costal margin, level with the fifth rib","The sternal angle, level with the second rib"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The sternal angle is the ridge where the manubrium meets the body of the sternum, and it sits at the level of the second rib, which makes it the standard starting point for counting. The jugular notch is the dip at the top of the manubrium rather than a ridge, and the xiphoid process is the small tip at the bottom, useful for hand placement in CPR but not for counting from the top.","topic":"Thoracic Cage","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"How do rib pairs 8, 9, and 10 reach the sternum, and what does that make them?","options":["Each joins the costal cartilage of the rib above rather than the sternum itself, which makes them false ribs","Each joins the sternum directly by its own costal cartilage, which makes them true ribs","Each has no anterior attachment at all, which makes them floating ribs","Each joins the xiphoid process directly, which makes them false ribs"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"Pairs 8 to 10 are false ribs because their cartilage merges with the cartilage of the rib above instead of reaching the sternum on its own. True ribs are pairs 1 to 7, each with its own direct cartilage to the sternum, and floating ribs are pairs 11 and 12, the false ribs with no anterior attachment at all.","topic":"Thoracic Cage","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A cyclist falls onto an outstretched arm and breaks the middle third of the most commonly fractured bone in the body. Which bone is it, and what does its position explain about the injury?","options":["The scapula, which takes the force because it rides directly against the back of the rib cage","The humerus, which takes the force because its surgical neck is the narrowest point of the limb","The clavicle, which takes the force because it is the slim strut linking the limb to the axial skeleton","The acromion, which takes the force because it is the highest point of the shoulder"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The clavicle is the only bony link between the upper limb and the axial skeleton, so a force travelling up the arm passes through this slim S-shaped strut and usually snaps it in the middle third. The scapula is held mainly by muscle and slides on the rib cage, and the surgical neck of the humerus and the acromion do fracture, but neither is the most commonly broken bone in the body.","topic":"Pectoral Girdle","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"At the elbow, which distal marking of the humerus meets which forearm bone?","options":["The trochlea meets the radius, and the capitulum meets the ulna","The olecranon fossa meets the radius, and the trochlea meets the ulna","The capitulum meets the radius, and the medial epicondyle meets the ulna","The capitulum meets the radius, and the trochlea meets the ulna"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The capitulum is the lateral knob and it receives the disc-shaped head of the radius, the lateral forearm bone, while the medial spool-shaped trochlea is gripped by the trochlear notch of the ulna. The olecranon fossa is a depression that receives the olecranon when the elbow straightens rather than an articulating surface for the radius, and the epicondyles are muscle attachment points that touch neither forearm bone.","topic":"Arm and Forearm","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient wakes at night with numbness and tingling in the thumb, index, and middle fingers. Which structure is being compressed, and what forms the roof of the space it passes through?","options":["The median nerve, in the carpal tunnel roofed by the flexor retinaculum","The ulnar nerve, in the carpal tunnel roofed by the flexor retinaculum","The median nerve, behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus","The radial nerve, in the radial groove on the posterior shaft of the humerus"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The carpal tunnel is the space between the carpal bones and the flexor retinaculum that roofs them, and it carries the long finger flexor tendons together with the median nerve, so narrowing there produces the classic thumb-side numbness. The ulnar nerve runs behind the medial epicondyle, the funny bone, and sends its jolt to the little finger, and the radial nerve lies in the radial groove of the humerus, far from the wrist.","topic":"The Hand","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which three bones fuse to form each hip bone, and where do they meet?","options":["The ilium, the sacrum, and the pubis, meeting at the obturator foramen","The ilium, the ischium, and the pubis, meeting at the acetabulum","The ilium, the ischium, and the sacrum, meeting at the sacroiliac joint","The ischium, the pubis, and the coccyx, meeting at the pubic symphysis"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Each os coxae begins as three bones, the ilium above, the ischium behind and below, and the pubis in front, and they fuse at the acetabulum, the deep cup that receives the head of the femur. The sacrum and coccyx are axial bones that join the girdle at the sacroiliac joint rather than fuse into it, and the obturator foramen is the large opening framed by the ischium and pubis.","topic":"Pelvic Girdle","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"An older adult with thinned bone falls and suffers what is usually called a broken hip. Which part of the femur is most often fractured, and why does the injury threaten the head of the bone?","options":["The greater trochanter, because the gluteal muscles pull the fragments apart","The linea aspera, because so many muscles attach along it","The intercondylar fossa, because the cruciate ligaments attach there","The neck, because it is narrow and angled and the blood supply to the head runs close by"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"A broken hip is nearly always a fracture of the femoral neck, the narrow angled region just below the head, and the vessels feeding the head travel along it, so the head itself can lose its supply. The greater trochanter and linea aspera are muscle attachment sites on the shaft, and the intercondylar fossa is at the distal end of the bone, nowhere near the hip.","topic":"Femur","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A runner rolls an ankle and fractures the bony knob on the lateral side of the ankle. Which bone was broken, and what does that tell you about weight bearing afterward?","options":["The medial malleolus of the tibia, so weight bearing is lost because the tibia carries the load","The lateral malleolus of the tibia, so weight bearing is lost because the tibia carries the load","The lateral malleolus of the fibula, and the tibia still carries body weight","The head of the fibula, and the tibia still carries body weight"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The lateral malleolus is the distal end of the fibula and it is the common site in a rolled ankle, while the medial malleolus belongs to the tibia. Because the fibula does not bear body weight and serves mainly for muscle attachment and ankle stability, the tibia keeps carrying the load, which is also why a length of fibula can be taken as a graft. The head of the fibula is at the opposite, proximal end of the bone.","topic":"Leg and Foot","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which single feature separates a synovial joint from both a fibrous joint and a cartilaginous joint, and what functional class does it place the joint in?","options":["The presence of a fluid-filled synovial cavity between the bones, which makes it a diarthrosis","The presence of dense connective tissue holding the bones together, which makes it an amphiarthrosis","The presence of hyaline cartilage on the bone ends, which makes it a synarthrosis","The absence of any ligaments crossing the joint, which makes it a diarthrosis"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"Only synovial joints have a synovial cavity, and every synovial joint is a diarthrosis, meaning freely movable. Dense connective tissue is the hallmark of fibrous joints, hyaline cartilage caps the bone ends of a synovial joint but also joins a synchondrosis, so it does not separate the classes, and synovial joints are full of ligaments rather than free of them.","topic":"Joint Classification","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A tooth is held in its bony socket by the periodontal ligament. How is that joint classified by structure and by function, and what is the joint called?","options":["Cartilaginous and amphiarthrotic, a symphysis","Fibrous and amphiarthrotic, a syndesmosis","Synovial and diarthrotic, a plane joint","Fibrous and synarthrotic, a gomphosis"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"A tooth in its socket is a gomphosis, a cone-shaped peg held by a ligament, and it is fibrous in structure and immovable, a synarthrosis. A syndesmosis is also fibrous but has more connective tissue and more space, so it is slightly movable, as at the distal tibiofibular joint, and a symphysis is cartilaginous with a fibrocartilage disc.","topic":"Fibrous Joints","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Two joints are compared: the joint between the first rib and the manubrium, and the joint between the two pubic bones. How does each one classify?","options":["Both are symphyses, joined by fibrocartilage and slightly movable","The first costal joint is a synchondrosis and the pubic joint is a symphysis","The first costal joint is a symphysis and the pubic joint is a synchondrosis","Both are synchondroses, joined by hyaline cartilage and immovable"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"A synchondrosis is united by hyaline cartilage and is immovable, and the first rib joining the manubrium is the standard example along with the epiphyseal plate. A symphysis is united by a broad flat disc of fibrocartilage, is slightly movable, and always lies in the midline, which fits the pubic symphysis and the joints between vertebral bodies.","topic":"Cartilaginous Joints","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Articular cartilage has no blood vessels of its own, yet its chondrocytes stay alive in a healthy joint. What keeps them supplied, and which part of the joint produces it?","options":["Blood from the vessels of the fibrous membrane, which penetrate the cartilage directly","Interstitial fluid from the periosteum, which continues over the cartilage surface","Synovial fluid, secreted by the synovial membrane lining the capsule","Lymph drained from the articular fat pads inside the capsule"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The synovial membrane, the inner layer of the articular capsule, secretes synovial fluid, and that fluid nourishes the avascular articular cartilage as well as lubricating the joint and clearing debris. Vessels in the fibrous membrane supply the capsule and ligaments but stop short of the cartilage, and the periosteum ends where the articular cartilage begins.","topic":"Synovial Joint Structure","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient can bend and straighten the index finger at the knuckle and can also spread it away from the middle finger and bring it back, but cannot rotate it. Which type of synovial joint is the knuckle, and how many axes does it move around?","options":["A condyloid joint, biaxial","A hinge joint, uniaxial","A saddle joint, biaxial","A ball-and-socket joint, triaxial"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The metacarpophalangeal joint is condyloid, an oval projection sitting in an oval depression, so it is biaxial and allows flexion and extension plus abduction and adduction but no true rotation. A hinge joint such as an interphalangeal joint allows flexion and extension only, a saddle joint is found at the base of the thumb, and a ball-and-socket joint such as the shoulder is triaxial.","topic":"Synovial Joint Types","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A soccer player is struck on the lateral side of a planted knee and tears three structures at once, the injury known as the unhappy triad. Which three are torn, and what does the cruciate ligament among them normally prevent?","options":["The fibular collateral ligament, the lateral meniscus, and the posterior cruciate ligament, which normally stops the tibia sliding forward","The tibial collateral ligament, the lateral meniscus, and the anterior cruciate ligament, which normally stops the tibia sliding backward","The patellar ligament, the medial meniscus, and the posterior cruciate ligament, which normally stops the tibia sliding backward","The tibial collateral ligament, the medial meniscus, and the anterior cruciate ligament, which normally stops the tibia sliding forward"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"A blow from the lateral side opens the medial joint line, so it tears the tibial collateral ligament, the medial meniscus attached to it, and the anterior cruciate ligament. The anterior cruciate is the intracapsular band that stops the tibia sliding forward on the femur, while the posterior cruciate stops it sliding backward and matters most on stairs and inclines.","topic":"Knee Joint","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Order the levels of skeletal muscle organization from largest to smallest.","options":["Muscle, fascicle, muscle fiber, myofibril, myofilament","Muscle, muscle fiber, fascicle, myofilament, myofibril","Fascicle, muscle, myofibril, muscle fiber, myofilament","Muscle, myofibril, fascicle, muscle fiber, myofilament"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"A whole muscle is a bundle of fascicles, each fascicle is a bundle of muscle fibers, each fiber is filled with myofibrils, and each myofibril is built of thick and thin myofilaments. The other three sequences either put the fiber above the fascicle or slot the myofibril in above the cell that contains it.","topic":"Muscle Structure","dok":1,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A slide shows one sarcomere at rest. Which region holds thick filaments only, with no thin filaments overlapping them?","options":["The I band","The zone of overlap","The H zone","The Z disc"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The H zone is the pale center of the A band, the stretch of thick filament that the thin filaments do not reach. The I band is the opposite situation, thin filaments only, the zone of overlap has both by definition, and the Z disc is the protein plate that anchors the thin filaments.","topic":"Sarcomere","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A four-year-old boy has progressive weakness in his hips and shoulders, calves that look enlarged but feel rubbery, and a creatine kinase level in the thousands. Testing shows he cannot make dystrophin. Which structural job is going undone in his muscle fibers?","options":["Anchoring the thick filaments to one another at the M line","Setting the length of the thin filament along its full run","Storing and releasing the calcium held in the terminal cisternae","Linking the thin filaments of the sarcomere out to the sarcolemma"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"Dystrophin transfers the pull of the sarcomere to the cell membrane and the connective tissue outside it, so without it every contraction tears the sarcolemma and the fiber slowly destroys itself. Myomesin binds thick filaments at the M line, nebulin sets thin filament length, and calcium storage belongs to the sarcoplasmic reticulum, none of which is the missing protein here.","topic":"Myofilament Proteins","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"You are handed a slide of muscle tissue. The cells are striated, short and branching, each carries a single central nucleus, and neighboring cells meet end to end at dark cross bands. Which tissue is on the slide, and what are those dark bands?","options":["Skeletal muscle, and the bands are Z discs","Cardiac muscle, and the bands are intercalated discs","Smooth muscle, and the bands are dense bodies","Skeletal muscle, and the bands are intercalated discs"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Branching cells with one central nucleus and intercalated discs are the signature of cardiac muscle. Skeletal fibers are long, unbranched, and multinucleated with the nuclei pushed to the edge, and smooth muscle is spindle-shaped and not striated at all, so neither can be what you are looking at.","topic":"Muscle Tissue Types","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Trace the connective tissue of a skeletal muscle outward from a single fiber. Which sequence is correct, and what do those sheaths become at the end of the muscle?","options":["Endomysium, perimysium, epimysium, all merging into the tendon","Epimysium, perimysium, endomysium, all merging into the tendon","Perimysium, endomysium, epimysium, all merging into the periosteum","Endomysium, epimysium, perimysium, all merging into the aponeurosis"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"Endomysium wraps the fiber, perimysium wraps the fascicle, epimysium wraps the whole muscle, and all three run together into the tendon that attaches to bone. One option reverses the order, one puts epimysium in the middle, and the tendon rather than the periosteum is what the sheaths themselves become.","topic":"Connective Tissue Coverings","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which list names the structures you would label at a neuromuscular junction, moving from the nerve side across to the muscle side?","options":["Motor end plate, synaptic cleft, then the axon terminal with terminal cisternae","Axon terminal with synaptic vesicles, triad, sarcoplasmic reticulum","Schwann cell, T tubule, terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Axon terminal with synaptic vesicles, synaptic cleft, folded motor end plate"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The junction runs axon terminal, then the gap, then the folded region of sarcolemma called the motor end plate. Terminal cisternae, triads, T tubules, and the sarcoplasmic reticulum are all inside the muscle fiber rather than at the synapse, and one option also reverses the direction of travel.","topic":"Neuromuscular Junction","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"What is a triad in a skeletal muscle fiber, and why does its arrangement matter?","options":["Three myofibrils bound together by titin, so the whole fiber pulls as a single rod","One T tubule flanked by two terminal cisternae, so a surface signal reaches deep into the fiber","Two T tubules flanked by one terminal cistern, so calcium is drawn back out of the sarcoplasm","Three sarcomeres in series sharing one Z disc, so shortening adds up along the myofibril"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The triad is the meeting point where an inward fold of the sarcolemma sits between two swollen ends of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, which is how a signal at the surface reaches the deepest sarcomeres almost at once. The other options invert the counts or describe myofibrils and sarcomeres, which are contractile structures rather than the membrane arrangement.","topic":"Muscle Fiber","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A surgeon is choosing a muscle to move to a new site and needs one that produces a large force over a fairly short distance. Which fascicle arrangement fits that requirement, and why?","options":["Parallel, because long fascicles let the muscle shorten a great deal","Circular, because concentric rings close an opening tightly","Pennate, because many short fascicles pull on a central tendon at an angle","Convergent, because a broad origin spreads the pull over a wide area"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Pennate muscles pack far more short fascicles per unit of muscle, so many more fibers pull on the tendon and power is high even though the range of shortening is small. Parallel muscles make the opposite trade, and circular and convergent describe shape and sphincter or fan geometry rather than the power to range trade-off.","topic":"Fascicle Arrangement","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient with a common fibular nerve injury at the neck of the fibula cannot lift the front of her foot, so her toes drag as she walks. Which compartment of the leg has lost its supply?","options":["The anterior compartment, holding tibialis anterior and extensor digitorum longus","The posterior compartment, holding gastrocnemius and soleus","The medial compartment, holding the adductor group","The deep posterior compartment, holding tibialis posterior and flexor hallucis longus"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"Dorsiflexion belongs to the anterior compartment, so losing it produces the dragging toe of foot drop. The posterior compartments plantar flex and invert instead, and the adductor group is a compartment of the thigh, not the leg.","topic":"Leg Muscles","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Decode the name flexor carpi radialis. Which reading of the three parts is correct?","options":["It flexes, it acts at the ankle, and it arises from the radius","It is flexible in shape, it lies over the carpals, and it is the largest of its group","It flexes, it has three heads, and it inserts on the radius","It flexes, it acts at the wrist, and it lies on the radial side"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"Muscle names combine action, region, and position, so this one tells you the action is flexion, carpi points to the wrist, and radialis places it on the thumb side of the forearm. The other readings misplace the region at the ankle, treat flexor as a shape, or read radialis as a head count.","topic":"Muscle Naming","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"While you curl a dumbbell, muscles around your scapula contract to hold the shoulder blade steady so the biceps brachii can act cleanly on the forearm. What role are those scapular muscles playing?","options":["Agonist, since they produce the movement that is being performed","Antagonist, since they lengthen as the biceps brachii shortens","Fixator, since they stabilize the bone that the prime mover pulls from","Synergist, since they add force directly to elbow flexion"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"A fixator is the synergist whose job is to stabilize the origin bone, which is exactly what the scapular muscles are doing here. The biceps is the agonist, the triceps is the antagonist, and a plain synergist would be adding force to elbow flexion the way brachialis does.","topic":"Muscle Roles","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"You rise onto your toes. The ball of the foot is the fulcrum, body weight bears down through the ankle, and the calf muscles pull up on the heel. Which lever class is at work, and what does the body gain from it?","options":["First class, with the fulcrum between the effort and the load, gaining stability","Second class, with the load between the fulcrum and the effort, gaining mechanical advantage","Third class, with the effort between the fulcrum and the load, gaining speed and range","Second class, with the effort between the fulcrum and the load, gaining speed and range"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"With the load in the middle the effort acts farther from the fulcrum than the load does, which is mechanical advantage, the wheelbarrow arrangement. A first class lever puts the fulcrum in the middle as in nodding the head, a third class lever puts the effort in the middle as in elbow flexion, and the last option pairs the second class name with the third class arrangement.","topic":"Lever Systems","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Three of the four muscles of mastication elevate the mandible. Which one instead protrudes the jaw and helps open the mouth?","options":["Masseter","Temporalis","Medial pterygoid","Lateral pterygoid"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The lateral pterygoid is the odd member of the group, protruding the mandible and assisting depression. Masseter and medial pterygoid elevate, and temporalis elevates and retracts, so all three close the jaw rather than open it.","topic":"Muscles of Mastication","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which cranial nerve supplies the muscles of expression, including orbicularis oculi, orbicularis oris, buccinator, and zygomaticus major?","options":["The trigeminal nerve","The facial nerve","The vagus nerve","The oculomotor nerve"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Every muscle of facial expression is supplied by the facial nerve. The trigeminal nerve supplies the muscles of mastication instead, the vagus serves the pharynx, larynx, and viscera, and the oculomotor nerve moves the eyeball.","topic":"Muscles of Facial Expression","dok":1,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"The trapezius and the rhomboids both retract the scapula, yet they oppose each other in a second action. What is that action?","options":["The trapezius rotates the glenoid cavity upward and the rhomboids rotate it downward","The trapezius protracts the scapula while the rhomboids retract it toward the spine","The trapezius depresses the clavicle while the rhomboids elevate the clavicle","The trapezius abducts the arm at the shoulder and the rhomboids adduct it"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"Both muscles pull the scapula toward the midline, but the trapezius turns the glenoid cavity up while the rhomboids and levator scapulae turn it down, which makes them antagonists in rotation. Protraction belongs to serratus anterior and pectoralis minor, clavicular depression belongs to subclavius, and neither muscle moves the arm directly at the shoulder joint.","topic":"Back Muscles","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient is asked to push against a wall and the medial border of her right scapula lifts away from her back. Which muscle and which nerve are involved?","options":["Rhomboid major, supplied by the dorsal scapular nerve","Latissimus dorsi, supplied by the thoracodorsal nerve","Serratus anterior, supplied by the long thoracic nerve","Pectoralis minor, supplied by the medial pectoral nerve"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Serratus anterior holds the scapula flat against the chest wall, so losing its long thoracic nerve produces the winged scapula seen on pushing. The rhomboids retract, latissimus dorsi acts on the humerus, and pectoralis minor depresses and protracts, and none of their injuries lifts the medial border off the back this way.","topic":"Thoracic Wall Muscles","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which muscle is the deepest of the flat muscles of the anterior abdominal wall, and how do its fibers run?","options":["Rectus abdominis, with fibers running vertically","External abdominal oblique, with fibers running downward and forward","Internal abdominal oblique, with fibers running upward and forward","Transversus abdominis, with fibers running horizontally across the abdomen"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"Transversus abdominis is the innermost layer and its name tells you the fibers run across the belly. Rectus abdominis is the vertical strap muscle in front rather than a flat layer, and the two obliques lie superficial to transversus with their fibers running at angles.","topic":"Abdominal Wall","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Three of the four rotator cuff muscles insert on the greater tubercle of the humerus. Which one is the exception, and what does that odd insertion go along with?","options":["Supraspinatus, inserting on the lesser tubercle, and it starts abduction","Infraspinatus, inserting on the lesser tubercle, and it laterally rotates the arm","Subscapularis, inserting on the lesser tubercle, and it medially rotates the arm","Teres minor, inserting on the lesser tubercle, and it adducts the arm"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Subscapularis is the only cuff muscle on the anterior surface of the scapula, the only one reaching the lesser tubercle, and the only one that rotates the arm medially, so the odd insertion and the odd action belong to the same muscle. Supraspinatus starts abduction and infraspinatus and teres minor rotate laterally, all from the greater tubercle.","topic":"Rotator Cuff","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient who golfs has pain and tenderness right over the medial epicondyle of the humerus. Which group of muscles takes its common origin there?","options":["The wrist and finger extensors of the posterior forearm, plus supinator","The wrist and finger flexors of the anterior forearm, plus pronator teres","The rotator cuff muscles of the posterior shoulder, plus teres major","The intrinsic thenar muscles at the base of the thumb, plus the lumbricals"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Almost every superficial flexor of the forearm, plus pronator teres, arises from the medial epicondyle, which is why irritation there is called golfer's elbow. The extensors share the lateral epicondyle instead, giving tennis elbow, while the cuff muscles arise from the scapula and the thenar muscles from the carpal bones and flexor retinaculum.","topic":"Forearm Muscles","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which set names the three hamstring muscles of the posterior thigh, and what action do they share at the knee?","options":["Biceps femoris, semitendinosus, and semimembranosus, and they flex the knee","Rectus femoris, vastus lateralis, and vastus medialis, and they flex the knee","Gluteus maximus, gluteus medius, and tensor fasciae latae, and they extend the knee","Adductor magnus, adductor longus, and gracilis, and they extend the knee"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The hamstrings are biceps femoris, semitendinosus, and semimembranosus, and they flex the knee while extending the hip. The quadriceps group extends the knee rather than flexing it, the gluteal group acts at the hip, and the adductor group pulls the thigh toward the midline.","topic":"Thigh Muscles","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Where do you place a stethoscope to listen at the apex of the heart, and which chamber forms that apex?","options":["Second intercostal space at the right sternal border, and the right atrium forms it","Fifth intercostal space at the left midclavicular line, and the left ventricle forms it","Fourth intercostal space at the left sternal border, and the right ventricle forms it","Second intercostal space at the left sternal border, and the left atrium forms it"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The apex is the tip of the left ventricle pointing down and to the left, and it lies at the fifth intercostal space in the midclavicular line where the mitral valve is best heard. The second intercostal spaces are the aortic and pulmonary listening points, and the lower left sternal border is the tricuspid point.","topic":"Heart Position","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Fluid is collecting around a patient's heart and pressing on it so the chambers cannot fill properly. In which space is that fluid sitting, and what bounds it on either side?","options":["The pericardial cavity, between the parietal and visceral layers of the serous pericardium","The pericardial cavity, between the fibrous pericardium and the parietal serous layer","The mediastinum, between the fibrous pericardium and the pleura","The space between the myocardium and the endocardium"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The pericardial cavity is the thin fluid-filled gap between the two serous layers, and the visceral layer is the same membrane as the epicardium of the heart wall. The parietal serous layer is fused to the fibrous pericardium so there is no cavity between them, and the myocardium and endocardium are solid layers of the wall itself.","topic":"Pericardium","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"During a dissection you open the right atrium and find a shallow oval depression in the wall between the two atria. What is it, and what was it before birth?","options":["The crista terminalis, the remnant of the ductus venosus","The opening of the coronary sinus, the remnant of the ductus arteriosus","The conus arteriosus, the remnant of the umbilical vein","The fossa ovalis, the remnant of the foramen ovale"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The fossa ovalis marks where the foramen ovale let blood cross from the right atrium to the left before the lungs were in use. The crista terminalis is a muscular ridge, the coronary sinus opening is a true vein draining the heart wall, and the conus arteriosus is the smooth outflow tract of the right ventricle.","topic":"Heart Chambers","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A valve on your specimen has cusps tied down by fibrous cords that run to cone-shaped muscular projections of the ventricle wall. Which valves can it be, and what is that arrangement for?","options":["The pulmonary or aortic valve, and it holds the cusps open while the ventricle contracts","The tricuspid or mitral valve, and it keeps the cusps from being pushed back into the atrium","The aortic valve by itself, and the cords brace its cusps against the pressure in the aorta","Any of the four valves, since each is anchored by cords running to the ventricle wall"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Chordae tendineae and papillary muscles belong only to the atrioventricular valves, where they hold the cusps shut against the force of a contracting ventricle. The semilunar valves have pocket-shaped cusps that fill from above and close themselves, so they need no cords at all.","topic":"Heart Valves","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"One drop of blood leaves the right ventricle. Which sequence does it follow before it finally reaches the aorta?","options":["Pulmonary valve, pulmonary veins, lungs, pulmonary arteries, left atrium, aortic valve, left ventricle, mitral valve","Tricuspid valve, pulmonary trunk, lungs, pulmonary veins, right atrium, mitral valve, left ventricle, aortic valve","Pulmonary valve, pulmonary trunk, lungs, pulmonary veins, left atrium, mitral valve, left ventricle, aortic valve","Aortic valve, aorta, lungs, pulmonary trunk, left atrium, tricuspid valve, left ventricle, mitral valve"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Blood leaves the right ventricle through the pulmonary valve, reaches the lungs by the pulmonary trunk and arteries, returns by the four pulmonary veins to the left atrium, and crosses the mitral and then the aortic valve. The wrong options swap the pulmonary arteries and veins, place a valve after the chamber it guards, or send the blood through the tricuspid valve on the way out of the heart.","topic":"Pathway of Blood","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient's right coronary artery is occluded near its origin. Besides the muscle of the inferior wall, which structures are most at risk in a person with the usual coronary pattern?","options":["The sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes, so the rhythm can fail too","The anterior two thirds of the interventricular septum and the apex","The lateral wall of the left ventricle and the left atrium behind it","The pulmonary veins and the posterior wall of the left atrium"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The right coronary artery feeds the SA node in most people and the AV node in nearly all of them, which is why an inferior infarct so often brings bradycardia or heart block along with it. The anterior septum and apex belong to the anterior interventricular branch, the lateral left ventricle and left atrium belong to the circumflex, and the pulmonary veins are not supplied by a coronary artery at all.","topic":"Coronary Circulation","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"An older patient has P waves marching along at 75 per minute and QRS complexes appearing independently at 35 per minute, with no relationship between them. Where has the conduction system failed, and what is producing the slow ventricular beat?","options":["The sinoatrial node has failed, and the atria are being paced by the atrioventricular node","The Purkinje fibers have failed, so the atria have taken over the ventricular rhythm","The internodal pathways have failed, so the ventricles are being paced directly by the sinoatrial node","The atrioventricular node and bundle have stopped conducting, so the ventricles set their own pace"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The AV bundle is the only electrical bridge through the insulating fibrous skeleton, so a block there leaves the atria on the SA node at 75 and the ventricles escaping on their own much slower rhythm. The SA node clearly still works because the P waves are regular and fast, and the atria can never pace the ventricles once that bridge is cut.","topic":"Conduction System","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which statement about the three tunics of a blood vessel wall is correct?","options":["The tunica intima is smooth muscle and elastic fibers, and it is the thickest layer in an artery","The tunica media is endothelium, and it is the only layer a capillary has","The tunica intima is endothelium on a basement membrane, and it is the only layer a capillary has","The tunica externa is endothelium, and it is continuous with the endocardium of the heart"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The intima is the endothelial lining, it is continuous with the endocardium, and it is the whole of a capillary wall. Smooth muscle and elastic fibers belong to the media, which is the thickest layer in an artery, and the externa is collagen that anchors the vessel to surrounding tissue.","topic":"Vessel Wall","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Two vessels lie side by side on a slide. One has a round open lumen with a thick middle layer, and the other has a wider, collapsed-looking lumen with a thin middle layer and a comparatively thick outer layer. How do you tell them apart, and why?","options":["The round thick-walled vessel is a vein, since veins carry the higher pressure of the two","The round thick-walled vessel is an artery, since a heavy tunica media resists high pressure","Both vessels are arteries, because a vein lacks the tunica externa entirely","The collapsed vessel is a capillary, since capillaries have the thinnest walls"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The artery keeps its round shape because its thick smooth muscle and elastic media resists the pressure it carries, while the low pressure vein collapses and shows a thick externa around a wide lumen. Veins carry the lower pressure, veins have all three tunics too, and a capillary is a single endothelial layer far too small to be confused with either of these.","topic":"Arteries and Veins","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which capillary type would you expect in a kidney glomerulus and in the lining of the small intestine, where fluid and small solutes have to cross quickly?","options":["Fenestrated capillaries","Continuous capillaries","Sinusoids","Metarterioles"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"Fenestrated capillaries carry small pores through the endothelium, which suits rapid filtration and absorption in the kidney, the intestine, and the endocrine glands. Continuous capillaries are the tight kind found in muscle, skin, and brain, sinusoids are the leaky kind in liver, spleen, and marrow, and a metarteriole is the tapered end of an arteriole rather than a capillary type.","topic":"Capillaries","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"What makes the hepatic portal circulation a portal system?","options":["An artery connects directly to a vein and skips the capillary bed","Two arteries join so that blood has an alternate route if one is blocked","Blood flows through the liver twice before it enters the inferior vena cava","Blood passes through two capillary beds in series before it returns to the heart"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"A portal system links two capillary beds with a vein between them, here the capillaries of the gut wall and then the sinusoids of the liver, so the liver can process what the gut absorbs. An arteriovenous anastomosis is the direct artery to vein connection, and an anastomosis proper is the joining of two vessels for collateral flow.","topic":"Circulatory Routes","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A clot forms in a deep vein of the calf, breaks loose, and lodges in an artery of the lung. Which route did it travel?","options":["Femoral artery, abdominal aorta, left atrium, left ventricle, pulmonary veins","Femoral vein, inferior vena cava, right atrium, right ventricle, pulmonary trunk","Great saphenous vein, superior vena cava, left atrium, aorta, bronchial arteries","Femoral vein, hepatic portal vein, liver sinusoids, right ventricle, pulmonary veins"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The clot simply follows normal venous return, up the deep veins of the limb to the inferior vena cava, into the right side of the heart, and out the pulmonary trunk to wedge in a lung artery. Arteries carry blood away from the heart so they cannot deliver a leg clot to the lungs, the leg drains into the inferior rather than the superior vena cava, and the hepatic portal vein drains the digestive organs, not the limb.","topic":"Venous Disorders","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A premature infant has a continuous machinery murmur at the left upper sternal border because a fetal shunt has failed to close. Which shunt is still open, what did it connect, and what should it have become?","options":["The foramen ovale, connecting the two atria, which should have become the fossa ovalis","The ductus venosus, connecting the umbilical vein to the inferior vena cava, which should have become the ligamentum venosum","The ductus arteriosus, connecting the pulmonary trunk to the aortic arch, which should have become the ligamentum arteriosum","The umbilical vein, carrying blood from the placenta, which should have become the ligamentum teres"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"A patent ductus arteriosus leaves an open channel between the aorta and the pulmonary trunk, and because aortic pressure stays higher through the whole cycle the flow and the murmur are continuous. The foramen ovale sits between the atria, the ductus venosus lies in the liver, and the umbilical vein is a cord vessel, so none of the three sits between those two great arteries.","topic":"Fetal Circulation","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A tube of blood is spun down in a centrifuge. Which description of the layers and the plasma proteins is correct?","options":["Plasma is about 55 percent of the volume, the buffy coat is leukocytes and platelets, and albumin is the most abundant plasma protein","Plasma is about 45 percent of the volume, the buffy coat is erythrocytes, and fibrinogen is the most abundant plasma protein","Plasma is about 55 percent of the volume, the buffy coat is erythrocytes, and the globulins are the most abundant plasma protein","Plasma is about 92 percent of the volume, the buffy coat is platelets alone, and albumin is the clotting protein"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"Plasma is roughly 55 percent of blood volume, the formed elements roughly 45 percent, and the thin pale buffy coat between them is white cells and platelets. Erythrocytes pack at the bottom and set the hematocrit, water rather than plasma makes up about 92 percent of plasma itself, and fibrinogen is the clotting protein while albumin is the most abundant.","topic":"Blood Composition","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"On a blood smear you find the largest white cell in the field, with plenty of pale cytoplasm and a kidney-shaped nucleus. Which cell is it, and what does it become once it leaves the blood?","options":["A neutrophil, which becomes a plasma cell in the tissues","A lymphocyte, which becomes a megakaryocyte in the marrow","An eosinophil, which becomes a mast cell in the tissues","A monocyte, which becomes a macrophage in the tissues"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"Largest leukocyte plus a kidney-shaped nucleus is the monocyte, and once it migrates into tissue it matures into a macrophage that engulfs debris and pathogens. A neutrophil has a multi-lobed nucleus, a lymphocyte is small with a round nucleus filling the cell, and an eosinophil has a two-lobed nucleus with coarse red-orange granules.","topic":"Leukocytes","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Every formed element traces back to one stem cell in the red bone marrow. Which statement about the two lines that branch from it is correct?","options":["The lymphoid line gives rise to the erythrocytes and platelets, while the myeloid line gives rise to the lymphocytes","The myeloid line makes erythrocytes, platelets, granulocytes, and monocytes, and the lymphoid line makes lymphocytes","Both lines branch from the megakaryocyte, which serves as the hematopoietic stem cell of the marrow","The myeloid line forms just the granulocytes, and the lymphoid line forms the rest of the formed elements"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The hemocytoblast splits into a myeloid line that produces red cells, platelets from megakaryocytes, granulocytes, and monocytes, and a lymphoid line that produces the lymphocytes. The megakaryocyte is a mature myeloid cell rather than the stem cell, and monocytes belong to the myeloid line even though they are agranulocytes.","topic":"Hematopoiesis","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient bruises easily and bleeds for a long time from small cuts, but her red cell count and white cell count are both normal. Which formed element is most likely at fault, and what is the disorder called?","options":["Erythrocytes, and the disorder is polycythemia","Leukocytes, and the disorder is leukopenia","Platelets, and the disorder is thrombocytopenia","Erythrocytes, and the disorder is sickle cell disease"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Easy bruising and prolonged bleeding with normal red and white counts points straight at too few platelets, which is thrombocytopenia. Polycythemia is an excess of red cells that thickens the blood, leukopenia is a low white count that weakens defense, and sickle cell disease distorts red cells and jams capillaries.","topic":"Blood Disorders","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Follow the arterial supply from the aortic arch to the wrist on the left side. Where does each name change?","options":["Subclavian becomes brachial at the lateral border of the first rib, brachial becomes axillary at the lower border of teres major, and the axillary divides into radial and ulnar at the wrist","Brachiocephalic becomes axillary at the lateral border of the first rib, axillary becomes subclavian at the elbow, and the subclavian divides into radial and ulnar in the palm","Subclavian becomes axillary at the clavicle, axillary becomes radial at the lower border of teres major, and the radial divides into brachial and ulnar in the forearm","Subclavian becomes axillary at the lateral border of the first rib, axillary becomes brachial at the lower border of teres major, and the brachial divides into radial and ulnar below the elbow"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"Each name change is tied to a landmark, the first rib and then the lower border of teres major, with the split into radial and ulnar happening in the cubital fossa. On the left there is no brachiocephalic trunk at all, and no arrangement puts the axillary distal to the brachial or the radial proximal to it.","topic":"Upper Limb Arteries","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A mid-shaft fracture of the humerus leaves a patient unable to extend the wrist and fingers, with numbness over the back of the thumb side of the hand. Which nerve was injured, and where does it lie against the bone?","options":["The radial nerve, spiraling in the radial groove of the humerus","The ulnar nerve, passing behind the medial epicondyle","The median nerve, passing through the carpal tunnel","The axillary nerve, wrapping the surgical neck of the humerus"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The radial nerve is pressed against the shaft in the radial groove, so a mid-shaft fracture produces wrist drop and sensory loss over the dorsum of the thumb side. The ulnar nerve at the medial epicondyle gives clawing and medial hand numbness, the median nerve at the wrist wastes the thenar muscles, and the axillary nerve is threatened at the surgical neck rather than mid-shaft.","topic":"Upper Limb Nerves","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Name the unpaired visceral branches of the abdominal aorta in order from superior to inferior.","options":["Renal artery, gonadal artery, common iliac artery","Superior mesenteric artery, celiac trunk, inferior mesenteric artery","Celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery, inferior mesenteric artery","Celiac trunk, renal arteries, inferior mesenteric artery"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The three unpaired visceral branches come off in the order celiac trunk, superior mesenteric, then inferior mesenteric before the aorta splits into the common iliacs. The renal and gonadal arteries are paired branches, and one option reverses the celiac trunk and the superior mesenteric artery.","topic":"Abdominal Aorta","dok":1,"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A student labeling a diagram of the airway needs to mark the last structure of the conducting zone, the point just before gas exchange becomes possible. Which structure is that?","options":["The segmental (tertiary) bronchus","The terminal bronchiole","The respiratory bronchiole","The alveolar duct"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The conducting zone runs from the nose to the terminal bronchioles, so the terminal bronchiole is the last conducting airway. The respiratory bronchiole and the alveolar duct are already in the respiratory zone, and the segmental bronchus is several branchings too high.","topic":"Conducting and Respiratory Zones","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Eight of the nine laryngeal cartilages are hyaline. Which one is elastic instead?","options":["The thyroid cartilage","The cricoid cartilage","The epiglottis","The arytenoid cartilages"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The epiglottis has to bend over the laryngeal inlet and spring back with every swallow, and only elastic cartilage does that. The thyroid, cricoid, and arytenoid cartilages are hyaline and hold their shape.","topic":"Larynx","dok":1,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Paramedics cannot pass a tube through a swollen upper airway, so they feel for the soft gap between two cartilages at the front of the neck and cut through the membrane that fills it. Which cartilage forms the lower edge of that gap?","options":["Cricoid cartilage","Thyroid cartilage","Epiglottis","Corniculate cartilage"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The cricothyroid membrane fills the gap between the thyroid cartilage above and the cricoid cartilage below, and the cricoid is the only cartilage in the airway that forms a complete ring. The thyroid cartilage is the shield above the gap, the epiglottis is a flap inside the larynx, and the corniculate cartilages are tiny caps on the arytenoids.","topic":"Larynx","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Crying makes the nose run because tears drain from the eye into the nasal cavity. Into which passage does the nasolacrimal duct empty?","options":["The superior meatus","The middle meatus","The nasal vestibule","The inferior meatus"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The nasolacrimal duct opens into the inferior meatus, beneath the inferior concha. Most paranasal sinuses drain into the middle meatus and the sphenoid sinus drains above the superior concha, while the vestibule is only the hair-lined space just inside the nostril.","topic":"Nasal Cavity","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which part of the airway carries air only, is lined with pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium, and holds the openings of the auditory tubes?","options":["The oropharynx","The laryngopharynx","The nasopharynx","The larynx"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The nasopharynx sits behind the nasal cavity above the soft palate, carries air alone, and holds both the auditory tube openings and the pharyngeal tonsil. The oropharynx and laryngopharynx carry food as well as air and are lined with stratified squamous epithelium for friction, and the larynx carries air alone but has no auditory tubes opening into it.","topic":"Pharynx","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"After a tumor is removed from the larynx, a patient can still close her airway when she swallows, but she has lost her voice entirely. Which structures did the surgery most likely have to sacrifice?","options":["The vestibular folds","The epiglottis","The vocal folds","The corniculate cartilages"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The vocal folds, the true cords, are the pearly white avascular folds that vibrate to make sound, so losing them takes the voice. The vestibular folds close the airway during swallowing but play no part in sound, which is why airway protection survived.","topic":"Larynx","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient swallows a large piece of meat that lodges in the esophagus, and he complains that it has become hard to breathe. Which feature of the tracheal wall explains how the esophagus can press on the airway?","options":["The tracheal rings are complete circles of elastic cartilage that flatten when something presses on them","The tracheal cartilages are C shaped and open at the back, where only the trachealis muscle lies between the trachea and the esophagus","The trachea is lined by a thick layer of stratified squamous epithelium that buckles when it is squeezed","The carina sits directly behind the esophagus and is pushed forward into the airway when food passes"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The tracheal cartilages are incomplete rings whose open ends face backward, and the soft trachealis muscle bridges that gap right against the esophagus. The rings are hyaline, not elastic, and the lining is pseudostratified ciliated columnar rather than stratified squamous.","topic":"Trachea","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which three layers make up the respiratory membrane, the barrier oxygen crosses to reach the blood?","options":["Alveolar epithelium, smooth muscle, and capillary endothelium","Alveolar epithelium, a plate of hyaline cartilage, and capillary endothelium","Type II alveolar cells, alveolar macrophages, and pulmonary capillaries","Alveolar epithelium, fused basement membranes, and capillary endothelium"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The respiratory membrane is the alveolar epithelium, the fused basement membranes of the alveolus and the capillary, and the capillary endothelium, which together are well under a micron thick. Smooth muscle and cartilage belong to the conducting airways, and type II cells and macrophages sit near the barrier rather than forming it.","topic":"Respiratory Membrane","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Fluid has collected in the pleural cavity of a patient who is sitting upright, and a needle is placed to drain it. Which part of the pleural cavity is the target?","options":["The costodiaphragmatic recess","The costomediastinal recess","The hilum of the lung","The apex of the pleural cavity"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The costodiaphragmatic recess is the deepest part of the pleural cavity, between the ribs and the diaphragm, and the lung does not fill it during quiet breathing, so fluid pools there in an upright patient. The costomediastinal recess sits anteriorly and is shallower, and the hilum and the apex are the wrong end of the cavity for fluid that sinks.","topic":"Pleurae","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A surgeon working at the aortic hiatus of the diaphragm must protect the other two structures that pass through that same opening. What are they?","options":["The esophagus and the vagus nerves","The inferior vena cava and the phrenic nerve","The thoracic duct and the azygos vein","The esophagus and the inferior vena cava"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The aortic hiatus at T12 carries the aorta, the thoracic duct, and the azygos vein. The esophagus and the vagus nerves pass through the esophageal hiatus at T10, and the inferior vena cava passes through the caval opening at T8.","topic":"Diaphragm","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A long-time smoker has emphysema. On a section of his lung the air spaces are far larger and far fewer than normal. Which structural change explains the loss of gas exchange?","options":["The bronchioles have filled with thick mucus, so air can no longer reach the alveoli beyond them","Alveolar walls have been destroyed, so many small alveoli have merged into a few large sacs with far less surface","Air has leaked into the pleural cavity, so the lung has pulled away from the chest wall and partly collapsed","The respiratory membrane has thickened with a plate of hyaline cartilage laid down between air and blood"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Emphysema destroys alveolar walls, and merging many small sacs into a few large ones trades an enormous surface for a small one. Mucus plugging describes chronic bronchitis and asthma, air in the pleural cavity is a pneumothorax, and cartilage never appears in the alveolar wall.","topic":"Alveoli","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which sequence correctly traces lymph from the tissue spaces back to the bloodstream?","options":["Collecting vessels, lymphatic capillaries, nodes, ducts, trunks, subclavian veins","Lymphatic capillaries, trunks, nodes, collecting vessels, ducts, subclavian veins","Lymphatic capillaries, nodes, ducts, trunks, collecting vessels, subclavian veins","Lymphatic capillaries, collecting vessels, nodes, trunks, ducts, subclavian veins"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"Lymph is picked up by blind-ended lymphatic capillaries, moves into valved collecting vessels, is filtered through nodes, gathers into regional trunks, and empties through the right lymphatic duct or the thoracic duct where the subclavian and internal jugular veins meet. The vessels get larger the whole way, so any order that puts trunks or ducts before collecting vessels is backward.","topic":"Lymph Pathway","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"After a fatty meal the lymph draining the small intestine turns milky. Which structure took up the fat, and where does that lymph first collect?","options":["The lacteal in each villus, and the lymph collects in the cisterna chyli","The blood capillary in each villus, and the fat travels directly in the hepatic portal vein","The mesenteric lymph nodes, and the fat drains through the right lymphatic duct","The Peyer patches of the ileum, and the lymph collects in the cisterna chyli"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"Each villus holds a lacteal, a specialized lymphatic capillary that absorbs dietary fat, and that lymph gathers in the cisterna chyli where the thoracic duct begins. Blood capillaries in the villus take sugars and amino acids rather than fat, Peyer patches are immune tissue rather than absorptive tissue, and the whole abdomen drains through the thoracic duct rather than the right lymphatic duct.","topic":"Lymphatic Vessels","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Six months after a mastectomy with axillary lymph node dissection, a patient's arm on that side is swollen and heavy, and her ring no longer fits. Which explanation fits the anatomy?","options":["The subclavian vein was tied off during the surgery, so venous blood cannot leave the arm","The spleen no longer filters the lymph coming back from that arm, so fluid collects in the tissues","Nearly all lymph from the upper limb passes through the axillary nodes, so removing them leaves the arm with no return route","The axillary artery was narrowed by scar tissue, so less blood now reaches the arm"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The upper limb drains through the axillary nodes, so taking those nodes and the channels around them leaves protein-rich fluid with no reliable way back to the bloodstream, which is secondary lymphedema. The spleen filters blood rather than lymph, and a narrowed artery would leave the arm pale and thin rather than heavy and swollen.","topic":"Lymph Nodes","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which statement correctly pairs a region of the spleen with what it does?","options":["White pulp removes worn-out erythrocytes from the blood, and red pulp is the lymphocyte-rich immune tissue","Red pulp filters the blood and removes worn-out erythrocytes, and white pulp is the lymphocyte-rich immune tissue","Red pulp filters lymph arriving through afferent vessels, and white pulp holds it until it drains onward","White pulp is the fibrous capsule around the spleen, and red pulp is where T cells finish maturing"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The spleen filters blood rather than lymph, so red pulp clears worn-out red blood cells while white pulp holds the lymphocytes that do the immune work. Afferent vessels and lymph filtering belong to lymph nodes, the capsule of the spleen is dense connective tissue, and T cells mature in the thymus.","topic":"Spleen","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A study group is sorting the lymphatic organs into primary and secondary. Which grouping is correct?","options":["Primary: red bone marrow and thymus. Secondary: lymph nodes, spleen, tonsils, and MALT","Primary: thymus and spleen. Secondary: red bone marrow, lymph nodes, tonsils, and MALT","Primary: lymph nodes and tonsils. Secondary: red bone marrow, thymus, spleen, and MALT","Primary: red bone marrow and spleen. Secondary: thymus, lymph nodes, tonsils, and MALT"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"Only two organs are primary, because only two are places where lymphocytes are made and matured: the red bone marrow, where all lymphocytes form and B cells mature, and the thymus, where T cells mature. Everything else on the list is a secondary organ, a place where a finished lymphocyte meets what it was built to fight.","topic":"Lymphatic Organs","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Lymphatic capillaries take up protein, cell debris, and whole bacteria that blood capillaries leave behind. Which structural feature makes that possible?","options":["A continuous basement membrane sealed by tight junctions between the endothelial cells","A layer of smooth muscle in the capillary wall that pumps tissue fluid inward","Fenestrations shared with the blood capillaries running alongside them","Endothelial cells that overlap like roof shingles and swing inward as one-way flaps"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The overlapping endothelial cells of a lymphatic capillary work as inward-opening flap valves, so large particles get in and rising internal pressure keeps them from getting back out. Tight junctions and a continuous basement membrane would make the vessel less permeable, and lymphatic capillaries have no muscle of their own.","topic":"Lymphatic Vessels","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Name the four layers of the alimentary canal wall in order from the lumen outward.","options":["Serosa, muscularis, submucosa, mucosa","Mucosa, submucosa, muscularis, serosa","Mucosa, muscularis, submucosa, serosa","Submucosa, mucosa, muscularis, adventitia"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The wall runs mucosa, submucosa, muscularis, then serosa, moving from the food outward. The mucosa is the epithelium plus lamina propria and muscularis mucosae, the submucosa carries vessels and the submucosal plexus, and the serosa is replaced by a fibrous adventitia on the esophagus.","topic":"Gut Wall","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A peptic ulcer erodes deeper and deeper through the wall of the stomach until the contents of the lumen spill into the peritoneal cavity. Which layer had to be breached last?","options":["The serosa","The mucosa","The submucosa","The muscularis mucosae"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"An ulcer begins in the mucosa and works outward, so the serosa, the outermost layer and the visceral peritoneum of the stomach, is the last barrier before perforation. The muscularis mucosae is a thin part of the mucosa itself, and the submucosa is crossed well before the wall gives way.","topic":"Gut Wall","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient describes burning behind the breastbone after large meals and when she lies flat. Which structure is failing, and what is moving where?","options":["The pyloric sphincter, letting duodenal contents back into the stomach","The lower esophageal sphincter, letting stomach contents back up into the esophagus","The upper esophageal sphincter, letting air swallowed with the meal collect in the esophagus","The ileocecal sphincter, letting colon contents back into the ileum"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Gastroesophageal reflux disease is a weak lower esophageal sphincter, so acidic stomach contents rise into an esophagus that has no protection against them, which is felt behind the breastbone. The pyloric and ileocecal sphincters sit far lower in the canal and would not produce chest symptoms.","topic":"Canal Disorders","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which region of the stomach is the rounded portion that bulges above and to the left of the opening from the esophagus?","options":["Cardia","Body","Pyloric antrum","Fundus"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The fundus is the dome that rises above and to the left of the cardia. The cardia surrounds the esophageal opening itself, the body is the large central portion, and the pyloric antrum is at the far end near the duodenum.","topic":"Stomach","dok":1,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"The lining of the small intestine is folded three times over to expand its absorptive surface. Rank the three modifications from the largest to the smallest.","options":["Villi, circular folds, microvilli","Microvilli, villi, circular folds","Circular folds, villi, microvilli","Circular folds, microvilli, villi"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The circular folds are ridges of the whole mucosa and submucosa, the villi are finger-like projections of the mucosa standing on them, and the microvilli are the brush border on the surface of a single absorptive cell. Each level is smaller than the one before it, and the lacteal sits inside the villus.","topic":"Small Intestine","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient has pain in the lower right abdomen, worst at a point one third of the way from the anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus. Which structure is inflamed, and what is it attached to?","options":["The vermiform appendix, hanging from the cecum where the three teniae coli come together","The sigmoid colon, held to the posterior wall by the sigmoid mesocolon","The gallbladder, attached to the visceral surface of the liver","The ileum, held to the posterior wall by the mesentery proper"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"That surface point overlies the base of the appendix, a narrow tube of lymphatic tissue hanging from the cecum where the three teniae coli come together. The sigmoid colon sits in the lower left, and gallbladder pain is felt in the upper right.","topic":"Large Intestine","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which pairing of a large intestine feature with its description is correct?","options":["Haustra are the three thickened bands of longitudinal muscle running the length of the colon","Teniae coli are three bands of longitudinal muscle whose tone gathers the wall into pouches called haustra","Omental appendices are the circular folds of mucosa that ring the inside of the colon","Rugae are the small fat-filled tags of peritoneum hanging from the outside of the colon"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The teniae coli are the muscle bands and the haustra are the pouches their tone gathers, so the two are cause and effect rather than two names for one thing. Circular folds belong to the small intestine, and rugae are folds of the stomach lining rather than a colon feature at all.","topic":"Large Intestine","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"An imaging study shows part of the stomach sitting above the diaphragm. Through which opening has it pushed, and at what vertebral level does that opening sit?","options":["The caval opening, at T8","The aortic hiatus, at T12","The inguinal canal, at the level of L1","The esophageal hiatus, at T10"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"A hiatal hernia is stomach pushed up through the esophageal hiatus, which sits at T10 and normally admits only the esophagus and the vagus nerves. The caval opening at T8 and the aortic hiatus at T12 are occupied by their own structures, and the inguinal canal is in the groin.","topic":"Canal Disorders","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which peritoneal fold runs from the concave upper border of the stomach and the first part of the duodenum up to the liver, carrying vessels to the liver between its layers?","options":["The lesser omentum","The greater omentum","The falciform ligament","The transverse mesocolon"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The lesser omentum bridges the lesser curvature and the duodenum to the liver. The greater omentum is the fatty apron hanging from the greater curvature, the falciform ligament ties the liver to the anterior wall, and the transverse mesocolon anchors the transverse colon behind.","topic":"Peritoneum","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A surgeon plans an approach that reaches an organ from behind without opening the peritoneal cavity at all. Which set of organs could be reached that way?","options":["The stomach, the jejunum, and the transverse colon","The liver, the spleen, and the ileum","The kidneys, the pancreas, and most of the duodenum","The appendix, the sigmoid colon, and the gallbladder"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Retroperitoneal organs lie behind the peritoneum rather than inside its cavity, and the kidneys, the pancreas, and the duodenum past its first part are the classic examples along with the ascending and descending colon. The stomach, jejunum, ileum, liver, spleen, appendix, and sigmoid colon all hang inside the peritoneal cavity on folds.","topic":"Peritoneum","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which route traces bile correctly from the cell that makes it all the way to the duodenum?","options":["Hepatocyte, bile canaliculi, cystic duct, common hepatic duct, hepatopancreatic ampulla, minor duodenal papilla","Hepatocyte, bile canaliculi, right and left hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct, common bile duct, hepatopancreatic ampulla, major duodenal papilla","Hepatocyte, hepatic sinusoid, central vein, common hepatic duct, common bile duct, hepatopancreatic ampulla, major duodenal papilla","Hepatocyte, bile canaliculi, common hepatic duct, main pancreatic duct, accessory pancreatic duct, minor duodenal papilla"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Bile leaves the hepatocyte into the canaliculi between cells, gathers into the right and left hepatic ducts and then the common hepatic duct, joins the cystic duct to form the common bile duct, and enters the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla past the sphincter of Oddi. The sinusoids and the central vein carry blood rather than bile, and the pancreatic ducts and the minor papilla belong to the pancreas.","topic":"Biliary Tree","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A slide of the liver is examined at the corner of a lobule, where three structures always travel together. What is in the portal triad?","options":["The central vein, a hepatic sinusoid, and a bile canaliculus between the cells","A hepatic vein, a hepatic artery branch, and a bile duct","A branch of the hepatic portal vein, the central vein, and a lymphatic vessel","A branch of the hepatic portal vein, a hepatic artery branch, and a bile duct"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The portal triad at each corner of a lobule holds a portal venule, a hepatic arteriole, and a bile ductule, with blood coming in on two of them and bile going out on the third. The central vein sits in the middle of the lobule rather than at a corner, and hepatic veins carry blood away from the liver entirely.","topic":"Liver","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A gallstone lodges at the hepatopancreatic ampulla, and the patient develops pancreatitis. Why can a stone in the biliary tree inflame the pancreas?","options":["The common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct meet just before that point, so one stone dams both","The gallbladder stores pancreatic juice along with bile, so the stone traps both secretions","The accessory pancreatic duct empties into the gallbladder and backs up when a stone forms","The pancreas lies inside the peritoneal cavity and swells directly against the impacted stone"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The two ducts share one short final passage at the ampulla before the major duodenal papilla, so a stone wedged there dams both systems at once. The gallbladder handles only bile, the accessory duct opens separately at the minor papilla, and the pancreas is retroperitoneal.","topic":"Accessory Organ Disorders","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A child has painful swelling in front of and below the ear, spreading over the masseter muscle. Which gland is inflamed, and where does its duct open?","options":["The submandibular gland, with a duct opening near the lingual frenulum","The sublingual gland, with ducts opening onto the floor of the mouth","The parotid gland, with a duct opening in the cheek opposite the second upper molar","The lingual glands, with ducts opening at the base of the tongue"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The parotid gland lies between the skin and the masseter, anterior and inferior to the ear, which is exactly where mumps swells, and its duct crosses the cheek to open by the second upper molar. The submandibular and sublingual glands sit in the floor of the mouth and would swell under the jaw instead.","topic":"Salivary Glands","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"On a pancreas slide a student sees densely stained clusters of cells filling most of the field and a few pale islands scattered among them. What is she looking at?","options":["Pancreatic islets making digestive enzymes, with acini scattered among them making hormones","Hepatocyte plates with a central vein sitting in the middle of each lobule","Acini, the exocrine tissue that makes digestive enzymes, with pale endocrine islets among them","Thyroid follicles filled with colloid, with parafollicular cells scattered between them"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"About 99 percent of the pancreas is acinar tissue that sends enzymes down ducts to the duodenum, and the pale islets scattered through it are the endocrine one percent that release hormones straight into the blood. The first option reverses the two roles, and the other two describe the liver and the thyroid.","topic":"Pancreas","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"The liver is unusual because two different vessels bring blood to it. Which statement about that pair is correct?","options":["Both vessels arise from the celiac trunk and both deliver freshly oxygenated blood","The hepatic portal vein carries blood away from the liver and empties it into the inferior vena cava","The hepatic artery brings nutrient-rich blood from the gut and the portal vein brings the oxygen","The hepatic artery brings oxygenated blood and the hepatic portal vein brings nutrient-rich blood from the gut"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"Two supplies arrive and one drainage leaves: the hepatic artery brings oxygen and the hepatic portal vein brings nutrients absorbed from the gut, and both pour into the sinusoids before the blood leaves by the central veins and hepatic veins to the inferior vena cava. The third option reverses the two, and the portal vein arrives at the liver rather than draining it.","topic":"Liver","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Why does the posterior lobe of the pituitary connect to the hypothalamus by a bundle of nerve fibers while the anterior lobe connects by a set of blood vessels?","options":["The posterior lobe is nervous tissue, an extension of the hypothalamus, and the anterior lobe is glandular epithelium","The posterior lobe is glandular epithelium, and the anterior lobe is nervous tissue grown down from the hypothalamus","Both lobes are nervous tissue, but only the anterior lobe sits inside the bony sella turcica","The anterior lobe grows down from the infundibulum and the posterior lobe grows up from the roof of the mouth"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The tissue tells you the link. Nervous tissue is wired, so the neurohypophysis receives axons running down the infundibulum, and glandular epithelium is fed by blood, so the adenohypophysis receives the hypophyseal portal system. Both lobes sit together in the sella turcica.","topic":"Pituitary Gland","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A surgeon removing the thyroid gland takes great care with four tiny glands nearby. Where exactly are they, and what makes them so easy to lose?","options":["Inside the isthmus in front of the trachea, where they blend into the thyroid tissue around them","On the back surface of the thyroid lobes, small enough to be mistaken for thyroid tissue or fat","Inside the wall of the trachea just below the cricoid cartilage, hidden by the thyroid in front","In the superior mediastinum beside the thymus, well away from the surgeon's field"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The parathyroid glands are usually four small round glands embedded on the back of the thyroid lobes, so a thyroidectomy passes directly over them and their size makes them easy to remove by accident. The isthmus is the anterior bridge between the two thyroid lobes, and the thymus is a separate organ in the mediastinum.","topic":"Parathyroid Glands","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"An adrenal gland is cut in half and examined from the capsule inward. In what order do the regions appear?","options":["Capsule, zona fasciculata, zona glomerulosa, zona reticularis, medulla","Capsule, medulla, zona glomerulosa, zona fasciculata, zona reticularis","Capsule, zona reticularis, zona fasciculata, zona glomerulosa, medulla","Capsule, zona glomerulosa, zona fasciculata, zona reticularis, medulla"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"Working inward you meet the capsule, the thin zona glomerulosa, the wide zona fasciculata with its straight columns of cells, the branching zona reticularis, and finally the medulla at the core. The medulla is never on the outside, since it is the inner core formed from different tissue than the cortex around it.","topic":"Adrenal Glands","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"The adrenal medulla behaves very differently from the cortex wrapped around it. Which description fits the medulla?","options":["It is modified nervous tissue of the sympathetic system, and its chromaffin cells pour epinephrine into the blood","It is glandular epithelium arranged in three layered zones, and each zone releases its own steroid hormone","It is lymphatic tissue that filters the blood arriving from the kidney just below it","It is exocrine tissue that sends its secretion down a duct into the top of the kidney"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The medulla is essentially a modified sympathetic ganglion, so its chromaffin cells act as the long-distance arm of sympathetic outflow by dumping epinephrine and norepinephrine straight into the blood. The three zones of steroid-producing glandular epithelium describe the cortex, and the adrenal gland has no duct at all.","topic":"Adrenal Glands","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient reports that she keeps bumping into things at the edges of her vision, and imaging finds an enlarged pituitary gland. Which anatomical relationship explains her symptom?","options":["The pituitary rests on the cribriform plate and presses upward on the olfactory tracts above it","The pituitary hangs beside the internal carotid artery and presses outward on the oculomotor nerve","The pituitary sits just below the thyroid gland and presses backward on the trachea and esophagus","The pituitary sits in the bony sella turcica, so a growing gland can only expand up into the optic chiasm"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The sella turcica is a closed bony pocket, so a growing pituitary is pushed upward into the optic chiasm and the first sign is often a loss of peripheral vision. The cribriform plate carries the olfactory nerves far anteriorly, and the pituitary sits in the skull rather than the neck.","topic":"Pituitary Gland","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"On a slide of an endocrine gland from the front of the neck a student sees hollow spheres filled with a pink protein-rich material, with a second, larger cell type scattered in the spaces between the spheres. Name the three things she is seeing.","options":["Acini, pancreatic juice, and islet cells","Thyroid follicles, colloid, and parafollicular (C) cells","Hepatic lobules, bile canaliculi, and Kupffer cells","Alveolar sacs, surfactant, and alveolar macrophages"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The thyroid is built of follicles, hollow spheres of follicular cells storing colloid in the middle, with parafollicular cells sitting between the follicles rather than in their walls. The other three options describe the pancreas, the liver, and the lung.","topic":"Thyroid Gland","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Some organs whose main job is something else still contain pockets of hormone-secreting cells. Which pairing is correct?","options":["The femur, with hormone-producing cells scattered through its compact bone","The gallbladder, with hormone-producing cells in the neck beside the cystic duct","The heart, with hormone-producing cells in the wall of the atria","The urinary bladder, with hormone-producing cells in the trigone"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The atrial walls contain cells that release a hormone acting on fluid balance, which is why the heart appears on the list of organs with endocrine tissue along with the kidneys, the stomach and small intestine, the skin, adipose tissue, and the placenta. The femur, gallbladder, and bladder carry no endocrine cells of their own.","topic":"Organs with Endocrine Tissue","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A kidney is cut in the coronal plane, into a front half and a back half. A band of tissue as pale as the outer region dips inward between two neighboring cone-shaped masses. What is that band called?","options":["Renal papilla","Renal pyramid","Renal column","Renal pelvis"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Renal columns are extensions of the cortex that dip down between the renal pyramids. The pyramid is the cone itself and the papilla is only its tip, while the renal pelvis is the funnel at the hilum, not a band of tissue between pyramids.","topic":"Kidney Internal Anatomy","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A drug narrows one vessel of the kidney. Downstream the peritubular capillaries receive less blood, yet the glomerulus stays full and even engorged. Which vessel was narrowed?","options":["Afferent arteriole","Efferent arteriole","Arcuate artery","Cortical radiate artery"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The efferent arteriole is the vessel between the two capillary beds, so narrowing it backs blood up in the glomerulus while starving the peritubular capillaries. Narrowing the afferent arteriole, the arcuate artery, or a cortical radiate artery would reduce flow into the glomerulus itself rather than trapping blood there.","topic":"Renal Blood Supply","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which layer of the filtration membrane is the leaky capillary wall whose pores let plasma through but hold the blood cells back?","options":["Fenestrated endothelium","Basement membrane","Slit membranes between the podocyte processes","Capsular space"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The fenestrated endothelium of the glomerular capillary is the first layer, and its pores are small enough to keep blood cells in the vessel. The basement membrane holds back large plasma proteins and the slit membranes hold back medium-sized proteins, and the capsular space is not a layer at all but the space the filtrate collects in.","topic":"Filtration Membrane","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A stone forms quietly in the renal pelvis and causes no pain for months. The patient then develops sudden, severe flank pain that travels toward the groin. What has the stone most likely done?","options":["Blocked a minor calyx at a renal papilla","Lodged in the urethra just past the internal urethral sphincter","Settled onto the trigone of the bladder","Passed out of the renal pelvis and into the ureter"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"A stone sitting in the roomy renal pelvis causes little trouble until it moves into the ureter, a tube only a few millimeters wide, where it scrapes the wall and blocks the flow of urine. A calyx or the trigone would not produce pain radiating to the groin, and the urethra is well past the point where flank pain would arise.","topic":"Ureters and the Path of Urine","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A slide shows two nephrons side by side. One has its renal corpuscle down near the medulla and a long loop that dives deep into it. Which vessels follow that long loop?","options":["Peritubular capillaries","Vasa recta","Glomerular capillaries","Arcuate veins"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"A juxtamedullary nephron has a long loop served by the vasa recta, and those long loops are what let the kidney concentrate urine. Peritubular capillaries wrap the short loops of cortical nephrons, the glomerular capillaries sit up in the renal corpuscle, and the arcuate veins run along the border between cortex and medulla.","topic":"Nephron Types","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"During cystoscopy a clinician sees a smooth triangular patch on the floor of the bladder that stays flat while the rest of the lining is thrown into folds. What marks the three corners of that patch?","options":["The two ureteric openings and the internal urethral orifice","The two ureteric openings and the external urethral orifice","The internal and external urethral orifices and the apex of the bladder","The two ureteric openings and the ureteropelvic junction"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The trigone is bounded by the two ureteric openings and the internal urethral orifice, and it stays smooth while rugae elsewhere flatten only as the bladder fills. The external urethral orifice is at the far end of the urethra, and the ureteropelvic junction is up at the kidney.","topic":"Urinary Bladder","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"The juxtaglomerular apparatus forms where the distal convoluted tubule touches the arterioles of its own renal corpuscle. Which cells sit in the wall of the tubule there and monitor the fluid passing through it?","options":["Juxtaglomerular cells","Podocytes","Macula densa cells","Interstitial cells"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The macula densa is the patch of tubule cells that senses the sodium and chloride content of the filtrate. Juxtaglomerular cells sit in the wall of the afferent arteriole and sense pressure, podocytes wrap the glomerular capillaries, and interstitial cells belong to the testis.","topic":"Juxtaglomerular Apparatus","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which part of a sperm cell is packed with mitochondria that power the movement of the tail?","options":["The acrosome","The head","The principal piece","The middle piece"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The middle piece holds the mitochondria that supply the energy for swimming. The head carries the nucleus under its acrosome cap, and the principal piece is simply the longest stretch of the tail.","topic":"Sperm Cell Structure","dok":1,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which accessory gland contributes the largest share of semen, an alkaline fluid rich in fructose that nourishes the sperm?","options":["Seminal vesicles","Prostate","Bulbourethral glands","Epididymis"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The seminal vesicles add the alkaline, fructose-rich fluid that makes up the largest part of semen. The prostate adds a milky, slightly acidic fluid, the bulbourethral glands add a small amount of alkaline mucus, and the epididymis is a duct rather than an accessory gland.","topic":"Male Accessory Glands","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A newborn is found to have one testis still inside the abdomen rather than in the scrotum. Which anatomical fact best explains why this is corrected rather than left alone?","options":["The abdomen offers no tunica albuginea to cover and protect the testis","Sperm production needs a temperature below the body core, and the scrotum provides it","The ductus deferens cannot reach the ejaculatory duct from inside the abdomen","Interstitial cells only make testosterone once the testis is below the inguinal canal"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The testes hang outside the body because sperm formation needs a cooler temperature, and an undescended testis held at core temperature can lose its sperm-forming cells. The tunica albuginea travels with the testis, the ductus deferens is long enough either way, and interstitial cells make testosterone regardless of position.","topic":"Male Reproductive Disorders","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"The innermost layer of the uterine wall is built in two sublayers. Which statement describes them correctly?","options":["The perimetrium sheds each cycle and the myometrium rebuilds it","The myometrium sheds each cycle and the perimetrium rebuilds it","The stratum functionalis sheds each cycle and the stratum basalis rebuilds it","The stratum basalis sheds each cycle and the stratum functionalis rebuilds it"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The endometrium is the inner lining, and its stratum functionalis is shed in menstruation while the permanent stratum basalis rebuilds it. The perimetrium is the outer serosa and the myometrium is the thick smooth muscle, and neither one sheds.","topic":"Uterine Wall","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A woman with severe one-sided pelvic pain is found to have an embryo implanted outside the uterus. Given where fertilization normally occurs, which site is the most likely?","options":["The cortex of the ovary near a mature follicle","The ampulla of the uterine tube","The canal of the cervix below the uterus","The posterior fornix of the vagina"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Fertilization normally happens in the ampulla, so an embryo held up there is most likely to implant in the wall of the uterine tube. The ovarian cortex holds follicles rather than a stretch of the route the embryo travels, and the cervical canal and the vaginal fornix both lie downstream of the uterus.","topic":"Uterine Tubes","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A tumor in the breast tugs the overlying skin inward, producing a visible dimple. Which structure is being pulled?","options":["A lactiferous duct","The pigmented skin of the areola","A suspensory ligament of the breast","A lobule of the mammary gland"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The suspensory ligaments, the Cooper's ligaments, run from the deep fascia to the skin, so a tumor that shortens one pulls the skin in and dimples it. Lactiferous ducts and lobules sit inside the breast without a direct skin attachment, and the areola is pigmented skin around the nipple rather than a supporting structure.","topic":"Mammary Gland","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A nurse examines a newborn's umbilical cord and counts its vessels. What should she find?","options":["One umbilical artery and one umbilical vein","Two umbilical veins and one umbilical artery","Three umbilical veins and no artery","Two umbilical arteries and one umbilical vein"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The umbilical cord carries two umbilical arteries and one umbilical vein between the fetus and the placenta. The other counts reverse or invent vessels that do not exist in a normal cord.","topic":"Placenta and Fetal Membranes","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Trace a signal through a single neuron from where it is received to where it is passed on. Which order is correct?","options":["Dendrites, cell body, axon hillock, axon, axon terminals","Axon terminals, axon, axon hillock, cell body, dendrites","Cell body, dendrites, axon hillock, axon terminals, axon","Dendrites, axon, cell body, axon hillock, axon terminals"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"Dendrites receive the input, the cell body integrates it, the axon hillock is where the axon begins, and the axon terminals hand the signal to the next cell. The other orders either run the neuron backward or place the axon before the cell body.","topic":"Neuron Structure","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which glial cell lines the ventricles and the central canal and helps produce and circulate cerebrospinal fluid?","options":["Astrocyte","Ependymal cell","Microglial cell","Satellite cell"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Ependymal cells line the fluid-filled cavities of the CNS and wrap the capillaries of the choroid plexus. Astrocytes support neurons and help form the blood-brain barrier, microglia are the phagocytes of the CNS, and satellite cells surround cell bodies in peripheral ganglia.","topic":"Neuroglia","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A neuron drawn on the board has a single fused process and carries touch information from the skin toward the spinal cord. How is it classified structurally and functionally?","options":["Multipolar and motor","Bipolar and sensory","Unipolar and sensory","Multipolar and interneuron"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Most sensory neurons are unipolar, with one fused process, and they carry input toward the CNS. Multipolar neurons are the motor neurons and interneurons, and bipolar neurons are the special cases found in the retina, inner ear, and olfactory area.","topic":"Neuron Classification","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"In a cross-section of a peripheral nerve, which connective tissue layer wraps one whole fascicle?","options":["Endoneurium","Epineurium","Neurolemma","Perineurium"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The three wrappings nest from the inside out, and the perineurium is the middle layer around each fascicle. The endoneurium surrounds one axon, the epineurium surrounds the whole nerve, and the neurolemma is the outer layer of a Schwann cell rather than a connective tissue wrapping.","topic":"Nerve Structure","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which three structures make up the blood-brain barrier?","options":["The dura mater, the arachnoid mater, and the pia mater of the brain","The choroid plexus, the ependymal cells, and the arachnoid granulations","The microglia, the oligodendrocytes, and the Schwann cells","Capillary tight junctions, the basement membrane, and astrocyte end-feet"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The barrier is built from brain capillary endothelium sealed by tight junctions, the basement membrane around it, and the end-feet of astrocytes. The meninges are membranes around the whole brain, the choroid plexus makes cerebrospinal fluid, and the glia listed in the third choice do not form the barrier.","topic":"Blood-Brain Barrier","dok":1,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient severs a nerve in the forearm and over several months regains some feeling in the hand. A second patient severs a tract in the spinal cord and regains nothing. Which anatomical difference best explains the outcome?","options":["Schwann cells leave a neurolemma that guides a regrowing axon, oligodendrocytes leave none","Peripheral axons are unmyelinated, so they have less sheath to rebuild","Spinal tracts carry only sensory axons, and sensory axons cannot regrow","Peripheral neurons can divide and replace themselves, while central neurons cannot"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"One Schwann cell myelinates one segment of one PNS axon and leaves behind a neurolemma that acts as a guide for regrowth, while an oligodendrocyte serves up to fifteen CNS axons and leaves no neurolemma. Peripheral axons are myelinated, spinal tracts carry both sensory and motor axons, and no mature neuron divides.","topic":"Myelination and Regeneration","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient can feel a light brush on the fingertip but cannot feel a tuning fork buzzing against the same patch of skin. Which receptor, sitting in the deep dermis and hypodermis, is most likely affected?","options":["Tactile (Meissner) corpuscle","Tactile (Merkel) disc","Lamellar (Pacinian) corpuscle","Muscle spindle of the underlying muscle"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Lamellar corpuscles lie deep in the dermis and the hypodermis and answer to deep pressure and vibration, which is exactly what a tuning fork delivers. Tactile corpuscles and tactile discs handle the light touch that is still intact, and muscle spindles sit inside skeletal muscle and sense stretch.","topic":"Sensory Receptors","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A cord cross-section shows a small extra projection of gray matter on each side, sitting between the anterior and posterior horns. What is that projection, and where in the cord would you find it?","options":["The anterior horn, which is present at every level of the cord","The lateral horn, which is present only in the thoracic and lumbar segments","The posterior horn, which is present at every level of the cord","The posterior root ganglion, which sits just outside the cord at every level"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The lateral horn is the small extra projection between the anterior and posterior horns, and it appears only in the thoracic and lumbar segments, where it holds autonomic motor nuclei. The anterior and posterior horns run the length of the cord and hold somatic motor nuclei and sensory interneurons, and the posterior root ganglion lies outside the cord altogether.","topic":"Spinal Cord Gray Matter","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"How many pairs of spinal nerves arise from the cord, and which branch of each nerve supplies the deep muscles and the skin of the back?","options":["31 pairs, and the posterior ramus","31 pairs, and the anterior ramus","33 pairs, and the meningeal branch","12 pairs, and the rami communicantes"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"There are 31 pairs, eight cervical, twelve thoracic, five lumbar, five sacral, and one coccygeal, and the posterior ramus serves the back. The anterior rami serve the limbs and the front and sides of the trunk, the meningeal branch turns back to supply the vertebrae and meninges, and the rami communicantes carry autonomic fibers.","topic":"Spinal Nerves and Rami","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Shingles produces a painful rash confined to one narrow band of skin on one side of the trunk. Where has the reactivated virus been living all those years?","options":["In the anterior horn of the spinal cord","In the anterior root of a spinal nerve","In a sympathetic trunk ganglion","In a posterior root ganglion"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The posterior root ganglion holds the cell bodies of all the sensory neurons for one segment, so a virus hiding there produces a rash in that one strip of skin. The anterior horn and anterior root are motor, and the sympathetic trunk ganglion supplies vessels and glands rather than a single sensory band.","topic":"Posterior Root Ganglion","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A needle is placed in the midline between the L4 and L5 spines. After the skin, the subcutaneous fat, and the supraspinous and interspinous ligaments, which structures does it cross next, in order, before cerebrospinal fluid appears?","options":["Dura mater, arachnoid mater, ligamentum flavum, subarachnoid space","Ligamentum flavum, epidural space, dura mater, arachnoid mater","Epidural space, ligamentum flavum, pia mater, subarachnoid space","Ligamentum flavum, dura mater, pia mater, epidural space"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"After the interspinous ligament the needle meets the tough ligamentum flavum, then the fat-filled epidural space, then the dura and the arachnoid, and CSF appears in the subarachnoid space. The pia clings to the cord itself and is never crossed, and the epidural space lies outside the dura, not inside it.","topic":"Lumbar Puncture","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Imaging after a head injury shows blood collecting between the dura mater and the arachnoid mater. Which bleed is this, and which space is it filling?","options":["A subdural hematoma, filling the subdural space","An epidural hematoma, filling the epidural space","A subarachnoid hemorrhage, mixing into the cerebrospinal fluid","A hemorrhage into the lumbar cistern"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The subdural space lies between the dura and the arachnoid, so blood there is a subdural hematoma. An epidural hematoma sits outside the dura against the skull, a subarachnoid hemorrhage mixes with the CSF beneath the arachnoid, and the lumbar cistern is far below the head.","topic":"Meningeal Spaces","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A tumor in the midbrain compresses the cerebral aqueduct. Which parts of the ventricular system would be expected to enlarge behind the blockage?","options":["The fourth ventricle only","The fourth ventricle and the central canal","The lateral ventricles and the third ventricle","The subarachnoid space over the cerebral hemispheres"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"CSF flows from the lateral ventricles to the third ventricle and then down the cerebral aqueduct, so a block in the aqueduct backs fluid up into the third and lateral ventricles. The fourth ventricle, the central canal, and the subarachnoid space all lie downstream of the blockage.","topic":"Ventricles and CSF","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which groove separates the frontal lobe from the parietal lobe, with the primary motor cortex on the gyrus in front of it and the primary somatosensory cortex on the gyrus behind it?","options":["Lateral sulcus","Central sulcus","Parieto-occipital sulcus","Longitudinal fissure"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The central sulcus divides the frontal lobe from the parietal lobe and separates the precentral gyrus from the postcentral gyrus. The lateral sulcus marks off the temporal lobe, the parieto-occipital sulcus marks off the occipital lobe, and the longitudinal fissure divides the two hemispheres.","topic":"Cerebral Surface","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient understands everything said to her and follows written instructions without trouble, but her own speech is slow, halting, and effortful. Which cortical area is most likely damaged, and in which lobe does it sit?","options":["Wernicke's area, in the temporal lobe","The primary auditory cortex, in the temporal lobe","The prefrontal cortex, in the frontal lobe","Broca's area, in the frontal lobe"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"Broca's area in the inferior frontal gyrus drives the motor production of speech, so losing it leaves comprehension intact while speech becomes laborious. Damage to Wernicke's area would wreck comprehension, damage to the primary auditory cortex would affect hearing, and the prefrontal cortex handles judgment and personality.","topic":"Functional Cortical Areas","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which group of structures is the deep gray matter buried within the cerebral white matter that regulates movement, including the background arm swing of walking?","options":["The thalamus and the hypothalamus","The hippocampus, amygdala, and cingulate gyrus","The caudate nucleus, putamen, and globus pallidus","The superior and inferior colliculi"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The basal ganglia are the caudate nucleus, putamen, and globus pallidus, with the substantia nigra and subthalamic nucleus grouped in by function. The thalamus and hypothalamus form the diencephalon, the second choice lists limbic structures, and the colliculi sit on the roof of the midbrain.","topic":"Basal Ganglia","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A very small stroke deep in one hemisphere leaves the opposite side of the body weak, the arm, the leg, and the face together. Which white matter structure was struck, and which class of fibers does it carry?","options":["The internal capsule, carrying projection fibers","The corpus callosum, carrying commissural fibers","The arcuate fasciculus, carrying association fibers","The anterior commissure, carrying projection fibers"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The internal capsule funnels nearly all projection fibers between the cortex and the lower brain and cord into one narrow band, so a tiny lesion there cuts a great deal of wiring at once. The corpus callosum and anterior commissure are commissural and join the hemispheres, and the arcuate fasciculus is an association bundle within one hemisphere.","topic":"Cerebral White Matter Tracts","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"On a midsagittal brain, which structure forms most of the diencephalon, relays nearly all sensory input on its way to the cortex, and is joined across the midline by the massa intermedia?","options":["The hypothalamus","The thalamus","The epithalamus","The pineal gland"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The thalamus makes up about eighty percent of the diencephalon and is the sensory relay to the cortex, with the massa intermedia bridging its two halves. The hypothalamus sits below it as the homeostatic control center, and the epithalamus is the small posterior part carrying the pineal gland.","topic":"Diencephalon","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A patient walks with a wide, unsteady gait and overshoots when reaching for a cup, yet strength and sensation test normal. Which region is most likely damaged?","options":["The precentral gyrus","The posterior columns of the spinal cord","The basal ganglia","The cerebellum"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The cerebellum coordinates movement, posture, balance, and timing, so its loss produces clumsy, poorly aimed movement with normal strength. Precentral gyrus damage would weaken the opposite side, posterior column damage would cost position sense and fine touch, and basal ganglia disease shows as tremor or rigidity rather than overshooting.","topic":"Cerebellum","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A stroke in the right cerebral hemisphere weakens the left side of the body. Which feature, and in which brainstem region, explains the crossover?","options":["The decussation of the pyramids, in the medulla","The cerebral peduncles, on the front of the midbrain","The middle cerebellar peduncles, in the pons","The optic chiasm, at the base of the diencephalon"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"Most corticospinal fibers cross at the decussation of the pyramids on the front of the medulla, so one hemisphere controls the opposite side of the body. The cerebral peduncles and middle cerebellar peduncles carry fibers without being the crossing point for voluntary motor control, and the optic chiasm is a visual crossing.","topic":"Medulla Oblongata","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"The reticular activating system is the ascending part of the reticular formation. What does it do?","options":["It sets the basic rhythm of breathing","It carries voluntary motor commands down to the cord","It keeps the cortex awake, alert, and attentive","It produces cerebrospinal fluid in the ventricles"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The ascending reticular activating system maintains consciousness and arousal and filters unimportant incoming stimuli. Breathing rhythm comes from the respiratory center of the medulla, voluntary motor commands run in the corticospinal tracts, and CSF is made by the choroid plexus.","topic":"Reticular Formation","dok":1,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A clot lodges in one posterior cerebral artery. Based on the territory that vessel supplies, which loss would you predict?","options":["Weakness of the opposite leg from loss of the medial frontal cortex","Loss of the ability to shrug one shoulder","Loss of fine touch on the opposite side of the face only","Loss of part of the visual field from damage to the occipital lobe"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The posterior cerebral artery supplies the occipital lobe and the inferior temporal lobe, so blocking it costs vision. The medial frontal and parietal cortex with the leg area belongs to the anterior cerebral artery, shoulder shrug depends on cranial nerve XI, and facial sensation is carried by cranial nerve V.","topic":"Cerebral Arterial Supply","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A tumor at the jugular foramen produces difficulty swallowing, a hoarse voice, and weakness shrugging one shoulder. Which cranial nerves pass through that opening together?","options":["CN V, CN VII, and CN VIII","CN III, CN IV, and CN VI","CN IX, CN X, and CN XI","CN VII, CN VIII, and CN XII"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The jugular foramen carries the glossopharyngeal, vagus, and accessory nerves side by side, which is why one mass there causes swallowing, voice, and shoulder signs at once. The internal acoustic meatus carries VII and VIII, the superior orbital fissure carries the eye movers, and XII leaves by the hypoglossal canal.","topic":"Cranial Nerves and Foramina","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Name the five components of a reflex arc in the order a signal passes through them.","options":["Receptor, sensory neuron, integration center, motor neuron, effector","Receptor, motor neuron, integration center, sensory neuron, effector","Effector, sensory neuron, integration center, motor neuron, receptor","Sensory neuron, receptor, motor neuron, integration center, effector"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"A reflex begins at the receptor, travels in by the sensory neuron, is processed at the integration center in the CNS, and returns by the motor neuron to the effector. The other orders reverse the incoming and outgoing neurons or start at the wrong end of the arc.","topic":"Reflex Arc","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"How does the motor pathway of the autonomic nervous system differ in structure from the somatic motor pathway?","options":["It uses one neuron running from the cord straight to the effector","It uses one neuron and always excites its effector","It uses two neurons with a synapse in the posterior root ganglion","It uses two neurons with a synapse in an autonomic ganglion"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The autonomic system always uses a preganglionic neuron, a synapse in an autonomic ganglion, and a postganglionic neuron out to the effector. The somatic system is the one that runs a single neuron straight to skeletal muscle and always excites it, and the posterior root ganglion holds sensory cell bodies, not an autonomic synapse.","topic":"Autonomic Two-Neuron Pathway","dok":2,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"A fall injures the nerve network formed by the anterior rami of C5 through T1. Which region loses movement and sensation?","options":["The neck and the diaphragm on that side","The shoulder and the entire upper limb on that side","The buttock, the back of the thigh, and the foot","The anterolateral abdominal wall and the front of the thigh"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"C5 to T1 form the brachial plexus, which serves the shoulder and the whole upper limb. The cervical plexus from C1 to C4 serves the neck and gives off the phrenic nerve, the sacral plexus serves the buttock and lower limb, and the lumbar plexus serves the abdominal wall and the front of the thigh.","topic":"Nerve Plexuses","dok":3,"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"stem":"Which formed element lacks a nucleus in its mature form and is packed with hemoglobin to carry oxygen?","options":["Neutrophil","Erythrocyte","Lymphocyte","Monocyte"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"A mature erythrocyte ejects its nucleus and most organelles to make room for hemoglobin, and its biconcave shape maximizes surface area for gas exchange. The others are nucleated leukocytes that defend the body rather than carry oxygen.","topic":"Blood","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A blood smear shows the most numerous leukocyte, with a multi-lobed nucleus, fine pale granules, and a role as the first responder to a bacterial infection. Which cell is it?","options":["Eosinophil","Basophil","Neutrophil","Lymphocyte"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The neutrophil is the most numerous leukocyte and the first responder that engulfs bacteria. Eosinophils target parasites and moderate allergy, basophils are the rarest and release histamine, and lymphocytes are agranulocytes of specific immunity.","topic":"Blood","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which plasma protein is converted into the fibrin threads that form the framework of a blood clot?","options":["Albumin","Globulin","Fibrinogen","Hemoglobin"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Fibrinogen converts to fibrin to build the clot framework. Albumin maintains osmotic pressure, globulins include antibodies and transport proteins, and hemoglobin is inside erythrocytes, not a plasma protein.","topic":"Blood","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Blood leaving the right ventricle passes through which valve on its way toward the lungs?","options":["Aortic valve","Mitral valve","Tricuspid valve","Pulmonary valve"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The right ventricle pumps through the pulmonary (semilunar) valve into the pulmonary trunk. The tricuspid valve is the entry into the right ventricle, the mitral valve serves the left atrium to left ventricle, and the aortic valve guards the aorta.","topic":"Heart","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The chordae tendineae and papillary muscles work together to accomplish what?","options":["Generate the heartbeat","Hold the atrioventricular valve cusps shut against ventricular pressure","Supply oxygen to the myocardium","Separate the two atria"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Papillary muscles anchor the chordae tendineae to the AV valve cusps so the valves hold shut when the ventricle contracts. The heartbeat is generated by the conduction system, the coronary arteries supply the myocardium, and the interatrial septum divides the atria.","topic":"Heart","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which layer of the heart wall is the thick middle layer of cardiac muscle that actually contracts?","options":["Endocardium","Myocardium","Epicardium","Parietal pericardium"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The myocardium is the thick contractile middle layer. The endocardium lines the chambers, the epicardium is the visceral serous pericardium on the surface, and the parietal pericardium is part of the surrounding sac.","topic":"Heart","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which layer forms the innermost lining of a blood vessel, in direct contact with the flowing blood?","options":["Tunica externa","Tunica media","Tunica intima","Pericardium"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The tunica intima is the innermost layer, a smooth endothelium the blood flows against. The tunica media is the muscular middle layer and the tunica externa is the outer connective-tissue coat; the pericardium is the sac around the whole heart.","topic":"Blood Vessels","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Sinusoidal capillaries are found in the liver, spleen, and bone marrow because they","options":["have the tightest junctions of any capillary","have large gaps that let even whole blood cells cross","carry blood away from the heart","contain one-way valves"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Sinusoids are wide and leaky with large gaps, so whole cells can pass, which those organs require. Tight continuous capillaries appear in the brain, and valves belong to veins, not capillaries.","topic":"Blood Vessels","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Why do limb veins contain valves while arteries do not?","options":["Veins carry oxygen-poor blood","Veins carry blood under low pressure and must prevent backflow","Arteries lie deeper in the body","Veins have thicker walls than arteries"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Venous pressure is low, so valves plus the skeletal muscle pump are needed to stop backflow and keep blood moving toward the heart. Oxygen content, depth, and wall thickness do not explain the presence of valves.","topic":"Blood Vessels","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A clot forms in a deep vein of the leg, breaks free, travels through the venae cavae and right heart, and lodges in an artery of the lung. This is a","options":["aneurysm","pulmonary embolism","varicose vein","atheroma"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"A traveling clot that lodges in a lung artery is a pulmonary embolism, and the route it follows is simply the normal venous return. An aneurysm is an arterial bulge, varicose veins involve failed superficial valves, and an atheroma is an arterial plaque.","topic":"Vessel Disorders","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Varicose veins are best explained anatomically by","options":["fatty plaque narrowing the lumen","failure of venous valves that lets blood pool","chronically high arterial pressure","a weakened, ballooning arterial wall"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Varicose veins result from failed venous valves, so blood pools and the superficial veins bulge. The other choices describe arterial problems such as atherosclerosis, hypertension, and aneurysm.","topic":"Vessel Disorders","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"After birth the fetal umbilical vein closes and becomes which structure in the adult?","options":["Ligamentum arteriosum","Ligamentum teres (round ligament of the liver)","Fossa ovalis","Ligamentum venosum"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The umbilical vein becomes the ligamentum teres, the round ligament of the liver. The ligamentum arteriosum is the remnant of the ductus arteriosus, the fossa ovalis of the foramen ovale, and the ligamentum venosum of the ductus venosus.","topic":"Fetal Circulation","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which component of the conduction system is the heart's natural pacemaker, initiating each heartbeat?","options":["AV node","AV bundle (bundle of His)","SA node","Purkinje fibers"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The SA node in the right atrial wall sets the pace and starts each beat. The AV node relays the signal to the ventricles and the AV bundle and Purkinje fibers distribute it, but none of those normally set the rhythm.","topic":"Conduction System","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"On an electrocardiogram, the QRS complex corresponds to which event in the conduction system?","options":["The signal spreading across the atria","The signal spreading across the ventricles","Electrical recovery of the ventricles","The SA node setting the pace"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The QRS complex marks the signal spreading across the ventricles, which then contract. The P wave marks atrial spread and the T wave marks ventricular recovery.","topic":"Conduction System","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"In which surface groove of the heart does the right coronary artery travel?","options":["Anterior interventricular sulcus","Coronary (atrioventricular) sulcus","Posterior interventricular sulcus","Interatrial groove"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The right coronary artery runs in the coronary (atrioventricular) sulcus between the atria and ventricles. The interventricular sulci carry the interventricular arteries and mark the boundary between the two ventricles.","topic":"Coronary Circulation","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The pulmonary veins are unusual among veins because they","options":["carry oxygen-poor blood to the lungs","carry oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium","drain the heart wall itself","branch directly from the aorta"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Pulmonary veins are unusual because they return oxygen-rich blood, delivering it from the lungs to the left atrium. Pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood to the lungs, and the coronary sinus drains the heart wall.","topic":"Great Vessels","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which structure folds down over the laryngeal opening during swallowing to keep food and drink out of the airway?","options":["Glottis","Epiglottis","Cricoid cartilage","Carina"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The epiglottis folds over the laryngeal opening during a swallow. The glottis is the opening between the vocal cords, the cricoid is the ring cartilage below the thyroid cartilage, and the carina is the ridge where the trachea forks.","topic":"Respiratory","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The respiratory zone, where gas exchange becomes possible, begins at the","options":["terminal bronchioles","respiratory bronchioles","main bronchi","trachea"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The respiratory zone begins at the respiratory bronchioles, where the first alveoli bud from the walls. The terminal bronchioles are the last part of the conducting zone, and the trachea and main bronchi only carry air.","topic":"Respiratory","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The left lung has only two lobes and a cardiac notch mainly because it","options":["is larger than the right lung","gives up room to accommodate the heart","has a horizontal fissure","lacks a hilum"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The left lung is smaller and carries a cardiac notch to make room for the heart. The right lung is larger with three lobes and a horizontal fissure, and both lungs have a hilum.","topic":"Respiratory","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"What structure forms the border of a sarcomere, marking where one sarcomere ends and the next begins?","options":["M line","Z disc","A band","H zone"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The Z disc bounds each sarcomere, which runs from one Z disc to the next. The M line sits at the center, and the A band and H zone are internal regions of the sarcomere.","topic":"Muscle Microanatomy","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A myofibril is best described as","options":["the plasma membrane of the muscle fiber","a bundle of whole muscle fibers","a rod of repeating sarcomeres running the length of the fiber","the connective tissue wrapping around a fascicle"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"A myofibril is a long rod made of sarcomeres joined end to end inside the muscle fiber. The membrane is the sarcolemma, a bundle of fibers is a fascicle, and the wrapping around a fascicle is the perimysium.","topic":"Muscle Microanatomy","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"In Duchenne muscular dystrophy the gene for dystrophin is faulty. Dystrophin normally","options":["forms the thick filaments","links the thin filaments to the sarcolemma to transfer contractile force","stores calcium ions","anchors the thick filaments at the M line"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Dystrophin links the thin filaments to the sarcolemma and transfers force out to the connective tissue, so when it fails the fiber damages itself with ordinary use. Calcium is stored by the sarcoplasmic reticulum and myomesin anchors thick filaments at the M line.","topic":"Muscle Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A pennate muscle such as the deltoid packs many short fascicles at an angle to a central tendon. This architecture gives it","options":["a greater range of motion","greater power","fewer fibers per unit of muscle","a spindle shape"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Packing many short angled fascicles means more fibers pull on the tendon, which delivers power at the cost of range of motion. Long parallel or fusiform fascicles give range instead.","topic":"Fascicle & Levers","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Flexing the elbow with the biceps, where the elbow joint is the fulcrum and the biceps pull lies between the fulcrum and the load in the hand, is an example of a","options":["first-class lever","second-class lever","third-class lever","fixed lever"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"When the effort lies between the fulcrum and the load it is a third-class lever, the most common in the body. A first-class lever has the fulcrum in the middle, and a second-class lever has the load in the middle.","topic":"Fascicle & Levers","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"In compact bone, which canals run crosswise and connect adjacent central (Haversian) canals?","options":["Canaliculi","Perforating (Volkmann) canals","Lacunae","Lamellae"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Perforating, or Volkmann, canals run crosswise to link central canals. Canaliculi connect lacunae to one another, lacunae house osteocytes, and lamellae are the concentric rings of matrix.","topic":"Bone Microanatomy","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which mature bone cell sits within a lacuna and maintains the surrounding bone matrix?","options":["Osteoblast","Osteocyte","Osteoclast","Osteogenic cell"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The osteocyte is the mature cell housed in a lacuna that maintains the matrix. The osteoblast builds matrix, the osteoclast resorbs it, and the osteogenic cell is the stem cell.","topic":"Bone Microanatomy","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Spongy bone differs from compact bone in that it","options":["is built from repeating osteons","forms an open lattice of trabeculae aligned along lines of stress","contains no marrow at all","forms the dense outer shell of the bone"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Spongy bone is a lattice of trabeculae aligned with stress, and its spaces hold red marrow. Compact bone is built from osteons and forms the dense outer shell.","topic":"Bone Microanatomy","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A blow to the pterion is dangerous because it is the thinnest part of the skull and overlies the","options":["internal carotid artery","middle meningeal artery","vertebral artery","internal jugular vein"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The middle meningeal artery runs just beneath the pterion, so a blow there can tear it and bleed into the skull. The other vessels do not run beneath that junction.","topic":"Axial Skeleton","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The cribriform plate, which transmits the olfactory nerve filaments, is part of which bone?","options":["Sphenoid bone","Frontal bone","Ethmoid bone","Temporal bone"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The cribriform plate belongs to the ethmoid bone and passes the olfactory nerve (CN I). The sphenoid carries the optic canal, and the temporal bone carries the internal acoustic meatus.","topic":"Axial Skeleton","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A herniated intervertebral disc sends pain down a limb because the soft ______ pushes through a torn anulus fibrosus and presses on a spinal nerve.","options":["nucleus pulposus","dens","lamina","costal facet"],"correctIndex":0,"rationale":"The soft nucleus pulposus herniates through the anulus fibrosus and compresses a spinal nerve at the intervertebral foramen. The dens is the peg of the axis, the lamina completes the vertebral arch, and a costal facet articulates with a rib.","topic":"Axial Skeleton","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which feature is found only on cervical vertebrae?","options":["Costal facets on the body","Transverse foramina that transmit the vertebral arteries","Kidney-shaped bodies","Long, sharply downward-angled spinous processes"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Transverse foramina occur only in cervical vertebrae and pass the vertebral arteries. Costal facets and long angled spinous processes are thoracic features, and kidney-shaped bodies are lumbar.","topic":"Axial Skeleton","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The heel bone, and the largest of the tarsal bones, is the","options":["Talus","Navicular","Calcaneus","Cuboid"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The calcaneus is the heel bone and the largest tarsal. The talus sits above it and transmits weight from the tibia, while the navicular and cuboid are smaller tarsals.","topic":"Appendicular Skeleton","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which forearm bone is lateral, on the thumb side, in anatomical position?","options":["Ulna","Radius","Humerus","Capitate"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The radius is lateral, on the thumb side, while the ulna is medial. The humerus is the arm bone and the capitate is a carpal bone.","topic":"Appendicular Skeleton","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A fracture of the femoral neck threatens the femoral head itself because","options":["the neck stores the body's red marrow","the blood supply to the head runs close to the neck","the neck bears no body weight","the patella sits directly against the neck"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The blood supply to the femoral head runs close to the narrow, angled neck, so a neck fracture can cut off the head's supply. The other statements do not explain the risk to the head.","topic":"Appendicular Skeleton","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which type of synovial joint permits triaxial movement and is found at the shoulder and hip?","options":["Hinge joint","Pivot joint","Ball-and-socket joint","Saddle joint"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The ball-and-socket joint moves around three axes and appears at the shoulder and hip. Hinge and pivot joints are uniaxial, and the saddle joint of the thumb is biaxial.","topic":"Joints","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The glenohumeral joint is the most freely movable joint in the body yet also the most unstable because","options":["its articular capsule is unusually tight","the glenoid cavity is shallow relative to the large humeral head, and most stability comes from the rotator cuff","it is a simple hinge joint","it has no supporting ligaments at all"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"A shallow glenoid and a loose capsule give the shoulder great mobility but little bony stability, so the rotator cuff muscles do most of the stabilizing. It is a ball-and-socket joint and does have ligaments, though they add little strength.","topic":"Joints","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which type of immovable fibrous joint is found between the flat bones of the skull?","options":["Symphysis","Suture","Synchondrosis","Gomphosis"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Sutures are the immovable fibrous joints that knit the skull bones together. A symphysis and a synchondrosis are cartilaginous joints, and a gomphosis anchors a tooth in its socket.","topic":"Joints","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which lymphatic organ filters blood and removes worn-out red blood cells?","options":["Thymus","Tonsil","Spleen","Lymph node"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The spleen filters blood and recycles aged red cells. The thymus matures T cells, tonsils guard the throat, and lymph nodes filter lymph rather than blood.","topic":"Lymphatic","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Place the epidermal strata of thick skin in order from deep to superficial.","options":["corneum, lucidum, granulosum, spinosum, basale","basale, spinosum, granulosum, lucidum, corneum","basale, granulosum, spinosum, lucidum, corneum","spinosum, basale, granulosum, corneum, lucidum"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"From deep to superficial the order is basale, spinosum, granulosum, lucidum, corneum, with the lucidum present only in thick skin. The other sequences scramble that order.","topic":"Integumentary","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The papillary layer of the dermis differs from the reticular layer in that it","options":["is the deep layer made of dense irregular connective tissue","is the superficial areolar connective tissue that forms the dermal papillae","contains no sensory receptors","is actually part of the epidermis"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The papillary layer is the superficial areolar connective tissue that projects up as dermal papillae and holds Meissner corpuscles for light touch. The reticular layer is the deep dense irregular layer, and both are dermis, not epidermis.","topic":"Integumentary","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A blood smear shows the largest cell in the field, with a kidney-shaped nucleus and abundant pale cytoplasm. After leaving the bloodstream this cell becomes a","options":["mast cell","macrophage","plasma cell","osteoclast"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The largest leukocyte is the monocyte, which enters tissues and matures into a macrophage. Mast cells, plasma cells, and osteoclasts arise by other routes.","topic":"Blood","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A patient's hematocrit is 62 percent, well above normal. The most direct physical consequence for the circulation is","options":["thinner, faster-flowing blood","reduced clotting ability","increased blood viscosity that makes the heart work harder","a drop in plasma osmotic pressure"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Packing more red cells into the blood raises its viscosity, so the heart must work harder to move it. It does not thin the blood or directly change clotting or osmotic pressure.","topic":"Blood","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Serum differs from plasma in that serum","options":["contains extra red cells","has no dissolved proteins at all","is identical to whole blood","lacks fibrinogen and the other clotting factors"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"Serum is what remains after blood clots, so it is plasma minus fibrinogen and the consumed clotting factors. It still contains many other proteins.","topic":"Blood","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"During isovolumetric ventricular contraction all four valves are shut. This is possible because ventricular pressure is","options":["below atrial pressure","exactly equal to aortic pressure","above atrial pressure but not yet above aortic pressure","zero"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Once pressure rises above the atria the AV valves shut, but until it exceeds arterial pressure the semilunar valves stay closed, so briefly all four are shut.","topic":"Heart","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"An incompetent (leaky) mitral valve would let blood flow backward into the","options":["right atrium during diastole","left atrium during ventricular contraction","aorta during systole","left ventricle during diastole"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The mitral valve guards the left atrium-to-ventricle opening, so if it leaks, ventricular contraction drives blood back into the left atrium.","topic":"Heart","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The first heart sound, the 'lub', is produced by closure of the","options":["semilunar valves","atrioventricular valves","aortic valve alone","pulmonary valve alone"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The AV valves snap shut as the ventricles begin to contract, making the first sound. The semilunar valves closing make the second sound.","topic":"Heart","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A vessel with a thick, smooth-muscle-dominated tunica media that constricts to redirect blood toward active organs is best classified as a","options":["elastic (conducting) artery","capillary","muscular (distributing) artery","venule"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Muscular arteries have a heavy smooth-muscle media and actively change diameter to distribute blood. Elastic arteries rely on recoil, and capillaries and venules are thin exchange or collecting vessels.","topic":"Vessel Histology","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Continuous capillaries with tight junctions predominate in the brain because that tissue requires","options":["rapid bulk filtration","a highly selective barrier","passage of whole blood cells","valve-controlled flow"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The brain needs a tight, selective barrier, so its capillaries are continuous with tight junctions. Leaky or fenestrated capillaries would let too much cross.","topic":"Vessel Histology","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Veins collapse when empty and depend on valves mainly because, compared with arteries, they have","options":["a thicker tunica media","greater elastic recoil","thin walls and carry blood under low pressure","no endothelial lining"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Low venous pressure and thin walls mean veins need valves and the muscle pump to move blood forward. Arteries stay open under their own high pressure.","topic":"Vessel Histology","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The axillary artery is the direct continuation of which vessel as it enters the armpit?","options":["brachial artery","subclavian artery","radial artery","common carotid artery"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The subclavian becomes the axillary at the first rib, then the brachial in the arm. The radial and common carotid are elsewhere.","topic":"Upper Extremity Vessels","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A deep laceration at the lateral (thumb-side) wrist would most likely sever the","options":["ulnar artery","brachial artery","radial artery","axillary artery"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The radial artery runs on the lateral wrist, where the pulse is taken, so a thumb-side wrist cut threatens it. The ulnar artery is medial and the others lie proximally.","topic":"Upper Extremity Vessels","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A hand often survives occlusion of the radial artery at the wrist because","options":["the brachial artery bypasses the wrist","the ulnar artery and palmar arches supply collateral flow","capillaries pump blood on their own","veins deliver the needed oxygen"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The radial and ulnar arteries join in the palmar arches, so the ulnar supply can perfuse the hand if the radial is blocked.","topic":"Upper Extremity Vessels","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Firm pressure on both common carotid arteries can cause fainting because these vessels","options":["drain venous blood from the brain","carry arterial blood to the brain","supply only the face and scalp","house the pacemaker"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The carotids deliver arterial blood to the brain, so compressing them starves it and can cause fainting. They are arteries, not draining veins.","topic":"Neck Vessels","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which vessel supplies the face and scalp rather than running up into the skull?","options":["internal carotid artery","external carotid artery","vertebral artery","basilar artery"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The external carotid supplies the face, scalp, and neck, while the internal carotid and vertebral arteries ascend into the skull to reach the brain.","topic":"Neck Vessels","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Predictable chest pain during exertion that eases with rest points to narrowing of the","options":["pulmonary arteries","coronary arteries","carotid arteries","renal arteries"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Exertional angina reflects coronary arteries that cannot meet the heart's increased demand. The other vessels do not supply the myocardium.","topic":"Coronary Circulation","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The coronary sinus returns venous blood from the heart wall into the","options":["left atrium","aorta","right atrium","pulmonary trunk"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The coronary sinus, on the back of the heart, empties oxygen-poor myocardial blood into the right atrium.","topic":"Coronary Circulation","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A prolonged PR interval on an ECG reflects slowed conduction at the","options":["SA node","AV node","Purkinje fibers","bundle branches"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The PR interval covers the delay between atrial and ventricular activation, which occurs at the AV node, so a long PR points there.","topic":"Conduction System","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The QRS complex of an ECG represents","options":["atrial depolarization","ventricular repolarization","ventricular depolarization","firing of the SA node"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The QRS marks ventricular depolarization, which triggers ventricular contraction. Atrial spread is the P wave and ventricular recovery is the T wave.","topic":"Conduction System","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Without pulmonary surfactant, alveoli tend to collapse because surfactant normally","options":["increases surface tension","adds cartilage support","widens the airway","lowers the surface tension inside the alveoli"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"Surfactant from type II cells lowers alveolar surface tension so the sacs resist collapse. Without it, high surface tension pulls the alveoli shut.","topic":"Respiratory","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The conducting zone is distinguished from the respiratory zone because the conducting zone","options":["is where gas exchange occurs","contains all of the alveoli","moves air but does not exchange gas","lacks cartilage entirely"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The conducting zone only channels air; gas exchange happens in the respiratory zone (respiratory bronchioles, ducts, and alveoli).","topic":"Respiratory","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A drug that blocks acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction would directly prevent","options":["oxygen delivery to the fiber","the muscle fiber from being excited to contract","calcium storage in the SR","the tendon from attaching to bone"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction triggers the muscle action potential; blocking its receptors stops the fiber from being excited and so prevents contraction.","topic":"Muscle Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A single skeletal muscle fiber is best described as","options":["a bundle of separate independent cells","a single long cell containing many nuclei","one sarcomere joined end to end","a strand of pure myosin protein"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"A skeletal muscle fiber is one long, multinucleated cell formed when many cells fuse in development; inside it lie many myofibrils, each a chain of sarcomeres.","topic":"Muscle Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The triad, one T tubule flanked by two terminal cisternae, functions to","options":["store oxygen for contraction","couple the surface impulse to calcium release","anchor thick filaments at the M line","form the thin filaments"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The triad lets the action potential in the T tubule trigger calcium release from the SR, coupling excitation to contraction.","topic":"Muscle Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Osteons line up along a long bone's lines of compressive stress. This best illustrates","options":["random, purposeless growth","wasted building material","structure matched to mechanical function","conversion of bone to cartilage"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Aligning osteons with the loads a bone bears is a clear case of structure suiting function, giving strength where it is needed.","topic":"Bone Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"If osteoclasts could not do their job, bone would become","options":["thin and brittle","abnormally dense because old matrix is never resorbed","unable to store any calcium","entirely cartilage"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Osteoclasts resorb bone; without them, matrix accumulates and bone becomes overly dense and poorly remodeled.","topic":"Bone Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Osteocytes trapped deep in solid compact bone stay alive because","options":["they grow their own blood vessels","the matrix is actually liquid","canaliculi carry nutrients from the central canal to each cell","they crawl to the surface each day"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Canaliculi connect lacunae to the central canal's vessels, ferrying nutrients and wastes to and from buried osteocytes.","topic":"Bone Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A blow to the pterion is dangerous mainly because it may tear the","options":["internal jugular vein","middle meningeal artery","vertebral artery","facial nerve"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The middle meningeal artery runs just deep to the thin pterion, so a blow there can rupture it and bleed inside the skull.","topic":"Axial Skeleton","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The dens of the axis (C2) is significant because it lets the head","options":["nod 'yes'","bend sideways","rotate 'no'","flex forward"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The atlas pivots around the dens, producing the side-to-side 'no' rotation of the head. Nodding 'yes' happens at the atlas-occiput joint.","topic":"Axial Skeleton","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A mid-shaft humeral fracture can cause wrist drop by injuring the","options":["ulnar nerve","radial nerve","axillary nerve","median nerve at the wrist"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The radial nerve spirals along the posterior humeral shaft, so a shaft fracture can damage it and produce wrist drop.","topic":"Appendicular Skeleton","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The only bony joint linking the upper limb to the axial skeleton is the","options":["glenohumeral joint","acromioclavicular joint","sternoclavicular joint","elbow joint"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The clavicle meets the sternum at the sternoclavicular joint, the sole bony connection between the upper limb and the axial skeleton.","topic":"Appendicular Skeleton","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A femoral neck fracture endangers the femoral head because","options":["the neck bears no body weight","the blood supply to the head runs along the neck","the neck holds all the red marrow","the patella presses on the neck"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Vessels to the femoral head travel along the narrow neck, so a neck fracture can cut off the head's blood supply.","topic":"Appendicular Skeleton","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which feature gives the hip joint far more stability than the shoulder?","options":["a shallower socket","a deep acetabulum that grips the femoral head","the absence of supporting ligaments","a simple hinge design"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The hip's deep acetabulum encloses much of the femoral head for bony stability, whereas the shoulder's shallow socket trades stability for mobility.","topic":"Joints","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The pubic symphysis is classified as a cartilaginous joint because the two pubic bones are united by","options":["a fluid-filled synovial cavity","fibrocartilage that permits slight movement","a fibrous suture","a solid bony fusion"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"A pad of fibrocartilage joins the pubic bones, allowing the slight movement important in childbirth, which defines a cartilaginous joint.","topic":"Joints","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A hinge joint such as the elbow permits movement in","options":["three axes","two axes","one axis, flexion and extension","no axes"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"A hinge joint is uniaxial, allowing flexion and extension only, like a door hinge.","topic":"Joints","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Lymph re-enters the bloodstream where the large lymphatic ducts empty into the","options":["aorta","veins near the junction of the subclavian and internal jugular veins","right atrium directly","hepatic portal vein"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The thoracic and right lymphatic ducts drain into the subclavian veins near where they meet the internal jugular veins, returning fluid to the blood.","topic":"Lymphatic","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"After a splenectomy, the function most reduced is","options":["maturation of T cells","production of bile","filtering of blood and removal of aged red cells","absorption of dietary fat"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The spleen filters blood and recycles old red cells, so removing it most affects that role. T-cell maturation is the thymus, and bile and fat handling are digestive.","topic":"Lymphatic","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Melanocytes shield deeper cells from UV damage by","options":["thickening the stratum corneum","transferring melanin that caps keratinocyte nuclei","secreting cooling sweat","producing keratin"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Melanocytes make melanin and pass it to keratinocytes, where it forms a shield over the nucleus against UV. Keratin and sweat serve other roles.","topic":"Integumentary","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Continuous renewal of the epidermis depends on cell division in the","options":["stratum corneum","stratum basale","stratum lucidum","stratum granulosum"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The deepest layer, the stratum basale, holds the dividing stem cells that replace the cells shed from the surface.","topic":"Integumentary","dok":2,"exam":1,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A pennate muscle produces more force than a parallel muscle of the same size because it","options":["has longer fibers","shortens over a greater distance","packs more short fibers pulling on the tendon","stores more elastic energy"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Angled pennate fibers let more of them attach to the tendon, so more fibers pull at once, favoring force over range of motion.","topic":"Fascicle & Levers","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Flexing the forearm with the biceps, where the effort lies between the fulcrum (elbow) and the load (hand), is a","options":["first-class lever","third-class lever","second-class lever","fixed lever"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"With the effort between fulcrum and load, it is a third-class lever, the most common type in the body.","topic":"Fascicle & Levers","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A complete blood count shows a large rise in lymphocytes with normal neutrophils. This pattern most suggests","options":["an acute bacterial infection","a viral infection","a parasitic worm infection","recent blood loss"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Lymphocytes climb in viral infections, whereas neutrophils lead the response to bacteria and eosinophils to parasites. Blood loss lowers red cells, not lymphocytes.","topic":"Blood","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Blood is classified as a connective tissue because it consists of","options":["a fluid with no cells","densely packed cells with no matrix","cells suspended in an extracellular matrix called plasma","flat sheets of lining cells"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Like all connective tissues, blood is cells within an extracellular matrix; here the matrix is the fluid plasma.","topic":"Blood","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which plasma protein is chiefly responsible for the osmotic pressure that holds water within the blood vessels?","options":["fibrinogen","albumin","hemoglobin","gamma globulin"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Albumin, the most abundant plasma protein, maintains the blood's osmotic pressure. Fibrinogen clots, hemoglobin sits inside red cells, and globulins mostly transport or defend.","topic":"Blood","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Leaving the right atrium, a drop of blood passes next through the","options":["mitral valve into the left ventricle","pulmonary valve into the aorta","tricuspid valve into the right ventricle","aortic valve into the body"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"From the right atrium blood crosses the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle. The mitral and aortic valves serve the left side, and the pulmonary valve comes after the right ventricle.","topic":"Heart","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"If the tricuspid valve failed to close during ventricular systole, blood would regurgitate into the","options":["left atrium","right atrium","aorta","pulmonary trunk"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The tricuspid valve guards the right atrium-to-ventricle opening, so a leak sends blood back into the right atrium when the ventricle contracts.","topic":"Heart","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The thick, contractile middle layer of the heart wall is the","options":["endocardium","epicardium","parietal pericardium","myocardium"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The myocardium is the muscular middle layer that contracts. The endocardium lines the chambers and the epicardium covers the outer surface.","topic":"Heart","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Fenestrated capillaries in the kidney and small intestine suit those organs because they allow","options":["a tight barrier against all molecules","rapid filtration and absorption of fluids and small solutes","passage of whole red blood cells","one-way valved flow"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Their pores speed the movement of fluids and small molecules, which the kidney and gut need for filtration and absorption. Whole cells cross only through leaky sinusoids.","topic":"Vessel Histology","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which vessel-wall layer is in direct contact with the flowing blood?","options":["tunica externa","tunica media","tunica intima","tunica adventitia"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The tunica intima, an endothelial lining, is the innermost layer the blood touches. The media is the muscular middle and the externa (adventitia) is the outer coat.","topic":"Vessel Histology","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Compared with its accompanying vein, an artery of the same size has","options":["a wider lumen and internal valves","a thicker tunica media and a rounder lumen that stays open under pressure","no tunica media at all","much thinner walls"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Arteries have a thick muscular media and stay round under high pressure, while veins have thinner walls, wider collapsed lumens, and valves.","topic":"Vessel Histology","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which sequence correctly orders the arteries carrying blood from the trunk to the forearm?","options":["brachial, axillary, subclavian, radial","radial, ulnar, brachial, axillary","subclavian, axillary, brachial, then radial and ulnar","axillary, subclavian, radial, ulnar"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The subclavian becomes the axillary in the armpit, then the brachial in the arm, which splits into the radial and ulnar at the elbow.","topic":"Upper Extremity Vessels","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which superficial vein crossing the front of the elbow is the usual site for drawing blood?","options":["cephalic vein","median cubital vein","basilic vein","subclavian vein"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The median cubital vein is a superficial vein in the cubital fossa, the standard venipuncture site. The cephalic and basilic run along the sides of the arm and the subclavian is deep.","topic":"Upper Extremity Vessels","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The palmar arches formed by the radial and ulnar arteries are advantageous because they","options":["slow the blood reaching the fingers","provide collateral circulation so the hand stays supplied if one artery is blocked","raise the pressure in the hand","carry lymph rather than blood"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"By joining the two forearm arteries, the arches let either one perfuse the hand, providing a backup if the other is compressed.","topic":"Upper Extremity Vessels","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"As it descends through the neck, the internal jugular vein runs alongside which artery?","options":["subclavian artery","common carotid artery","vertebral artery","facial artery"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The internal jugular vein descends beside the common carotid artery within the carotid sheath. The vertebral artery runs within the cervical transverse foramina.","topic":"Neck Vessels","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The vertebral arteries reach the brain by threading upward through the ______ of the cervical vertebrae.","options":["spinous processes","transverse foramina","vertebral bodies","intervertebral discs"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The vertebral arteries ascend through the transverse foramina, unique to cervical vertebrae, before entering the foramen magnum.","topic":"Neck Vessels","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A blockage of the anterior interventricular artery (LAD) would most affect the","options":["right atrium","anterior wall of the left ventricle","posterior right ventricle","lung tissue"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The LAD supplies the anterior interventricular septum and the front of the left ventricle, so a blockage injures that region.","topic":"Coronary Circulation","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The right and left coronary arteries arise from the","options":["pulmonary trunk","superior vena cava","base of the aorta just above the aortic valve","coronary sinus"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The coronary arteries are the first branches of the aorta, arising just above the aortic valve to feed the heart wall.","topic":"Coronary Circulation","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"If the SA node were destroyed, the heart could still beat, though more slowly, because the ______ can act as a backup pacemaker.","options":["Purkinje fibers","AV node","bundle branches","tunica media"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The AV node has its own slower intrinsic rhythm and can drive the heart if the SA node fails.","topic":"Conduction System","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The brief delay of the impulse at the AV node matters because it lets the","options":["ventricles contract before the atria","semilunar valves open early","atria finish emptying into the ventricles before the ventricles contract","SA node recover between beats"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The AV delay times the beat so the atria empty into the ventricles before ventricular contraction begins.","topic":"Conduction System","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"An inhaled peanut is more likely to lodge in the right main bronchus because it is","options":["longer and more horizontal than the left","wider, shorter, and more vertical than the left","the only bronchus lined with cilia","the only bronchus with cartilage"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The right main bronchus is wider, shorter, and more vertical, so gravity guides inhaled objects into it. Both bronchi have cilia and cartilage.","topic":"Respiratory","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The respiratory zone, where gas exchange first becomes possible, begins at the","options":["terminal bronchioles","main bronchi","respiratory bronchioles","trachea"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Respiratory bronchioles are the first structures with alveoli budding from their walls, marking the start of the respiratory zone. Terminal bronchioles still belong to the conducting zone.","topic":"Respiratory","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The C-shaped tracheal cartilages are incomplete on their posterior side so that the ______ behind the trachea can bulge forward during swallowing.","options":["aorta","esophagus","spinal cord","thyroid gland"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The gap in each ring lets the esophagus, lying just behind the trachea, expand as a food bolus passes.","topic":"Respiratory","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"As a sarcomere contracts, its two Z discs are","options":["pushed farther apart","pulled closer together","dissolved and rebuilt","left unchanged"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Myosin pulls the thin filaments inward, drawing the Z discs toward the center, which is how the sarcomere shortens.","topic":"Muscle Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which structure carries the surface action potential deep into the muscle fiber so it can reach the sarcoplasmic reticulum?","options":["the myofibril","the T tubule","the sarcomere","the tendon"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"T tubules are inward extensions of the sarcolemma that conduct the impulse into the fiber, triggering calcium release from the SR.","topic":"Muscle Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The plasma membrane of a muscle fiber is specifically called the","options":["sarcoplasm","sarcomere","endomysium","sarcolemma"],"correctIndex":3,"rationale":"The sarcolemma is the muscle cell membrane. The sarcoplasm is its cytoplasm, the sarcomere is the contractile unit, and the endomysium wraps the fiber.","topic":"Muscle Microanatomy","dok":2,"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which sequence correctly orders bone cells from stem cell to matrix-maintaining cell?","options":["osteoclast, osteoblast, osteocyte","osteogenic cell, osteoblast, osteocyte","osteocyte, osteoblast, osteoclast","osteoblast, osteogenic cell, osteoclast"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The osteogenic (stem) cell becomes a matrix-building osteoblast, which becomes a mature, maintaining osteocyte. The osteoclast is a separate resorbing cell.","topic":"Bone Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Canaliculi are essential in compact bone because they","options":["store calcium salts","form the central canal","let osteocytes exchange nutrients and wastes through the solid matrix","manufacture blood cells"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Canaliculi are the tiny channels that connect lacunae, allowing buried osteocytes to trade nutrients and wastes with the central canal's vessels.","topic":"Bone Microanatomy","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The concentric rings of hardened matrix surrounding the central canal of an osteon are the","options":["canaliculi","lamellae","lacunae","trabeculae"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Lamellae are the layered rings of matrix in an osteon. Lacunae house osteocytes, canaliculi connect them, and trabeculae form spongy bone.","topic":"Bone Microanatomy","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Costal facets, small surfaces that articulate with the ribs, are found only on which vertebrae?","options":["cervical","lumbar","thoracic","sacral"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Only thoracic vertebrae bear costal facets, because only they articulate with ribs. Cervical vertebrae have transverse foramina and lumbar have large bodies.","topic":"Axial Skeleton","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A herniated intervertebral disc sends pain down a limb when the soft ______ pushes through the anulus fibrosus onto a spinal nerve.","options":["lamina","nucleus pulposus","costal facet","dens"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The gel-like nucleus pulposus herniates through the torn anulus fibrosus and compresses a spinal nerve. The lamina, costal facet, and dens are bony features.","topic":"Axial Skeleton","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A scaphoid fracture is easily missed yet clinically serious because the bone","options":["never bears any weight","has a poor blood supply and may fail to heal","is the largest carpal bone","always dislocates the wrist"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The scaphoid's blood supply enters from one end, so a fracture can starve part of it and prevent healing; snuffbox tenderness is the clue.","topic":"Appendicular Skeleton","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"In anatomical position, the ulna lies on which side of the forearm?","options":["lateral, the thumb side","anterior","medial, the little-finger side","posterior only"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The ulna is medial, on the little-finger side, while the radius is lateral on the thumb side.","topic":"Appendicular Skeleton","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The patella is a sesamoid bone that develops within the tendon of the","options":["hamstrings","quadriceps femoris","gastrocnemius","sartorius"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The patella forms inside the quadriceps tendon, where it protects the knee and improves the muscle's leverage.","topic":"Appendicular Skeleton","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"The knee is easily injured because, as a modified hinge joint, its stability depends mostly on","options":["a deep bony socket","fused articular surfaces","ligaments and menisci rather than bone","a single continuous bone"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The rounded femoral condyles sit on a nearly flat tibia, so ligaments like the cruciates and the menisci provide most of the knee's stability, and these are what get injured.","topic":"Joints","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"A pivot joint, such as the joint between the atlas and axis, permits","options":["flexion and extension only","rotation around a single long axis","gliding movements only","triaxial movement"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"A pivot joint allows uniaxial rotation, like the turning of the head 'no'. Hinge joints flex and extend and ball-and-socket joints move triaxially.","topic":"Joints","dok":2,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Which joint type, found at both the shoulder and the hip, allows movement around three axes?","options":["hinge","pivot","ball-and-socket","saddle"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"The ball-and-socket joint is triaxial and appears at the shoulder and hip. Hinge and pivot joints are uniaxial and the saddle joint is biaxial.","topic":"Joints","dok":3,"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Removing the spleen would most reduce the body's ability to","options":["mature T lymphocytes","filter blood and recycle worn-out red cells","absorb dietary fat","produce bile"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"The spleen filters blood and recycles aged red cells, so its removal most affects that function. The thymus matures T cells and the digestive organs handle fat and bile.","topic":"Lymphatic","dok":3,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Lymph nodes function mainly to","options":["pump lymph back toward the heart","manufacture red blood cells","filter lymph and expose antigens to lymphocytes","store and concentrate bile"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"Lymph nodes filter lymph and let lymphocytes meet trapped antigens, launching immune responses. Lymph is moved by muscle action, not a nodal pump.","topic":"Lymphatic","dok":2,"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Apocrine sweat glands differ from merocrine (eccrine) glands in that apocrine glands","options":["cover the entire body surface","open into hair follicles and become active at puberty","secrete the watery sweat used for cooling","release product only by cell rupture"],"correctIndex":1,"rationale":"Apocrine glands empty into hair follicles in the axilla and groin and switch on at puberty, while merocrine glands make the widespread watery cooling sweat.","topic":"Integumentary","dok":3,"exam":1,"src":"summer"},{"stem":"Rising onto tiptoe, with the ball of the foot as the fulcrum and body weight between the fulcrum and the calf's pull at the heel, is which class of lever?","options":["first-class lever","third-class lever","second-class lever","fixed lever"],"correctIndex":2,"rationale":"When the load lies between the fulcrum and the effort, it is a second-class lever, as in standing on tiptoe.","topic":"Fascicle & Levers","dok":3,"exam":3,"src":"summer"}],"match":[{"topic":"Body Regions and Everyday Terms","columnA":["Antecubital","Popliteal","Sural","Buccal","Hallux","Olecranal","Inguinal","Axillary","Calcaneal","Mental"],"columnB":["the armpit","the sole of the foot","the back of the knee","the chin","the navel","the great toe","the front of the elbow","the heel","the wrist","the cheek","the groin","the calf","the back of the elbow"],"answers":[6,2,11,9,5,12,10,0,7,3],"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"topic":"Bones and Bone Markings","columnA":["Sella turcica","Dens","Glenoid cavity","Acetabulum","Linea aspera","Xiphoid process","Olecranon","Cribriform plate","Greater trochanter","Calcaneus"],"columnB":["The rough ridge running down the posterior shaft of the femur where muscles attach","The bump behind the ear on the temporal bone, a muscle attachment filled with air cells","The shallow socket of the scapula that receives the head of the humerus","The heel bone, the largest of the seven tarsal bones","The saddle on the body of the sphenoid whose deep seat cradles the pituitary gland","The medial bump of the ankle, formed by the distal end of the tibia","The point of the elbow, formed by the proximal end of the ulna","The perforated horizontal plate of the ethmoid that the olfactory nerve filaments pass through","The large opening framed by the ischium and the pubis","The deep cup where the ilium, ischium, and pubis meet, the socket for the head of the femur","The upward bony peg of the second cervical vertebra that the atlas pivots around","The large lateral projection just below the neck of the femur, a major muscle attachment","The small inferior tip of the sternum, cartilage that ossifies with age"],"answers":[4,10,2,9,0,12,6,7,11,3],"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"topic":"Endocrine Structures and What They Secrete","columnA":["Anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis)","Posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis)","Thyroid follicular cells","Parafollicular (C) cells of the thyroid","Chief cells of the parathyroid glands","Zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex","Zona fasciculata of the adrenal cortex","Chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla","Beta cells of the pancreatic islets","Pineal gland"],"columnB":["Calcitonin, which lowers blood calcium","Epinephrine and norepinephrine, poured into the blood as the far reach of the sympathetic nervous system","Glucagon, which raises blood glucose between meals","Oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone, two hormones made by neurons of the hypothalamus","Cortisol, a glucocorticoid released heavily during stress","Thymosins, which support the maturation of T cells","Insulin, which lowers blood glucose after a meal","Parathyroid hormone, which raises blood calcium","Melatonin, which rises and falls with the daily cycle of light and dark","Thyroid stimulating hormone, adrenocorticotropic hormone, growth hormone, and prolactin","Testosterone, the main androgen of the male gonad","Aldosterone, a mineralocorticoid that holds sodium and water in the body","Thyroid hormone (T3 and T4), which sets the body's overall metabolic rate"],"answers":[9,3,12,0,7,11,4,1,6,8],"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"topic":"Cranial Nerves and Their Functions","columnA":["CN I Olfactory","CN II Optic","CN III Oculomotor","CN V Trigeminal","CN VII Facial","CN VIII Vestibulocochlear","CN IX Glossopharyngeal","CN X Vagus","CN XI Accessory","CN XII Hypoglossal"],"columnB":["Motor: movement of the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles","Mixed: taste from the back of the tongue, swallowing, and saliva from the parotid gland","Sensory only: carries the sense of smell from the lining of the nasal cavity","Motor: eye movement by the lateral rectus muscle","Mixed: sensation from the face and motor supply to the muscles of chewing","Sensory only: hearing and balance","Motor: movement of the tongue","Sensory only: carries taste from the whole surface of the tongue","Motor: most eye movements, raising the eyelid, and constricting the pupil","Mixed: autonomic control of the thoracic and abdominal organs, plus voice and swallowing","Sensory only: carries vision from the retina","Mixed: facial expression, taste from the front of the tongue, and tear and saliva production","Motor: eye movement by the superior oblique muscle, the smallest cranial nerve"],"answers":[2,10,8,4,11,5,1,9,0,6],"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"topic":"Body Regions and Layman's Terms","columnA":["Antecubital","Popliteal","Sural","Axillary","Buccal","Carpal","Inguinal","Hallux"],"columnB":["The groin","The cheek","The sole of the foot","The front of the elbow","The back of the knee","The great toe","The wrist","The armpit","The calf","The navel"],"answers":[3,4,8,7,1,6,0,5],"exam":1,"src":"summer"},{"topic":"Bones and Bone Details","columnA":["Sella turcica","Foramen magnum","Dens","Glenoid cavity","Acetabulum","Linea aspera","Xiphoid process","Olecranon"],"columnB":["Large occipital opening that transmits the spinal cord","Rough ridge on the posterior femur for muscle attachment","Shallow scapular socket for the head of the humerus","Saddle on the sphenoid that cradles the pituitary gland","Hip socket where the ilium, ischium, and pubis meet","Inferior tip of the sternum used as a CPR landmark","Heel bone, the largest tarsal","Rounded knob of the occipital bone that meets the atlas","Point of the elbow formed by the ulna","Peg of the axis (C2) that the atlas pivots around"],"answers":[3,0,9,2,4,1,5,8],"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"topic":"Blood Cells and Their Facts","columnA":["Erythrocyte","Neutrophil","Eosinophil","Basophil","Lymphocyte","Monocyte","Platelet","Hemocytoblast"],"columnB":["The most abundant plasma protein","Most numerous leukocyte, first responder to bacteria","Cell fragment of a megakaryocyte that acts in clotting","Stem cell that every formed element arises from","Secretes surfactant in the alveoli","Largest leukocyte, leaves blood to become a macrophage","Core of specific immunity, includes the T and B cells","Rarest leukocyte, releases histamine in inflammation","Attacks parasites and moderates allergy, red-orange granules","Biconcave, no nucleus, carries oxygen on hemoglobin"],"answers":[9,1,8,7,6,5,2,3],"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"topic":"Vessels of the Upper Limb and Neck","columnA":["Axillary artery","Brachial artery","Radial artery","Ulnar artery","Subclavian artery","Internal jugular vein","Median cubital vein","Common carotid artery"],"columnB":["Neck artery that splits into internal and external branches","Main artery of the arm, palpated at the front of the elbow","Superficial vein along the thumb side of the forearm","Deep neck vein draining the brain, beside the carotid","Continuation of the subclavian, running through the armpit","Superficial elbow vein used to draw blood","Medial forearm artery, partner of the lateral wrist artery","Lateral forearm artery felt for a pulse at the wrist","First artery of the upper limb, branching under the clavicle","Vessel that returns blood from the heart wall"],"answers":[4,1,7,6,8,3,5,0],"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"topic":"Heart Anatomy and Great Vessels","columnA":["Right atrium","Left ventricle","Tricuspid valve","Mitral (bicuspid) valve","Pulmonary trunk","Aorta","Coronary sinus","Chordae tendineae"],"columnB":["Cords that anchor AV valve cusps to papillary muscles","Left AV valve, between left atrium and ventricle","Node that sets the heart rhythm","Largest artery, carries oxygen-rich blood to the body","Carries oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle to the lungs","Thick-walled pump for the systemic circuit","Large vein draining the myocardium into the right atrium","Receives oxygen-poor blood from the venae cavae","Right AV valve, between right atrium and ventricle","Serous sac that surrounds the heart"],"answers":[7,5,8,1,4,3,6,0],"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"topic":"Bone and Skeletal Landmarks","columnA":["Diaphysis","Epiphyseal plate","Osteon","Clavicle","Olecranon","Acetabulum","Dens","Sagittal suture"],"columnB":["Peg of the axis that the atlas pivots on","Basic cylindrical unit of compact bone","Tubular shaft of a long bone around the marrow cavity","Point of the elbow, on the proximal ulna","Joint line between the two parietal bones","Growth plate remnant seen as a line in adult bone","Cartilage growth plate where a long bone lengthens","Only bony strut linking the upper limb to the trunk","Hip socket formed by three fused pelvic bones","Deep socket of the shoulder for the humeral head"],"answers":[2,6,1,7,3,8,0,4],"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"topic":"Blood Cells and Blood Facts","columnA":["Erythrocyte","Neutrophil","Eosinophil","Basophil","Lymphocyte","Monocyte","Platelet","Hemoglobin"],"columnB":["Iron-containing oxygen-binding protein in red cells","Rarest leukocyte, releases histamine and heparin","Cell fragment from a megakaryocyte, acts in clotting","Straw-colored fluid matrix of blood","Most numerous leukocyte, first to a bacterial infection","Fights parasites and dampens allergy, red-orange granules","Stem cell of all formed elements","Agranulocyte of specific immunity, includes B and T cells","Largest leukocyte, becomes a tissue macrophage","Biconcave, no nucleus, carries oxygen"],"answers":[9,4,5,1,7,8,2,0],"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"topic":"Respiratory Passage and Membranes","columnA":["Larynx","Trachea","Carina","Main bronchus","Alveolus","Visceral pleura","Parietal pleura","Diaphragm"],"columnB":["Serous membrane lining the thoracic wall","Ridge where the trachea forks into the two bronchi","Dome-shaped muscle that drives quiet breathing","Air sac where gas is exchanged with the blood","Windpipe held open by C-shaped cartilage rings","Serous membrane clinging to the lung surface","Voice box holding the vocal cords","Flap that covers the airway during swallowing","Airway entering one lung, right one wider and more vertical","Smallest airway of the conducting zone"],"answers":[6,4,1,8,3,5,0,2],"exam":4,"src":"summer"},{"topic":"Joints and Skull Landmarks","columnA":["Hinge joint","Ball-and-socket joint","Pivot joint","Gomphosis","Suture","Synovial fluid","Mandible","Fontanelle"],"columnB":["Triaxial joint of the shoulder and hip","Fibrous peg-in-socket joint that holds a tooth","Cartilage growth plate of a long bone","Only freely movable bone of the skull","Lubricating fluid inside a joint capsule","Uniaxial rotating joint, as at the atlas and axis","Immovable fibrous joint between skull bones","Uniaxial joint for flexion and extension, as at the elbow","Fibrous soft spot between an infant's skull bones","Fluid-filled sac that cushions a tendon"],"answers":[7,0,5,1,6,4,3,8],"exam":2,"src":"summer"}],"fr":[{"prompt":"Dump the body cavities from the outside in. Name each cavity and its subdivisions, say what each one holds, and name the membrane that lines it. Finish by explaining the difference between the parietal and the visceral layer of a serous membrane and what sits between them.","topic":"Body Cavities and Serous Membranes","dok":2,"type":"lines","lines":10,"model":"The body has a dorsal cavity and a ventral cavity. The dorsal cavity divides into the cranial cavity, which holds the brain, and the vertebral cavity, which holds the spinal cord, and the meninges line both. The ventral cavity divides at the diaphragm into the thoracic cavity above and the abdominopelvic cavity below, and the abdominopelvic cavity divides further into the abdominal cavity above the pelvic brim and the pelvic cavity below it. Inside the thorax sit a right and a left pleural cavity, each holding a lung, with the mediastinum between them carrying the heart, great vessels, trachea, esophagus, and thymus, and the pericardial cavity around the heart inside that. Three serous membranes line these spaces: the pleura around the lungs, the pericardium around the heart, and the peritoneum around the abdominal organs. In every one of them the parietal layer lines the wall of the cavity and the visceral layer covers the organ itself, and a thin film of serous fluid in the space between the two layers lets the organ move against the wall without friction.","rubric":[{"text":"Names the dorsal cavity and both of its subdivisions, cranial and vertebral, with what each holds","pts":2},{"text":"Names the ventral cavity and both of its subdivisions, thoracic and abdominopelvic","pts":2},{"text":"Names the diaphragm as the boundary between the thoracic and abdominopelvic cavities","pts":2},{"text":"Names the two pleural cavities and the lung each one holds","pts":2},{"text":"Names the mediastinum as the central compartment between the pleural cavities and lists at least two of its contents","pts":2},{"text":"Names the pericardial cavity and the heart inside it","pts":2},{"text":"Names all three serous membranes: pleura, pericardium, and peritoneum","pts":3},{"text":"States that the parietal layer lines the wall and the visceral layer covers the organ","pts":3},{"text":"Names serous fluid in the space between the two layers and says it reduces friction","pts":2}],"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Write the route a protein takes from the gene to the outside of the cell, naming every structure it passes through in order and saying what happens at each stop. Then list every membrane-bound organelle you can, and name one structure that is not membrane bound.","topic":"The Cell and the Endomembrane System","dok":3,"type":"lines","lines":10,"model":"The route starts with DNA in the nucleus, where the gene is transcribed into messenger RNA. That messenger RNA leaves the nucleus through a nuclear pore and reaches a ribosome bound to the rough endoplasmic reticulum, where the protein is built and threaded into the interior of the endomembrane system. A transport vesicle buds off the rough endoplasmic reticulum and carries the protein to the Golgi apparatus, which modifies, sorts, and packages it. A secretory vesicle then buds off the Golgi, travels to the plasma membrane, fuses with it, and releases the protein outside the cell by exocytosis. The membrane-bound organelles include the nucleus with its envelope, the rough and smooth endoplasmic reticulum, the Golgi apparatus, lysosomes, peroxisomes, mitochondria, and transport and secretory vesicles, while ribosomes are not membrane bound at all.","rubric":[{"text":"Starts in the nucleus with DNA and names transcription into messenger RNA","pts":2},{"text":"Names the nuclear pore as the exit route for the messenger RNA","pts":2},{"text":"Names the ribosome on the rough endoplasmic reticulum as where the protein is built","pts":3},{"text":"Names a transport vesicle carrying the protein from rough endoplasmic reticulum to Golgi","pts":2},{"text":"Names the Golgi apparatus and states that it modifies, sorts, and packages","pts":3},{"text":"Names a secretory vesicle leaving the Golgi","pts":2},{"text":"Names the plasma membrane and exocytosis as the final step","pts":2},{"text":"Lists at least five membrane-bound organelles","pts":2},{"text":"Names the ribosome as a structure that is not membrane bound","pts":2}],"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Chart the four primary tissue types. In the first column name the tissue. In the second column write one defining feature you could actually see or check on a slide, then two real locations in the body where that tissue is found.","topic":"The Four Primary Tissues","dok":2,"type":"chart","lines":10,"model":"Epithelial tissue is made of tightly packed cells sitting on a basement membrane with almost no matrix between them, and it is avascular, which is a feature you can check by looking for the absence of vessels. It lines the alveoli and covers the surface of the skin. Connective tissue is the opposite picture, with relatively few cells scattered in a large extracellular matrix of ground substance and fibers, and it appears in tendons and in the reticular layer of the dermis. Muscle tissue is made of elongated cells packed with actin and myosin that generate force, and it is found in the wall of the heart and in the wall of the small intestine. Nervous tissue is made of neurons with long branching processes together with supporting neuroglia, and it is found in the brain and in the peripheral nerves.","rubric":[{"text":"Names all four primary tissue types: epithelial, connective, muscle, and nervous","pts":3},{"text":"Epithelial feature: tightly packed cells on a basement membrane, avascular, little matrix","pts":2},{"text":"Two real locations for epithelial tissue","pts":2},{"text":"Connective feature: few cells in a large extracellular matrix of ground substance and fibers","pts":3},{"text":"Two real locations for connective tissue","pts":2},{"text":"Muscle feature: elongated cells that generate force through actin and myosin","pts":2},{"text":"Two real locations for muscle tissue","pts":2},{"text":"Nervous feature: neurons with long branching processes together with supporting neuroglia","pts":2},{"text":"Two real locations for nervous tissue","pts":2}],"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Write the epidermal strata from deepest to most superficial and say one thing that happens in each. Note which layer is missing in thin skin. Then name the four principal cells of the epidermis, give each one a job, and say which layer you would find it in.","topic":"The Epidermis","dok":2,"type":"lines","lines":10,"model":"From deep to superficial the strata are the basale, the spinosum, the granulosum, the lucidum, and the corneum. Mitosis happens in the stratum basale, so every new keratinocyte starts there and is pushed upward. In the spinosum the cells are still living and are held together by strong junctions, in the granulosum they fill with keratin and release the lipid sealant of their lamellar granules, and in the corneum they are dead, flattened, fully keratinized cells that are shed from the surface. In the lucidum the cells form a clear, flattened band with no visible nuclei, and that is the layer missing from thin skin, since it appears only in the thick skin of the palms, the soles, and the fingertips. The four principal cells are the keratinocyte, which makes keratin and is present in every layer, the melanocyte in the stratum basale, which makes melanin and hands it to keratinocytes, the intraepidermal macrophage or Langerhans cell in the stratum spinosum, which alerts the immune system to invaders, and the tactile or Merkel cell in the stratum basale, which works with a sensory neuron ending to detect touch.","rubric":[{"text":"Lists all five strata in the correct order from deep to superficial","pts":4},{"text":"States that mitosis takes place in the stratum basale","pts":2},{"text":"States that the stratum lucidum is present only in thick skin, the palms and soles","pts":2},{"text":"Describes keratin filling the cells as they climb toward the surface","pts":2},{"text":"Describes the stratum corneum as dead, flattened, keratinized cells that are shed","pts":2},{"text":"Names the keratinocyte and states that it produces keratin","pts":2},{"text":"Names the melanocyte in the stratum basale and states that it produces melanin","pts":2},{"text":"Names the intraepidermal macrophage, also called the Langerhans or dendritic cell, in the stratum spinosum and gives its immune role","pts":2},{"text":"Names the tactile cell, also called the Merkel cell, in the stratum basale and gives its touch role","pts":2}],"exam":1,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Draw a long bone cut lengthwise and label every region and covering you can. Include the marrow, and say which kind sits where in an adult.","topic":"Long Bone in Section","dok":2,"type":"lines","lines":10,"model":"The diaphysis is the shaft, a tube of compact bone wrapped around a hollow medullary cavity, and each expanded end is an epiphysis, built of spongy bone under a thin compact shell. Between the two lies the metaphysis, which holds the epiphyseal plate in a growing bone and the epiphyseal line once growth in length has ended. The medullary cavity of an adult shaft stores fat as yellow marrow, while red marrow, the site of blood cell formation, fills the spaces of the spongy bone in the epiphyses. The outer surface is covered by the periosteum, a connective tissue membrane anchored into the bone by perforating fibers, and the internal surfaces, including the trabeculae, are lined by the endosteum. Where the bone meets another bone the periosteum stops and hyaline articular cartilage caps the epiphysis, and the nutrient artery enters the shaft through the nutrient foramen.","rubric":[{"text":"Names the diaphysis as the shaft and places compact bone in its wall","pts":2},{"text":"Names the epiphysis as each expanded end and places spongy bone inside it","pts":2},{"text":"Names the metaphysis and puts the epiphyseal plate or epiphyseal line there","pts":2},{"text":"Names the medullary cavity as the hollow core of the diaphysis","pts":2},{"text":"Places yellow marrow in the medullary cavity of the adult shaft","pts":2},{"text":"Places red marrow in the spongy bone of the epiphysis and links it to blood cell formation","pts":3},{"text":"Names the periosteum on the outer surface","pts":2},{"text":"Names the endosteum lining the internal surfaces","pts":2},{"text":"Names articular cartilage as hyaline cartilage capping the epiphysis at a joint","pts":2},{"text":"Adds either the perforating (Sharpey) fibers anchoring the periosteum or the nutrient foramen","pts":1}],"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Chart every skull foramen or opening you can name. For each one give the bone it pierces and what passes through it. Then, below the chart, list the bones that build the orbit.","topic":"Skull Openings and the Orbit","dok":2,"type":"chart","lines":10,"model":"The foramen magnum pierces the occipital bone and passes the spinal cord and the vertebral arteries. The optic canal and the superior orbital fissure both belong to the sphenoid, the first carrying the optic nerve and the second the nerves that move the eye, and the foramen ovale, also in the sphenoid, carries a branch of the trigeminal nerve to the lower jaw. The cribriform foramina perforate the cribriform plate of the ethmoid and pass the olfactory nerve filaments. In the temporal bone, the carotid canal carries the internal carotid artery and the internal acoustic meatus carries the nerves for hearing and facial movement, while the jugular foramen, between the temporal and occipital bones, passes the internal jugular vein. The orbit is built from seven bones: the frontal, sphenoid, zygomatic, maxilla, lacrimal, ethmoid, and palatine.","rubric":[{"text":"Foramen magnum, occipital bone, spinal cord and vertebral arteries","pts":3},{"text":"Optic canal, sphenoid bone, optic nerve","pts":2},{"text":"Superior orbital fissure, sphenoid bone, the nerves that move the eye","pts":2},{"text":"Foramen ovale, sphenoid bone, a branch of the trigeminal nerve to the lower jaw","pts":2},{"text":"Cribriform foramina, ethmoid bone, the olfactory nerve filaments","pts":2},{"text":"Carotid canal, temporal bone, the internal carotid artery","pts":2},{"text":"Internal acoustic meatus, temporal bone, the nerves for hearing and facial movement","pts":2},{"text":"Jugular foramen, between the temporal and occipital bones, the internal jugular vein","pts":2},{"text":"Lists at least five of the seven bones of the orbit","pts":3}],"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Write the vertebral counts by region from top to bottom. For each of the three movable regions give one feature that tells you a loose vertebra came from there. Then name the four curvatures and sort them as primary or secondary.","topic":"The Vertebral Column","dok":2,"type":"lines","lines":10,"model":"From the top down there are 7 cervical, 12 thoracic, and 5 lumbar vertebrae, then 5 sacral vertebrae fused into the sacrum and 4 coccygeal vertebrae fused into the coccyx, which leaves 26 bones in the adult column. A cervical vertebra is recognized by the transverse foramina in its transverse processes, which pass the vertebral arteries, and by its small body and short, often bifid, spinous process. A thoracic vertebra carries costal facets on the body and transverse processes where the ribs articulate, and its spinous process is long and angled sharply downward. A lumbar vertebra has a thick, kidney-shaped, weight-bearing body and a short blunt spinous process, with no costal facets and no transverse foramina. The four curvatures are the cervical, thoracic, lumbar, and sacral. The thoracic and sacral curvatures are primary and are present at birth, while the cervical and lumbar curvatures are secondary, developing as the infant holds up its head and as the child stands and walks.","rubric":[{"text":"Gives 7 cervical vertebrae","pts":1},{"text":"Gives 12 thoracic vertebrae","pts":1},{"text":"Gives 5 lumbar vertebrae","pts":1},{"text":"Gives 5 fused sacral and 4 fused coccygeal vertebrae","pts":2},{"text":"Cervical giveaway: transverse foramina, or a small body with a short bifid spinous process","pts":3},{"text":"Thoracic giveaway: costal facets for the ribs, or a long spinous process angled downward","pts":3},{"text":"Lumbar giveaway: a thick kidney-shaped body with a short blunt spinous process","pts":3},{"text":"Names all four curvatures: cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacral","pts":2},{"text":"Sorts thoracic and sacral as primary, present at birth","pts":2},{"text":"Sorts cervical and lumbar as secondary, developing with holding the head up and with standing","pts":2}],"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Classify joints both ways, by structure and by function, with an example of each class. Then draw a synovial joint in section and label everything that lets it move with so little friction.","topic":"Joints","dok":3,"type":"lines","lines":10,"model":"Joints are classified by structure into fibrous, cartilaginous, and synovial, and by function into synarthroses that do not move, amphiarthroses that move slightly, and diarthroses that move freely. The structural test is whether a synovial cavity is present, and only synovial joints have one, which is why every synovial joint is a diarthrosis. A fibrous joint holds bone to bone with dense connective tissue and no cavity, as in a skull suture or a tooth in its socket, and a cartilaginous joint uses cartilage instead, hyaline in a synchondrosis such as the epiphyseal plate and fibrocartilage in a symphysis such as the pubic symphysis. In a synovial joint the bone ends are capped with hyaline articular cartilage and enclosed by an articular capsule whose outer fibrous membrane joins the periosteum and whose inner synovial membrane lines the cavity. That membrane secretes synovial fluid, which fills the cavity, lubricates the surfaces, and nourishes the articular cartilage, since the cartilage has no blood supply of its own. Accessory structures such as menisci, a labrum, bursae, and tendon sheaths improve the fit and cut friction further.","rubric":[{"text":"Names the three structural classes: fibrous, cartilaginous, and synovial","pts":2},{"text":"Names the three functional classes: synarthrosis, amphiarthrosis, and diarthrosis","pts":2},{"text":"States that a synovial cavity is present only in synovial joints, and that all synovial joints are diarthroses","pts":2},{"text":"Gives an example of a fibrous joint such as a suture, a gomphosis, or a syndesmosis","pts":2},{"text":"Gives an example of a cartilaginous joint and names it as a synchondrosis or a symphysis","pts":2},{"text":"Labels articular cartilage as hyaline cartilage on the bone ends","pts":2},{"text":"Labels the articular capsule with its outer fibrous membrane and inner synovial membrane","pts":3},{"text":"Labels the synovial cavity and the synovial fluid inside it","pts":2},{"text":"States that synovial fluid both lubricates and nourishes the avascular articular cartilage","pts":2},{"text":"Names one accessory structure such as a meniscus, a labrum, a bursa, or a tendon sheath","pts":1}],"exam":2,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Draw the heart and trace one drop of blood from the vena cava all the way to the aorta. Name every chamber, valve, and vessel it passes, in order.","topic":"Pathway of Blood Through the Heart","dok":2,"type":"lines","lines":10,"model":"Oxygen-poor blood returns from the body through the superior and inferior venae cavae and enters the right atrium. It passes through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, and when that ventricle contracts the blood is driven through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk, which divides into the right and left pulmonary arteries. In the pulmonary capillaries around the alveoli the blood gives up carbon dioxide and picks up oxygen, then returns, now oxygen-rich, through the four pulmonary veins to the left atrium. From there it crosses the mitral valve into the left ventricle, the thickest chamber of the four. The left ventricle contracts and pushes the blood through the aortic valve into the aorta and out to the body, completing the loop.","rubric":[{"text":"Names the superior and inferior venae cavae emptying into the right atrium","pts":2},{"text":"Names the tricuspid valve between the right atrium and the right ventricle","pts":2},{"text":"Names the pulmonary valve leading into the pulmonary trunk","pts":2},{"text":"Names the pulmonary arteries carrying blood to the lungs","pts":2},{"text":"States that gas exchange happens at the pulmonary capillaries in the lungs","pts":2},{"text":"Names the four pulmonary veins returning blood to the left atrium","pts":2},{"text":"Names the mitral or bicuspid valve between the left atrium and the left ventricle","pts":2},{"text":"Names the aortic valve leading into the aorta","pts":2},{"text":"The whole sequence is in correct order with no chamber or valve out of place","pts":3},{"text":"Marks which parts of the route carry oxygen-poor blood and which carry oxygen-rich blood","pts":1}],"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Dump skeletal muscle from the whole organ down to the myofilament. Name every level and every wrapping in order, then draw one sarcomere and label its bands and lines.","topic":"Muscle Organization and the Sarcomere","dok":2,"type":"lines","lines":10,"model":"A whole skeletal muscle is wrapped in epimysium. Inside it, bundles of fibers called fascicles are each wrapped in perimysium, and inside a fascicle every single muscle fiber is wrapped in endomysium and bounded by its own sarcolemma. Each fiber is filled with myofibrils, and each myofibril is a chain of sarcomeres running from one Z disc to the next. Within the sarcomere the thick filaments are myosin, anchored at the M line, and the thin filaments are actin, anchored at the Z disc, with the dark A band as wide as the thick filament, the light I band holding thin filaments only, and the H zone marking the part of the A band the thin filaments do not reach. When the fiber contracts the I band and the H zone narrow while the A band keeps its width, because the A band is simply the length of the thick filament. All three connective tissue sheaths merge at the end of the muscle to form the tendon, so the pull of every fiber is carried to the bone.","rubric":[{"text":"Names the whole muscle wrapped in epimysium","pts":2},{"text":"Names the fascicle wrapped in perimysium","pts":2},{"text":"Names the muscle fiber wrapped in endomysium and identifies the sarcolemma","pts":2},{"text":"Names the myofibril as the contractile rod filling the fiber","pts":2},{"text":"Defines the sarcomere as the segment from one Z disc to the next","pts":2},{"text":"Identifies the thick filament as myosin and the thin filament as actin","pts":3},{"text":"Labels the A band, the I band, and the H zone on the drawing","pts":3},{"text":"States that the three sheaths merge into the tendon that attaches to bone","pts":2},{"text":"States that the I band and H zone narrow during contraction while the A band does not","pts":2}],"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Split the white blood cells into granulocytes and agranulocytes. Build a chart naming each one and the feature you would use to identify it on a slide.","topic":"The Five Leukocytes","dok":2,"type":"chart","lines":10,"model":"The granulocytes are the neutrophil, the eosinophil, and the basophil, and the agranulocytes are the lymphocyte and the monocyte. A neutrophil has a nucleus of three to five lobes and fine pale granules, and it is the most numerous white cell. An eosinophil has a two-lobed nucleus and coarse red-orange granules, while a basophil has large dark granules that hide its lobed nucleus and is the rarest of the five. A lymphocyte is small, with a large round nucleus that fills almost the whole cell, and a monocyte is the largest leukocyte, with a kidney-shaped nucleus and abundant pale cytoplasm. From most to least numerous the order runs neutrophil, lymphocyte, monocyte, eosinophil, basophil.","rubric":[{"text":"Sorts the five leukocytes correctly into granulocytes and agranulocytes","pts":3},{"text":"Neutrophil listed with its multi-lobed nucleus and fine pale granules","pts":2},{"text":"Eosinophil listed with its two-lobed nucleus and coarse red-orange granules","pts":2},{"text":"Basophil listed with large dark granules that obscure the nucleus","pts":2},{"text":"Lymphocyte listed with a large round nucleus filling almost the whole cell","pts":2},{"text":"Monocyte listed as the largest leukocyte with a kidney-shaped nucleus","pts":2},{"text":"Names the neutrophil as the most numerous leukocyte","pts":2},{"text":"Names the basophil as the rarest leukocyte","pts":2},{"text":"Gives the order from most to least numerous: neutrophil, lymphocyte, monocyte, eosinophil, basophil","pts":3}],"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Draw the brachial plexus in simple form and name its five terminal branches. For each branch, write which part of the limb it supplies, and name one deficit that follows if it is damaged.","topic":"Nerves of the Upper Limb","dok":3,"type":"lines","lines":10,"model":"The brachial plexus is built from the anterior rami of spinal nerves C5 through T1, and those roots gather into trunks, split into anterior and posterior divisions, regroup as the lateral, posterior, and medial cords around the axillary artery, and finish as five terminal branches. The musculocutaneous nerve leaves the lateral cord for the anterior compartment of the arm, supplying biceps brachii, brachialis, and coracobrachialis, so injury weakens elbow flexion and supination. The median nerve comes from the lateral and medial cords to the anterior forearm and the thenar muscles, and compression at the wrist wastes the thenar eminence. The ulnar nerve from the medial cord supplies the medial forearm, including flexor carpi ulnaris, and most of the intrinsic hand muscles, and injury behind the medial epicondyle causes clawing of the fourth and fifth fingers. The radial nerve from the posterior cord supplies the posterior compartments of the arm and forearm, so damage in the radial groove of the humerus produces wrist drop, and the axillary nerve, also from the posterior cord, supplies the deltoid and teres minor, so injury at the surgical neck weakens abduction.","rubric":[{"text":"Names the roots as the anterior rami of spinal nerves C5 to T1","pts":2},{"text":"Names the five levels in order: roots, trunks, divisions, cords, branches","pts":3},{"text":"Musculocutaneous nerve named with the anterior compartment of the arm","pts":3},{"text":"Median nerve named with the anterior forearm and the thenar muscles","pts":3},{"text":"Ulnar nerve named with the medial forearm and most of the intrinsic hand muscles","pts":3},{"text":"Radial nerve named with the posterior compartments of the arm and forearm","pts":3},{"text":"Axillary nerve named with the deltoid","pts":2},{"text":"Names at least one specific deficit that follows injury to one of these nerves, such as wrist drop after radial nerve damage","pts":1}],"exam":3,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Trace one molecule of air from the nostril to an alveolus, naming every structure it passes through in order. Mark the point where the conducting zone ends and the respiratory zone begins, and note what happens to cartilage and smooth muscle as you go deeper.","topic":"Nostril to Alveolus","dok":2,"type":"lines","lines":10,"model":"Air enters at the naris and passes through the nasal vestibule into the nasal cavity, where the conchae swirl it over the warm, moist respiratory mucosa. From there it moves through the nasopharynx, oropharynx, and laryngopharynx, then through the larynx and down the trachea. At the carina the trachea forks into the right and left main bronchi, which divide into lobar bronchi for each lobe and then segmental bronchi for each bronchopulmonary segment. Beyond those the airways become bronchioles and then terminal bronchioles, and the terminal bronchiole is the last structure of the conducting zone. Past it the respiratory zone begins with the respiratory bronchioles, which lead into alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs, and finally the alveolus itself. All the way down the tree the cartilage in the wall decreases until it disappears at the bronchioles, while the proportion of smooth muscle rises, which is why the small airways can constrict and the bronchi cannot.","rubric":[{"text":"Begins at the naris or nostril and names the nasal cavity","pts":1},{"text":"Names the nasopharynx, oropharynx, and laryngopharynx in that order","pts":3},{"text":"Names the larynx and then the trachea","pts":2},{"text":"Names the carina as the fork into the two main bronchi","pts":2},{"text":"Names the main, lobar, and segmental bronchi in that order","pts":3},{"text":"Names bronchioles and then terminal bronchioles","pts":2},{"text":"Names respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs, and alveoli in order","pts":3},{"text":"Places the line between the conducting and respiratory zones at the terminal bronchiole","pts":2},{"text":"States that cartilage disappears at the bronchioles while smooth muscle increases","pts":2}],"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Trace a drop of interstitial fluid from a tissue space back to the bloodstream, naming every vessel, node, and duct it passes and saying where it finally rejoins the blood. Then sort the lymphatic organs into primary and secondary and state what happens in each group.","topic":"The Lymphatic Route and the Lymphatic Organs","dok":2,"type":"lines","lines":10,"model":"Fluid that leaks from the blood capillaries sits in the tissue spaces as interstitial fluid, and it becomes lymph the moment it enters a lymphatic vessel. It is taken up by blind-ended lymphatic capillaries, whose overlapping endothelial cells work as one-way flaps, and passes into larger collecting vessels fitted with valves that keep it moving in a single direction. Along the way it is filtered through lymph nodes, entering by several afferent vessels and leaving by efferent vessels at the hilum. The collecting vessels merge into lymphatic trunks and then into the two ducts, the right lymphatic duct for the upper right quadrant and the larger thoracic duct, which begins at the cisterna chyli and drains the rest of the body. Both ducts empty where the subclavian and internal jugular veins meet, returning the lymph to the blood. The primary organs are the red bone marrow, where all lymphocytes form and B cells mature, and the thymus, where T cells mature, while the lymph nodes, spleen, tonsils, and MALT are secondary organs where finished lymphocytes meet the invaders they were built to fight.","rubric":[{"text":"States that interstitial fluid becomes lymph once it enters a lymphatic vessel","pts":2},{"text":"Names the lymphatic capillaries as the blind-ended vessels that take up the fluid","pts":2},{"text":"Names the collecting vessels and notes their one-way valves","pts":2},{"text":"Names the lymph nodes as the filters spaced along the vessels","pts":2},{"text":"Names the lymphatic trunks and then the lymphatic ducts","pts":2},{"text":"Identifies the right lymphatic duct as draining the upper right quadrant and the thoracic duct as draining the rest of the body","pts":3},{"text":"Ends the route at the junction of the subclavian and internal jugular veins","pts":2},{"text":"Names red bone marrow and thymus as the two primary organs and says lymphocytes are made or matured there","pts":3},{"text":"Names lymph nodes, spleen, tonsils, and MALT as secondary organs","pts":2}],"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Build a two-column chart that traces a bite of food from the mouth to the anus. In the left column name every organ and every sphincter it passes, in order. In the right column write one named structural feature of that region.","topic":"Mouth to Anus","dok":2,"type":"chart","lines":10,"model":"Food enters the oral cavity, which is bounded by the hard palate in front and the soft palate and uvula behind, and is pushed back through the fauces into the oropharynx and then the laryngopharynx. It passes the upper esophageal sphincter into the esophagus, a collapsible muscular tube covered by adventitia rather than serosa, and enters the stomach past the lower esophageal or cardiac sphincter. In the stomach it moves through the cardia, fundus, body, and pyloric part, where rugae flatten as the organ fills and a third oblique muscle layer helps churn the meal. The pyloric sphincter releases it into the duodenum, then the jejunum and the ileum, where circular folds, villi with their lacteals, and microvilli expand the absorptive surface. It crosses the ileocecal sphincter into the cecum and travels the ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon, all marked by teniae coli, haustra, and omental appendices, before reaching the rectum and the anal canal and leaving past the involuntary internal and voluntary external anal sphincters.","rubric":[{"text":"Begins with the oral cavity and names one feature such as the hard palate, soft palate, or uvula","pts":2},{"text":"Names the pharynx and specifies the oropharynx and laryngopharynx","pts":2},{"text":"Names the esophagus with the upper and lower esophageal sphincters at the correct ends","pts":3},{"text":"Names the stomach with at least two of its regions and one feature such as the rugae or the oblique muscle layer","pts":2},{"text":"Places the pyloric sphincter between the stomach and the duodenum","pts":2},{"text":"Names the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum in order with one surface feature such as circular folds, villi, or microvilli","pts":3},{"text":"Places the ileocecal sphincter between the ileum and the cecum","pts":1},{"text":"Names the cecum and the ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon in order","pts":3},{"text":"Ends with the rectum and anal canal and names both the internal and external anal sphincters","pts":2}],"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Draw the liver and label its lobes and the ligaments that hold it in place. Then trace a drop of bile from the hepatocyte that made it all the way to the duodenum, naming every duct along the way.","topic":"The Liver and the Path of Bile","dok":2,"type":"lines","lines":10,"model":"The liver has four lobes, a large right lobe and a left lobe separated in front by the falciform ligament, with the small quadrate and caudate lobes on the posterior and inferior surface. The falciform ligament suspends the liver from the anterior abdominal wall and the diaphragm, and the ligamentum teres, the remnant of the fetal umbilical vein, runs in its free lower edge. Bile is made by the hepatocytes and secreted into the bile canaliculi, the tiny channels running between neighboring liver cells in the opposite direction from the blood in the sinusoids. The canaliculi drain into ductules and then into the right and left hepatic ducts, which join to form the common hepatic duct. The cystic duct carries bile into the gallbladder for storage and concentration and back out again, and where the cystic and common hepatic ducts meet they form the common bile duct. That duct joins the main pancreatic duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla, and bile enters the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla once the sphincter of Oddi relaxes.","rubric":[{"text":"Names all four lobes: right, left, quadrate, and caudate","pts":3},{"text":"Names the falciform ligament and states that it attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm","pts":2},{"text":"Names the ligamentum teres or round ligament in the free edge of the falciform ligament","pts":2},{"text":"Names the hepatocyte as the cell that makes bile","pts":2},{"text":"Names the bile canaliculi as the first channels the bile enters","pts":2},{"text":"Names the right and left hepatic ducts joining to form the common hepatic duct","pts":3},{"text":"Names the cystic duct and the gallbladder as the storage detour","pts":2},{"text":"States that the common hepatic and cystic ducts join to form the common bile duct","pts":2},{"text":"Names the hepatopancreatic ampulla and the major duodenal papilla as the entry into the duodenum","pts":2}],"exam":4,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Draw a kidney cut in the coronal plane, into a front half and a back half, and label every region and structure you can. Then trace urine from a collecting duct to the outside of the body, naming each structure in order.","topic":"Kidney Coronal Section and the Path of Urine","dok":2,"type":"lines","lines":10,"model":"A kidney cut into a front half and a back half shows a fibrous renal capsule right on the surface, wrapped outside that by the adipose capsule and then the renal fascia. Inside the capsule the pale renal cortex forms the outer band, and the renal medulla within it is broken into cone-shaped renal pyramids, with renal columns of cortical tissue dipping down between them. The tip of each pyramid is a renal papilla, and every papilla is cupped by a minor calyx. Several minor calyces join into a major calyx, the major calyces open into the renal pelvis, and the pelvis funnels urine into the ureter at the hilum. From there urine follows one path: collecting duct, renal papilla, minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis, ureter, urinary bladder, and finally the urethra to the outside.","rubric":[{"text":"Names the fibrous renal capsule as the layer lying directly on the kidney","pts":2},{"text":"Labels the renal cortex and the renal medulla as separate regions","pts":2},{"text":"Names the renal pyramids and the renal columns that dip between them","pts":2},{"text":"Identifies the renal papilla as the tip of a pyramid","pts":2},{"text":"Names the minor calyx cupping each papilla and the major calyces they join","pts":2},{"text":"Names the renal pelvis and the hilum where the ureter leaves","pts":2},{"text":"Traces urine in order from collecting duct to papilla to minor calyx to major calyx to renal pelvis","pts":4},{"text":"Continues the path through the ureter, urinary bladder, and urethra to the outside","pts":2},{"text":"Places the adipose capsule and the renal fascia outside the renal capsule","pts":2}],"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Trace a sperm cell from the seminiferous tubule to the outside of the body. Name every duct it passes, and name each accessory gland along the way with what that gland adds.","topic":"The Path of Sperm and the Accessory Glands","dok":3,"type":"lines","lines":10,"model":"Sperm are made in the seminiferous tubules coiled inside the lobules of the testis, and only those tubules make sperm, while every structure after them stores, matures, or carries it. From the tubules the sperm pass into the epididymis, the comma-shaped coiled duct on the testis, where they are stored and gain the ability to move. From the tail of the epididymis the ductus deferens carries them up out of the scrotum inside the spermatic cord, alongside the testicular vessels, the nerves, and the cremaster muscle. Where the ductus deferens meets the duct of the seminal vesicle it becomes the short ejaculatory duct, which empties into the prostatic urethra. The urethra then carries the sperm through its prostatic, membranous, and spongy regions to the outside. Along that route the seminal vesicles add an alkaline fluid rich in fructose that makes up the largest share of semen, the prostate adds a milky fluid that keeps the sperm active, and the bulbourethral glands add an alkaline mucus that neutralizes urine in the urethra and lubricates.","rubric":[{"text":"Starts at the seminiferous tubules and names them as the site where sperm are made","pts":2},{"text":"Notes that only the seminiferous tubules make sperm and the rest of the route stores or carries them","pts":2},{"text":"Names the epididymis and states that sperm are stored there and gain motility","pts":2},{"text":"Names the ductus deferens and places it within the spermatic cord","pts":2},{"text":"Names the ejaculatory duct as the union of the ductus deferens and the seminal vesicle duct","pts":2},{"text":"Names the prostatic, membranous, and spongy regions of the urethra in order","pts":3},{"text":"Names the seminal vesicles and their alkaline fluid rich in fructose","pts":3},{"text":"Names the prostate and its milky fluid","pts":2},{"text":"Names the bulbourethral glands and their alkaline mucus","pts":2}],"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Trace cerebrospinal fluid from where it is made to where it is absorbed. Name every ventricle, every opening, and every structure it passes, in order.","topic":"The Circulation of Cerebrospinal Fluid","dok":3,"type":"lines","lines":10,"model":"Cerebrospinal fluid is produced by the choroid plexus, networks of capillaries wrapped in ependymal cells, and the largest of these sit in the two lateral ventricles, one in each cerebral hemisphere. From there the fluid passes through the paired interventricular foramina into the third ventricle, which lies in the midline of the diencephalon and holds choroid plexus of its own. It then runs down the cerebral aqueduct through the midbrain into the fourth ventricle, which sits between the pons and medulla in front and the cerebellum behind. The fluid leaves the fourth ventricle through the two lateral apertures and the single median aperture, and some passes down into the central canal of the spinal cord. Out in the subarachnoid space it bathes the whole surface of the brain and cord, and it is finally reabsorbed at the arachnoid granulations into the venous blood of the superior sagittal sinus.","rubric":[{"text":"Names the choroid plexus as the site of production and mentions its ependymal cells","pts":3},{"text":"Begins in the lateral ventricles, one in each cerebral hemisphere","pts":2},{"text":"Names the interventricular foramina as the passage to the third ventricle","pts":2},{"text":"Places the third ventricle in the diencephalon","pts":2},{"text":"Names the cerebral aqueduct running through the midbrain","pts":2},{"text":"Places the fourth ventricle between the brainstem and the cerebellum","pts":2},{"text":"Names the two lateral apertures and the median aperture as the exits","pts":3},{"text":"Names the subarachnoid space as where the fluid then surrounds the brain and cord","pts":2},{"text":"Names the arachnoid granulations returning CSF to the superior sagittal sinus","pts":2}],"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Build a two column chart comparing the sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions. For each one give the other name, where its fibers leave the central nervous system, where its ganglia sit, the relative length of its preganglionic and postganglionic fibers, and its overall job. Name at least one ganglion on each side.","topic":"The Sympathetic and Parasympathetic Divisions","dok":2,"type":"chart","lines":10,"model":"The sympathetic division is also called the thoracolumbar division because its preganglionic cell bodies sit in the lateral gray horns of the T1 to L2 spinal segments. Its ganglia lie close to the cord, either in the sympathetic trunk beside the vertebral column or in the prevertebral group such as the celiac ganglion, so its preganglionic fibers are short and its postganglionic fibers are long. The parasympathetic division is the craniosacral division, leaving in cranial nerves III, VII, IX, and X and from the S2 to S4 segments. Its terminal ganglia sit in or near the wall of the target organ, the ciliary, pterygopalatine, submandibular, and otic ganglia among them, so its preganglionic fibers are long and its postganglionic fibers short. Overall the sympathetic division runs fight or flight while the parasympathetic division runs rest and digest, and the vagus nerve carries most of the parasympathetic supply to the chest and abdomen.","rubric":[{"text":"Gives thoracolumbar as the other name for the sympathetic division","pts":2},{"text":"Gives craniosacral as the other name for the parasympathetic division","pts":2},{"text":"Places the sympathetic origin in the lateral gray horns of segments T1 to L2","pts":3},{"text":"Places the parasympathetic origin in cranial nerves III, VII, IX, and X and in segments S2 to S4","pts":3},{"text":"Places the sympathetic ganglia close to the spinal cord","pts":2},{"text":"Places the parasympathetic ganglia in or near the wall of the target organ","pts":2},{"text":"States that sympathetic preganglionic fibers are short and postganglionic long, with the reverse for parasympathetic","pts":2},{"text":"Names a sympathetic trunk (paravertebral) ganglion or a prevertebral ganglion such as the celiac","pts":2},{"text":"Names a parasympathetic terminal ganglion such as the ciliary, pterygopalatine, submandibular, or otic","pts":1},{"text":"Gives fight or flight for the sympathetic and rest and digest for the parasympathetic","pts":1}],"exam":5,"src":"module"},{"prompt":"Trace one drop of blood through the heart from the moment it returns from the body until it leaves again to the body. Name every chamber and valve in order, and label whether the blood is oxygen-poor or oxygen-rich at each step.","topic":"Heart","dok":2,"type":"chart","lines":10,"model":"Right atrium (oxygen-poor, from the venae cavae) &rarr; tricuspid valve &rarr; right ventricle &rarr; pulmonary valve &rarr; pulmonary trunk and arteries to the lungs (oxygen-poor) &rarr; lungs load oxygen &rarr; pulmonary veins to the left atrium (oxygen-rich) &rarr; mitral (bicuspid) valve &rarr; left ventricle &rarr; aortic valve &rarr; aorta to the body (oxygen-rich).","rubric":[{"text":"Right atrium receives oxygen-poor blood from the superior and inferior venae cavae","pts":2},{"text":"Blood passes through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle","pts":2},{"text":"Right ventricle pumps through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk","pts":2},{"text":"Blood travels to the lungs (oxygen-poor going out to the lungs)","pts":2},{"text":"Oxygen-rich blood returns through the pulmonary veins to the left atrium","pts":2},{"text":"Blood passes through the mitral (bicuspid) valve into the left ventricle","pts":2},{"text":"Left ventricle pumps through the aortic valve into the aorta and out to the body","pts":2},{"text":"Oxygen status labeled correctly throughout (poor on the right side, rich on the left side)","pts":2}],"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"prompt":"Lay out the structural ladder of skeletal muscle from the whole muscle down to the myofilament. Name each level and the connective tissue wrapping at that level where one exists.","topic":"Muscle Microanatomy","dok":2,"type":"chart","lines":10,"model":"Muscle (wrapped in epimysium) &rarr; fascicle (wrapped in perimysium) &rarr; muscle fiber, a single cell (wrapped in endomysium) &rarr; myofibril, the contractile rod &rarr; myofilament, the thick myosin and thin actin strands. The overlap of thick and thin filaments forms the sarcomere, the Z disc to Z disc unit. The three sheaths are continuous with one another and merge into the tendon that attaches the muscle to bone.","rubric":[{"text":"Muscle (whole organ) wrapped in epimysium","pts":2},{"text":"Fascicle (bundle of fibers) wrapped in perimysium","pts":2},{"text":"Muscle fiber (single cell) wrapped in endomysium","pts":2},{"text":"Myofibril, the contractile rod running the length of the fiber","pts":2},{"text":"Myofilament, the thick (myosin) and thin (actin) protein strands","pts":2},{"text":"Sarcomere named as the Z disc to Z disc contractile unit","pts":3},{"text":"The three sheaths are continuous and merge into the tendon that attaches to bone","pts":3}],"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"prompt":"Explain what makes blood a connective tissue, name its fluid matrix, list the three formed elements, and give the job of each formed element.","topic":"Blood","dok":3,"type":"lines","lines":6,"model":"Blood is a connective tissue because, like every connective tissue, it is cells suspended in an extracellular matrix; here the matrix is a fluid called plasma (about 55 percent of blood volume, roughly 92 percent water). The three formed elements (about 45 percent of blood volume) are erythrocytes, which carry oxygen on hemoglobin; leukocytes, which defend the body; and platelets, cell fragments that act in clotting. The formed elements are made in red bone marrow by hematopoiesis.","rubric":[{"text":"Blood is a connective tissue: cells suspended in an extracellular matrix","pts":2},{"text":"Its matrix is plasma, a fluid (about 55 percent of blood, mostly water)","pts":2},{"text":"Erythrocytes (red blood cells) carry oxygen using hemoglobin","pts":3},{"text":"Leukocytes (white blood cells) defend the body / immune function","pts":3},{"text":"Platelets (thrombocytes) are cell fragments that act in clotting","pts":3},{"text":"Formed elements are about 45 percent of blood and are made in red bone marrow (hematopoiesis)","pts":3}],"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"prompt":"Compare arteries, capillaries, and veins by wall structure and function, and explain why veins have valves while arteries do not.","topic":"Blood Vessels","dok":3,"type":"lines","lines":6,"model":"Arteries carry blood away from the heart under high pressure and have thick walls with a heavy tunica media. Capillaries connect arterioles to venules, have a wall of only the tunica intima, and are the exchange vessels where gases and nutrients cross. Veins carry blood toward the heart under low pressure, with thinner walls and a wider lumen. The three tunics are the intima, media, and externa. Veins have valves because their low pressure would otherwise let blood flow backward; the valves plus the skeletal muscle pump keep blood moving toward the heart. Arteries do not need valves because their high pressure keeps blood moving forward.","rubric":[{"text":"Arteries: carry blood away from the heart under high pressure, thick tunica media","pts":3},{"text":"Capillaries: wall of tunica intima only, the site of exchange between blood and tissue","pts":3},{"text":"Veins: carry blood toward the heart under low pressure, thinner walls, wider lumen","pts":3},{"text":"Names the three tunics: intima, media, externa","pts":2},{"text":"Veins have valves because low pressure risks backflow; valves and the muscle pump move blood forward","pts":3},{"text":"Arteries do not need valves because high pressure keeps blood moving forward","pts":2}],"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"prompt":"Compare the three vessel wall layers (tunics) and then contrast an artery, a capillary, and a vein. For each tunic name what it is made of and its job, then note the wall structure and function of the three vessel types.","topic":"Vessel Histology","dok":2,"type":"chart","lines":10,"model":"Tunica intima: inner endothelial lining, smooth so blood flows with little resistance. Tunica media: middle layer of smooth muscle and elastic fibers that changes vessel diameter (vasoconstriction and vasodilation). Tunica externa: outer connective tissue that anchors and protects the vessel. Artery: thick wall with a heavy tunica media, narrow lumen, carries blood away from the heart under high pressure. Capillary: wall of tunica intima (endothelium) only, the site of exchange between blood and tissue. Vein: thinner wall, wider lumen, carries blood toward the heart under low pressure, and has valves to prevent backflow.","rubric":[{"text":"Tunica intima: endothelial inner lining","pts":2},{"text":"Tunica media: smooth muscle and elastic fibers that control diameter","pts":2},{"text":"Tunica externa: outer connective tissue that anchors/protects","pts":2},{"text":"Artery: thick media, narrow lumen, high pressure, carries blood away from the heart","pts":3},{"text":"Capillary: intima (endothelium) only, the exchange vessel","pts":3},{"text":"Vein: thinner wall, wider lumen, low pressure, toward the heart, has valves","pts":4}],"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"prompt":"Lay out the connective tissue and structural organization of a skeletal muscle from the whole muscle down to the myofilament. Name each level and, where one exists, the connective tissue wrapping at that level.","topic":"Muscle Microanatomy","dok":2,"type":"chart","lines":10,"model":"Muscle (whole organ) wrapped in epimysium; a fascicle (bundle of fibers) wrapped in perimysium; a muscle fiber, a single cell, wrapped in endomysium; a myofibril, the contractile rod running the length of the fiber; and the myofilaments, the thick myosin and thin actin strands. The overlap of thick and thin filaments forms the sarcomere, the Z-disc-to-Z-disc unit. The three sheaths are continuous and merge into the tendon that attaches the muscle to bone.","rubric":[{"text":"Muscle (whole organ) wrapped in epimysium","pts":2},{"text":"Fascicle wrapped in perimysium","pts":2},{"text":"Muscle fiber (single cell) wrapped in endomysium","pts":2},{"text":"Myofibril, the contractile rod","pts":2},{"text":"Myofilaments: thick (myosin) and thin (actin)","pts":2},{"text":"Sarcomere named as the Z-disc-to-Z-disc unit","pts":3},{"text":"The three sheaths are continuous and merge into the tendon","pts":3}],"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"prompt":"Trace the cardiac conduction pathway in order from the pacemaker to the ventricle walls, naming each structure, and explain why the pause at the AV node matters for effective pumping.","topic":"Conduction System","dok":3,"type":"lines","lines":6,"model":"The impulse starts at the SA node in the right atrial wall, the heart's pacemaker, and spreads across both atria to make them contract. It reaches the AV node, where it is briefly delayed. That pause lets the atria finish emptying blood into the ventricles before the ventricles contract, so the ventricles fill properly. The impulse then travels down the AV bundle (bundle of His), splits into the right and left bundle branches in the interventricular septum, and finishes in the Purkinje fibers, which spread it through the ventricle walls so they contract from the apex upward.","rubric":[{"text":"SA node is the pacemaker in the right atrial wall; starts the beat","pts":3},{"text":"Impulse spreads across the atria, causing atrial contraction","pts":2},{"text":"AV node delays the impulse","pts":3},{"text":"The delay lets the atria finish emptying before the ventricles contract","pts":3},{"text":"AV bundle (of His) then bundle branches in the septum","pts":3},{"text":"Purkinje fibers spread the impulse through the ventricle walls","pts":2}],"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"prompt":"Describe the structure of an osteon in compact bone. Name and give the role of the central canal, lamellae, lacunae, canaliculi, and osteocytes, and explain how a buried osteocyte still receives nutrients.","topic":"Bone Microanatomy","dok":3,"type":"lines","lines":6,"model":"An osteon is the basic unit of compact bone, built as a series of concentric rings around a central (Haversian) canal that carries blood vessels and nerves. The rings of hardened matrix are the lamellae. Between the lamellae sit small cavities called lacunae, each housing one osteocyte, a mature bone cell that maintains the matrix. Tiny channels called canaliculi connect the lacunae to one another and to the central canal. Because bone matrix is solid, nutrients and wastes pass through these canaliculi from the central canal's blood vessels out to each osteocyte, so even a deeply buried cell stays alive.","rubric":[{"text":"Osteon is the structural unit of compact bone, arranged around a central canal","pts":2},{"text":"Central (Haversian) canal carries blood vessels and nerves","pts":3},{"text":"Lamellae are the concentric rings of bone matrix","pts":3},{"text":"Lacunae are cavities that each house one osteocyte","pts":3},{"text":"Canaliculi are channels connecting lacunae (and the central canal)","pts":3},{"text":"Nutrients reach buried osteocytes through the canaliculi","pts":2}],"exam":2,"src":"summer"},{"prompt":"Map the arteries that carry blood from the aorta out to the hand, and separately name the main arteries that supply the brain. Give the arteries of the upper limb in order from the trunk to the wrist, then name the two arterial routes to the brain.","topic":"Neck & Upper Limb Vessels","dok":3,"type":"chart","lines":10,"model":"Upper limb, from trunk to wrist: the subclavian artery leaves the aortic arch region, becomes the axillary artery in the armpit, continues as the brachial artery in the arm, and at the elbow divides into the radial artery (lateral, thumb side) and the ulnar artery (medial), which supply the hand and join in the palmar arches. Brain: the two common carotid arteries each split into an internal carotid (to the brain and eye) and an external carotid (to the face and scalp), and the two vertebral arteries ascend through the cervical transverse foramina to reach the brain.","rubric":[{"text":"Subclavian artery is the first artery of the upper limb","pts":2},{"text":"Becomes the axillary artery in the armpit","pts":2},{"text":"Continues as the brachial artery in the arm","pts":2},{"text":"Divides into radial (lateral) and ulnar (medial) arteries at the elbow","pts":3},{"text":"Common carotid splits into internal (brain/eye) and external (face/scalp) carotid","pts":4},{"text":"Vertebral arteries ascend through cervical transverse foramina to the brain","pts":3}],"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"prompt":"Explain why blood is classified as a connective tissue, name its fluid matrix, list the three formed elements, and give the job of each. Note where the formed elements are made.","topic":"Blood","dok":3,"type":"lines","lines":6,"model":"Blood is a connective tissue because, like all connective tissues, it consists of cells suspended in an extracellular matrix; here the matrix is a fluid called plasma, which is about 55 percent of blood volume and mostly water. The three formed elements are erythrocytes (red blood cells), which carry oxygen bound to hemoglobin; leukocytes (white blood cells), which defend the body against infection; and platelets, cell fragments that act in blood clotting. The formed elements are produced in red bone marrow by the process of hematopoiesis.","rubric":[{"text":"Blood is a connective tissue: cells suspended in an extracellular matrix","pts":2},{"text":"Its matrix is plasma, a fluid (about 55 percent of blood, mostly water)","pts":2},{"text":"Erythrocytes carry oxygen using hemoglobin","pts":3},{"text":"Leukocytes defend the body / immune function","pts":3},{"text":"Platelets are cell fragments that act in clotting","pts":3},{"text":"Formed elements are made in red bone marrow (hematopoiesis)","pts":3}],"exam":3,"src":"summer"},{"prompt":"Trace a breath of air from the nose to the site of gas exchange, naming the major passageways in order, and distinguish the conducting zone from the respiratory zone. Explain where and across what structure gas exchange finally occurs.","topic":"Respiratory","dok":3,"type":"lines","lines":6,"model":"Air enters the nasal cavity, passes through the pharynx, then the larynx (voice box), and down the trachea. The trachea forks at the carina into the right and left main bronchi, which branch into smaller bronchi and then bronchioles, ending at the terminal bronchioles. All of these only move air and make up the conducting zone. Beyond the terminal bronchioles lie the respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli, which form the respiratory zone. Gas exchange finally occurs in the alveoli, across the respiratory membrane where the thin alveolar wall and the capillary wall meet, letting oxygen enter the blood and carbon dioxide leave it.","rubric":[{"text":"Order: nasal cavity, pharynx, larynx, trachea","pts":3},{"text":"Trachea branches into main bronchi, then bronchi and bronchioles","pts":3},{"text":"Conducting zone (down to terminal bronchioles) only moves air","pts":2},{"text":"Respiratory zone: respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveoli","pts":3},{"text":"Gas exchange occurs in the alveoli","pts":2},{"text":"Exchange is across the respiratory membrane (alveolar + capillary walls)","pts":3}],"exam":4,"src":"summer"}]};

